HT_DATA.load("detail", {"HyzD8pNlpwI":{"wy":"HN treats it as the standard starting point for Game Boy emulator writers and homebrew developers, with several calling it one of their favorite talks.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend watching The Ultimate Game Boy Talk [1] before trying to dive into any code.","i":23080027,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":48548662,"author":"rtpg","month":"2026-06","story":"Game Engine White Papers Commander Keen","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed look at Game Boy internals and graphics","quote":"this talk is very very good at going in detail about a bunch of internals.","text":"the Gameboy, not the SNES, but this talk is very very good at going in detail about a bunch of internals. The graphics stuff is 29 minutes in but I love the whole video. Very much a high level guide to building a \"retro-y\" fantasy console for people into that stuff [VIDEO]"},{"id":43772743,"author":"msarnoff","month":"2025-04","story":"How MOS 6502 Illegal Opcodes Work (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Part of an author's series of in-depth hardware talks","quote":"talks are great and I rewatch them at least once a year.","text":"Michael’s blog is one of my favorites. His “Ultimate Guide” talks are great and I rewatch them at least once a year. Ultimate Guide to the C64: [LINK] Ultimate Guide to the Game Boy: [VIDEO] Ultimate Guide to the Apollo Guidance Computer: [LINK]"},{"id":19663834,"author":"jchw","month":"2019-04","story":"GameBoy CPU Manual (2013) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspired writing Game Boy emulators","quote":"an excellent 33c3 talk, The Ultimate Game Boy Talk, which I highly recommend.","text":"I really like the Gameboy hardware; it's limited, but the design feels very elegant in it's simplicity. I've written a couple of toy Gameboy emulators at this point. It's a bit challenging to get anything working, and honestly I have no idea how to approach audio at all. Nevertheless, it's still quite fun. The LCD timings are a point of curiosity for me. It seems there's still a slight bit of mystery around it, still today. I was inspired to play with emulating Gameboy specifically after an excellent 33c3 talk, The Ultimate Game Boy Talk, which I highly recommend. [VIDEO] (Some day I would like to approach emulation for more platforms, both newer and older, but I feel I still have so much time learn about designing emulators that I haven't bothered with that yet. It's so open ended, and handling things like cycle counting has a lot of different approaches of varying desirability.)"},{"id":15734224,"author":"rtpg","month":"2017-11","story":"A list of Game Boy development resources","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dense, comprehensive explanation of Game Boy hardware","quote":"is one of my favorite talks of all time.","text":"The Ultimate Game Boy Talk[0] (listed in the page) is one of my favorite talks of all time. If you were to send this talk to someone, with basically just the videe they could likely build a faithful Gameboy clone. It's so dense, easy to understand, and comprehensive. I'll listen to it form time to time and think about all the cool tricks it reveals. Super impressive to have that much knowledge for a system that only had a couple thousand programs ever written for it. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37333157,"author":"hadlock","month":"2023-08","story":"RyujinX – Open Source Nintendo Switch Emulator","stance":"rec","why":"Covers CPU, instruction sets and video of the Game Boy","quote":"This is a good place to start.","text":"This is a good place to start. Named \"the ultimate game boy talk\" dives into the CPU used, the instruction set(s) used by the CPU, how the video instruction sets work(ed) etc. The game boy from 1989 is a pretty simple device to start learning from. From there you can look at how various people emulated the system. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30135328,"author":"kaoD","month":"2022-01","story":"Choosing tools for Game Boy development","stance":"rec","why":"Details of the Game Boy CPU and more","quote":"I can recommend The Ultimate Gameboy Talk for details on it (and much more).","text":"It's actually Z80-ish with some features missing and some new ones included. It's a Sharp LR35902 SoC with an SM83 core (not sure if it has been used in non-GB SoCs). I can recommend The Ultimate Gameboy Talk for details on it (and much more). [VIDEO] EDIT: I had the SoC and the core names the wrong way around. Thanks svendahlstrand."},{"id":23080027,"author":"TheAceOfHearts","month":"2020-05","story":"Nintendo has reportedly suffered a significant legacy console leak","stance":"rec","why":"Background to read before diving into Game Boy code","quote":"I'd recommend watching The Ultimate Game Boy Talk [1] before trying to dive into any code.","text":"Modern Vintage Gamer just released a video [0] discussing this leak in greater detail along with its expected implications. Anyone who's curious can find the download links by backtracking through all the Pokemon Prototype General threads under the Pokemon board on 4chan. One of the leaks that caught my attention was the source for Pokemon Blue, partially because of nostalgia and partially out of curiosity to see what an old game's codebase even looks like. The first thing that stood out to me was the project's flat folder structure, full of hundreds of files; I guess I was expecting things to be a bit more structured. The source is more readable and approachable than I expected, although I've only poked around in some of the more obvious places and definitions. I'd recommend watching The Ultimate Game Boy Talk [1] before trying to dive into any code. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21894699,"author":"eliasson","month":"2019-12","story":"Emulating the Original Gameboy’s CPU","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the Game Boy works","quote":"Anyone interested in how the Game Boy works should watch","text":"Anyone interested in how the Game Boy works should watch \"The Ultimate Game Boy Talk\" by Michael Steil. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20020436,"author":"Kipters","month":"2019-05","story":"Making a Game Boy Game in 2017","stance":"rec","why":"Details of how a Game Boy works, including the PPU","quote":"lots of interesting details about how a Game Boy works, including the PPU","text":"For anyone interest, I recommend watching \"The Ultimate Game Boy Talk\" [0] from 33c3, lots of interesting details about how a Game Boy works, including the PPU [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19663587,"author":"ehaskins","month":"2019-04","story":"GameBoy CPU Manual (2013) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of Game Boy and its peripherals for emulator writers","quote":"for an overview of the Gameboy and it's internal peripherals.","text":"I've been working on a GB emulator for awhile. Step one is to understand the architecture of the system. What are the different hardware components? How do they interact? A simple emulator boils down to: While(true) Emulate_one_cpu_cycle() For each cycle you have to produce any side effects from the various peripherals (serial comms, update the display, generate audio, etc.), process any external changes (button presses, serial data), and emulate the next CPU instruction (basically a big switch). I recommend \"The Ultimate Gameboy Talk (33c3)\" [0], for an overview of the Gameboy and it's internal peripherals. There are various test ROMs available that you can use to verify the basic functionality. [1][2] 0. [VIDEO] 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK]"},{"id":17135632,"author":"anyfoo","month":"2018-05","story":"Why did I spend 1.5 months creating a Gameboy emulator? (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Covers nearly the entire Game Boy hardware, including new details","quote":"This entertaining 33c3 talk manages to describe the Game Boy hardware practically in its entirety","text":"Game Boy emulators are always great for educational or experimental projects, because the Game Boy platform is surprisingly simple, which leads to a very quick payoff, and yet very \"real\" in that the Game Boy was a great commercial success with thousands of software titles available, so you have plenty of software available to try in your project and actually make use of that payoff. One example to illustrate how simple the Game Boy is: There is no operating system at all, only a 256 byte boot ROM whose sole purpose is to display the Nintendo logo from the cartridge and halt if it does not checksum correctly (for both cartridge integrity and legal reasons). This entertaining 33c3 talk manages to describe the Game Boy hardware practically in its entirety (and with some previously unknown details, actually): [VIDEO]"},{"id":33849730,"author":"chrsig","month":"2022-12","story":"Gameboy Doctor: debug and fix your gameboy emulator","stance":"reference","why":"Used as a main resource when writing an emulator","quote":"I'd mostly been going off of The Ultimate Game Boy Talk[0] and the pandocs[1].","text":"Personally, I've had more trouble understanding the PPU behavior than i did about the cpu instructions and timings. I'd mostly been going off of The Ultimate Game Boy Talk[0] and the pandocs[1]. In trying to quickly dig up some resources now, I found what at first glance looks like a good blog series[2], so I might take some time to squint at them soon. If any HNer has other good resources describing it, I'd be thrilled. It's been a couple years since I've hacked on my own emulator, and I got through implementing quite a bit of the behavior. As I recall, I was able to get serial output from the test roms, which was pretty cool. I put it down after getting frustrated trying to render the screen. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZsRRCnque2E",2],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["ZDnShu5V99s",1],["FyKRubB5N60",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["yHXx3orN35Y",1],["Gs790JOeN3Y",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["64cEmjtwRgw",1],["Cyya23MPoAI",1],["ycPr5-27vSI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["oFeoS41xe7w",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["xx7Lfh5SKUQ",1],["2SdGkkp1aq8",1]]},"8X69_42Mj-g":{"wy":"HN repeatedly cites it as a striking example of Common Lisp used to push the frontier of science, and as one of the most inspiring tech talks.","q":{"x":"I challenge anyone to watch this lecture from Christian Schafmeister and say they are not impressed","i":11175151,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":16839608,"author":"pmarin","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Common Lisp compiler on LLVM used to design molecular machines","quote":"The most inspiring talk a I have ever seen","text":"The most inspiring talk a I have ever seen: Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Molecular Metaprogramming. ABSTRACT This talk describes our unique approach to constructing large, atomically precise molecules (called \"Molecular Lego\" or \"spiroligomers\") that could act as new therapeutics, new catalysts (molecules that make new chemical reactions happen faster) and ultimately to construct atomically precise molecular devices. Then I describe Clasp and CANDO, a new implementation of the powerful language Common Lisp. Clasp is a Common Lisp compiler that uses LLVM to generate fast machine code and it interoperates with C++. CANDO is a molecular design tool that uses Clasp as its programming language. Together I believe that these are the hardware (molecules) and the software (the CANDO/Clasp compiler) that will enable the development of sophisticated molecular nanotechnology. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11803206,"author":"golergka","month":"2016-05","story":"European Lisp Symposium 2016","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling talk with a memorable macros-versus-templates metaphor","quote":"This talk is even better.","text":"[VIDEO] This talk is even better. \"C++ templates are to Commmon Lisp macros what IRS tax return forms are to poetry\". I don't know both well enough to tell if it's true, but damn it's a good metaphor."},{"id":11175151,"author":"nabla9","month":"2016-02","story":"The Lisp Curse (2011)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Impressive lecture showing Common Lisp as a programmer amplifier","quote":"I challenge anyone to watch this lecture from Christian Schafmeister and say they are not impressed","text":"Lisp is programmable programming language, especially Common Lisp. It works as programmer amplifier. Better you are, more you benefit from it. I challenge anyone to watch this lecture from Christian Schafmeister and say they are not impressed: [VIDEO] and [LINK]"},{"id":9814991,"author":"radicalcut","month":"2015-07","story":"John Carmack working on Scheme as a VR scripting language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating talk on molecular metaprogramming with Clasp","quote":"Absolutely fascinating stuff.","text":"Unrelated to VR but still: if anyone here hasn't yet watched drmeister's talk about Clasp and his \"molecular metaprogramming\" project, please do. Absolutely fascinating stuff. [VIDEO] Also recently discussed on HN: [LINK]"},{"id":9754925,"author":"616c","month":"2015-06","story":"Making sexy molecules in PowerPoint","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Developer built a Lisp to design molecules; inspires studying chemistry","quote":"If you are on this article, you probably will love to watch this guy.","text":"So no one will mention the Clasp (Lisp on LLVM developer)? He built a language to build better, albeit not visualize, better molecules. If you are on this article, you probably will love to watch this guy. I am computer nerd and this guy makes me want to go back and study chemistry. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28876017,"author":"simiones","month":"2021-10","story":"Carp – A statically typed Lisp, without a GC, for real-time applications","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why and how Clasp was implemented with C++ templates and GC","quote":"There's a nice Google Tech Talk about it [0], that goes into both why he did this, and how he implemented this.","text":"CLASP is a really interesting project. For one, it's created and written by a Chemistry PhD researcher (Christian Schafmeister), not someone with a traditional CS background. For another, it's one of the few languages that has ever attempted to interface with C++ at the template level - you can instantiate C++ template classes from CL, and catch C++ exceptions etc. For yet another, he does compacting garbage collection for the C++ objects used in the CL implementation, with pointer patching and everything else. There's a nice Google Tech Talk about it [0], that goes into both why he did this, and how he implemented this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18847626,"author":"girishso","month":"2019-01","story":"Ask HN: Can we write a programming language for biology?","stance":"rec","why":"A DSL in Common Lisp generating C++ via LLVM for molecules","quote":"they developed a DSL in common lisp to generate C++ code using LLVM.","text":"That was my interest when I started my career, but didn’t do much on it. Bio-informatics deals with the stuff you mentioned in-silico. There’re a whole bunch of libraries for many different languages, but I’ve not come across any specific language for bio-informatics. You might find [VIDEO] interesting, they developed a DSL in common lisp to generate C++ code using LLVM."},{"id":10184266,"author":"nnq","month":"2015-09","story":"Lisp is still a secret weapon","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what problems Lisp suits: a chemist building his own Common Lisp","quote":"If you're asking yourself what kinds of problems is a Lisp good for you should probably watch this","text":"If you're asking yourself what kinds of problems is a Lisp good for you should probably watch this: [VIDEO] (A computational chemist built himself his own Common Lisp implementation because no other language available was powerful enough for his needs...) It basically boils to: if you're solving a truly new and interesting problems for which the current libraries and ecosystems don't matter that much to you anyhow (because what you do is too bleeding-edge / ahead of everyone else, so you'll write your own better stuff anyhow), than you might want to choose a Lisp. And if your problem is so bleeding-edge and exotic that you also need to build your own programming-language for it, than you might just as well build yourself a Lisp (like the guy in the video did) and add your needed features to it, because this would be easier than any other approaches and allow you to spend more time on your problem and not on the language... EDIT+: another example is DWave using SBCL: [LINK] - if quantum (or analog... anyway...) computing is not \"truly new and cutting edge\", than I don't know what is"},{"id":40980367,"author":"flobosg","month":"2024-07","story":"Leap – Chemistry Through a Computational Lens","stance":"reference","why":"Author of LEaP later created a Common Lisp implementation","quote":"went on to create a Common Lisp implementation that uses C++ and LLVM for applications in molecular nanotechnology","text":"Fun fact: LEaP’s original author, Christian Schafmeister, went on to create a Common Lisp implementation that uses C++ and LLVM for applications in molecular nanotechnology: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27297145,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2021-05","story":"SICL: A New Common Lisp Implementation","stance":"reference","why":"Related talks list on Clasp and Schafmeister's work","quote":"Ah, related to #clasp","text":"Ah, related to #clasp: [VIDEO] Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Molecular Metaprogramming [LINK] 2018 LLVM Developers Meeting: C. Schafmeister lessons Learned Implementing Common Lisp with LLVM [LINK] Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Designing Molecules [LINK] Modular Polymer Catalysts - Chris Schafmeister [LINK] 10S6P4 Christian Schafmeister 2010 UNTT 3rd Annual Conference Session 6 Presentation 4"},{"id":21567728,"author":"lispm","month":"2019-11","story":"PAIP: Run Common Lisp in Browser?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Common Lisp with deep C++ and LLVM integration","quote":"CLASP has deep C++ and LLVM integration.","text":"Common Lisp traditionally favors other forms of interoperability: with Assembler, C, C++, Objective-C, UNIX/POSIX, etc... For example one prefers to directly call old-fashioned GTK+, Cocoa or Windows - instead of calling via a Java UI layer or a Javascript library. Common Lisp has interoperability with the JVM in various forms (from FFIs to ABCL, a Common Lisp on JVM implementation), but it's not a popular choice. There are several CL implementations with deep C integration, for example by compilation to C. CLASP has deep C++ and LLVM integration. Some implementation can create shared libraries which are being loaded from other programs. CLASP: [VIDEO] It's possible to bring Common Lisp on top of runtimes like the JVM or Javascript - but there is some larger mismatch, because Common Lisp (different from Clojure) a platform independent full programming language. For example Common Lisp specifies a numeric tower with bignums, some float types, complex numbers and rational numbers. JavaScript internally does only offer floats. Common Lisp also specifies its own object system, which does not map well on top of the JVM (single inheritance, non-dynamic) or JavaScript."},{"id":20615065,"author":"leanthonyrn","month":"2019-08","story":"Switching from Common Lisp to Julia (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of exposing Common Lisp to C++","quote":"You could take the Clasp path by exposing CL to C++.","text":"I was thinking about this the other day. How can I expose my Common Lisp code to Julia. 1. You could take the Clasp path by exposing CL to C++. [VIDEO] This means taking / writing a CL in Julia. 2. Julia has an AST representation written in femtolisp. So it would be in interesting target for CL or some glue middle language like Shen. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":19319485,"author":"cracauer","month":"2019-03","story":"Let over Lambda – Common Lisp Book (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Longer programming-side talk by the team behind Clasp","quote":"If you want to learn more about the programming side of our work (not so chemical), check out our other videos.","text":"That is me, I'm half of that team (Martin Cracauer). If you have any questions I'd be happy to answer. If you want to learn more about the programming side of our work (not so chemical), check out our other videos. They are from programming conferences: Longer talk: [VIDEO] Shorter one from the LLVM meeting: [LINK] (yes, the whole system is a compiler using LLVM as the last stage to make machine code from abstract assembly. The thing I always missed in SBCL)"},{"id":19514387,"author":"e12e","month":"2019-03","story":"I Built a Lisp Compiler","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped segment on how Clasp is built","quote":"See around 20-30 minutes","text":"> whose C++ implementation from scratch... You're aware of clasp? [LINK] Which builds (in part) on embeddable common lisp: [LINK] See around 20-30 minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17236700,"author":"drmeister","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: Are you working on interesting technical problems?","stance":"reference","why":"Project description from the talk's team, seeking programmers","quote":"We are developing a matter compiler.","text":"We are developing a matter compiler. We are working on designing molecular Lego to assemble them into new catalysts, therapeutic compounds, atomically precise membranes and sensors for proteins and small molecules. The goal is to make molecules that can do everything that proteins can do and that can be designed by human beings with computer assistance. We've developed a programming environment with a custom compiler and Common Lisp implementation for designing these molecules. We are looking for programmers with chemistry experience. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14516152,"author":"sedachv","month":"2017-06","story":"AI and Robotics in Agriculture in Japan [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Summary of Schafmeister's approaches and why copying nature's proteins won't work","quote":"There is a brief summary of the history of Schafmeister's approaches","text":"Check out Christian Schafmeister's research if you are interested in this: [LINK] There is a brief summary of the history of Schafmeister's approaches (and why he thinks direct copying of protein-based mechanisms from nature will not work) in this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13878993,"author":"bloaf","month":"2017-03","story":"Why programmers will never be the brightest and the best","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'd argue that there are essentially two problems in computer science: 1. Math. 2. Dealing with the hardware. Virtually all the ugliness is a product of the interface between these two areas. Dealing with the hardware requires a fascination with minutiae and imperfection, while working in Math requires a fascination with generalities and perfection. The people on the math side create systems like Haskell, which let you express very abstract concepts, and reason very generally about your code, but make dealing with hardware issues difficult. The people on the hardware side use things like Forth, which is very powerful for building code that works closely with the hardware, but whose paradigms actively discourage the generality present in many other languages. And so, what's needed is someone who can develop a system that can fully express high level math concepts in such a way that there is a straightforward mapping to the messy world of hardware capabilities. However, I think the \"killing blow\" for pure programmers' motivations comes when they realize that mapping between the pure world of math and the messy world of hardware isn't enough. The focus on \"end users\" is the tacit admission that even if you do successfully build a beautiful system, most people don't want to work at a high level of mathematical abstraction. If you really want to revolutionize computer science, in order to accomplish the actual revolution, your system needs to be able to let people work in the term"},{"id":11700456,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2016-05","story":"Whoever does not understand Lisp is doomed to reinvent it (2007)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Common Lisp used in cutting-edge chemistry","quote":"for a recent example of Common Lisp pushing the limits of science and computing in chemistry.","text":"A small few: [LINK] . EDIT: Also, [VIDEO] for a recent example of Common Lisp pushing the limits of science and computing in chemistry."},{"id":9814395,"author":"istvan__","month":"2015-07","story":"John Carmack working on Scheme as a VR scripting language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Same here, I never understood Java interfaces, abstract classes, and a ton of other \"features\" but picking up Clojure was a breeze. I don't understand why complicating thing that supposed to be simple helps you by any mean. On the top of that, I have seen several cases when Java programmers tipped over in their own code because of the complexity that they thought they understand, except there was a particular case when it was doing something else then expected. Reasoning about Clojure (LISP) code is always easy because if you follow best practices you have small functions with very little context to understand. On the top of these, I see the ratio of LOC 50:1 (worst case even higher) for Java : Clojure code that does the same thing. Usually people get triggered and say why does it matter, but in reality less code is better for everybody. Easier to understand, less chance of errors, etc. Correctness was long time lost for majority of Java developers, just put out a survey and you can see it for yourself. It is also pretty common practice not to handle exceptions well and just let a DNS not found error explode as an IOexception and good luck tracking down what caused it (literary happened to me). I understand that the average Java dev does not see any value in LISP (Clojure) but it silly to expect that the average of any group is going to lead the scientific advancement of any field including computer science. One tendency that you can see if you are walking around with open ey"},{"id":9735499,"author":"justifier","month":"2015-06","story":"Aneurysm","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential link in a comment about medical technology progress","quote":"","text":"\" I rarely clip aneurysms now. All the skills that I slowly and painfully acquired to become an aneurysm surgeon have been rendered obsolete by technological change. Although neurosurgery is no longer what it once was, the neurosurgeon’s loss has been the patient’s gain. \" so nice to read this many of the entrenched remain so through superstition at the loss of progress, but to read someone speak highly of a practice that rendered a section of their skill set obsolete is heartening i have family who work in trauma and they condescendingly balk at my excitement over new medical technology: [LINK] [VIDEO] personally, i look forward to bones: [LINK]"}],"r":[["b0EF0VTs9Dc",1],["d6iQrh2TK98",1],["2f2kGHcdJYU",1],["xc8epam4NyY",1],["0rSMt1pAlbE",1],["loCBvaj4eSg",1],["S7nEZ3TuFpA",1],["Gzj723LkRJY",1],["JqNg819PiZY",1]]},"TfYf_rPWUdY":{"wy":"Frequently linked as a mind-blowing visualization of how RNA is translated into protein, especially in threads on mRNA vaccines and cell biology.","q":{"x":"viewing these visualizations blew my mind","i":21053714,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":29653585,"author":"jcims","month":"2021-12","story":"DNA seen through the eyes of a coder","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing animations of DNA operations including translation","quote":"Some amazing animations that show some of the primary operations related to DNA","text":"Some amazing animations that show some of the primary operations related to DNA: Transcription (copying sections of DNA into RNA) - [LINK] Translation (turning messenger RNA into proteins) - [VIDEO] RNA Splicing (removing non-coding sections of mRNA) - [LINK] DNA Wrapping & Replication(two part video) - [LINK] One thing I would encourage folks to do as you listen to the narration of these is to consider any time words are used that imply agency and remember that these are basically blobs of magnets in a jostling goo. You'll find the names Drew Berry and WEHI referenced in most of these. There are many more stunning and IMHO profoundly informative examples of biological machinery from them on youtube."},{"id":21053714,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2019-09","story":"Face transplant recipient’s donor face now failing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizations of mRNA translation that blew the commenter's mind","quote":"viewing these visualizations blew my mind","text":"Indeed. I had read about it before but viewing these visualizations blew my mind: [LINK] (DNA transcription) [VIDEO] (mRNA translation) [LINK] (DNA replication)"},{"id":42230124,"author":"jcims","month":"2024-11","story":"NASA: Mystery of Life's Handedness Deepens","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing videos illustrating transcription and translation","quote":"because they are so amazing here are a few videos that illustrate the process of transcription","text":"Only tangentially related, but because they are so amazing here are a few videos that illustrate the process of transcription (creating mRNA from DNA) and translation (creating a protein from mRNA). [LINK] [VIDEO] The common complaint with these videos is that everything is more complex. One thing that isn't evident is that these specific videos (built mostly by Drew Barry) actually model a lot of other molecules to create a more realistic physical environment with brownian motion and whatnot. Then the irrelevant molecules are simply made transparent in the rendering. Obviously it's still much much more complex (eg the constant stream of ATP used to drive many of these operations is not illustrated). There are these and many more great illustrations/explanations at WEHImovies on youtube [LINK]"},{"id":26640614,"author":"shadowofneptune","month":"2021-03","story":"Moderna mRNA sequence released to GitHub [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"3D visualization of the ribosome in real time","quote":"3d visualizations of cellular processes in real time.","text":"These might interest you then: 3d visualizations of cellular processes in real time. I was shown them in my intro to biology class, which filled me with the same interest. Transcriptase: [LINK] DNA polymerase: [LINK] The Ribosome: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25901631,"author":"jcims","month":"2021-01","story":"18.7 Million Americans Vaccinated","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how mRNA is used to create proteins","quote":"This is how mRNA is then used to create proteins","text":"It’s broader than that actually, this is a generalized protein factory. This is how mRNA is created by the cells in your body to create the proteins needed to keep you alive (aka transcription): [LINK] This is how mRNA is then used to create proteins, either from strands manufactured by your own cells, those coming from viruses that have infected your body, or from mRNA vaccines (aka translation): [VIDEO] Not directly related, but here is an amazing video showing how HIV infection works, including parts you will now recognize from the above: [LINK]"},{"id":25272382,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-12","story":"What is protein folding? A brief explanation","stance":"rec","why":"Describes protein creation from RNA in the ribosome","quote":"I'm so intrigued by the dynamic complexity of this whole thing.","text":"Apologies if I spam this stuff too much but I'm so intrigued by the dynamic complexity of this whole thing. The following three videos describe the process of DNA Transcription (copying a single gene's worth of RNA from the DNA strand), Translation (subject of TFA, creation of the protein from that RNA), and Replication (the process of making a complete copy of DNA for purposes of cell division. Keep in mind that the functional components you see in here are themselves (generally) proteins that are created (and have evolved) by a similar process. Transcription - [LINK] Translation - [VIDEO] Replication - [LINK]"},{"id":13562377,"author":"ambrop7","month":"2017-02","story":"Influenza A (H1N1) – How many bits does it take to kill a human? (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Informative animation of RNA to protein translation","quote":"The RNA->protein translation process is pretty awesome. Here's a very informative video","text":"The RNA->protein translation process is pretty awesome. Here's a very informative video: [VIDEO] Also Wikipedia page on the ribosome has a good description if you're patient enough to read through."},{"id":43652945,"author":"timschmidt","month":"2025-04","story":"MusiCoT, a chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting technique for music generation [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like I said man, whatever helps you sleep at night. Even if it's a straw man like an unreferenced assertion about unnamed people in the 18th century. p.s. which part is the clockwork? the ticker tape with the encoded data, or the part that reads it? whole thing?: [VIDEO] edit: Probably none of those since rhibosomes were discovered in the 1950s it seems."},{"id":26628801,"author":"zappo2938","month":"2021-03","story":"Moderna mRNA sequence released to GitHub [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of mRNA encoding translated to proteins in a ribosome","quote":"Here is an animation of mRNA encoding translated to proteins inside a ribosome.","text":"Wow Looks like it is analogous to having a header on a TCP packet. [0] Here is an animation of mRNA encoding translated to proteins inside a ribosome. [1] \"The ribosome is composed of one large and one small sub unit that assemble around the messenger RNA, which then passes through the ribosome like a computer tape. The amino acid building blocks, that's the small glowing red molecules, are carried into the ribosome attached to specific transfer RNAs; that's the larger green molecules also referred to as tRNA. The small sub unit of the ribosome positions the mRNA so that it can be read in groups of three letters known as a codon.\" Very analogous indeed. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26631059,"author":"jcims","month":"2021-03","story":"Moderna mRNA sequence released to GitHub [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of translation at the ribosome","quote":"","text":"This is a great animation of the life cycle of an HIV virus. It’s not exactly what happens with the pandemic virus but it gives you a good idea of the complexity of the process of viral reproduction (vaccine or immune response isn’t covered here): [LINK] Animation of transcription from mRNA: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25204420,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-11","story":"The Timing of Evolutionary Transitions Suggests Intelligent Life Is Rare","stance":"reference","why":"Translation animation prompting questions about how and why","quote":"when listening to these videos, ask yourself 'how' and 'why' any time an action is described.","text":">On the other hand, the ribosome is an amazingly complex machine Just a bit: Transcription - [LINK] Translation - [VIDEO] Replication - [LINK] It's not clear to me how much these features can be degraded without destroying the ability for organisms to reproduce with heritable traits. Also, when listening to these videos, ask yourself 'how' and 'why' any time an action is described. For example, the following parenthetical questions from a small chunk of the 'Translation' video: \"The addition of each amino acid is a three-step cycle; (Why three step?) First the tRNA enters the ribosome at the A-Site (Why does it only enter at the A-Site? How are other options prevented and/or made inconsequential? Was it always this way? How did the features of the A-Site evolve for this to happen. What happened before this? ), and is tested for a codon / anti-codon match with the mRNA. (How is the tRNA tested? What happens when it fails the test or is missing the amino acid? What is the energy budget of this test and what are the specific features of the ribosome, RNA, tRNA and amino acid that make it possible? What happened before this testing was done? How did we get from the lack of ability to test and the ability to test?) Next, provided there is a correct match, the tRNA is shifted to the P-Site (What is the mechanism of this shifting? Why does it only go one direction? What is the energy budget of this process and how is it powered? How do we prevent multiple tRNA from shifting or keep "},{"id":10727351,"author":"zappo2938","month":"2015-12","story":"Are only poets safe from robots?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I wonder what T.S.Eliot would say about a robot winning a prize named after him? \"Poetry is not a turning loose of emotion, but an escape from emotion; it is not the expression of personality, but an escape from personality. But, of course, only those who have personality and emotions know what it means to want to escape from these things.\"[1] -- T.S. Eliot from 'Tradition and the Infividual Talent' In this essay, Eliot uses the metaphor of chemistry to describe the poetic process. However, I think using the metaphor of the chemical process inside the ribosome is a better analogy. The poet's mind takes a finite amount of emotions and feelings as there are a finite amount of condons which can be processed through a ribosome in an infinite number of ways to create all sorts of proteins [2] as the poet can move the reader to experience new feelings. Whereas a computer can test if a new protein will bind to a cell or act in a certain way, the computer doesn't have the ability to quantify if a reader is experiencing a new feeling or epiphany. Or perhaps, a person can be placed in a MRI machine so the computer can have a feed back loop. A good example of the artistic process that T.S.Eliot described is how George Lucas, who has said in interviews that T.S.Eliot has influenced his work, stitched together several scenes from dog fights in WWII movies [3] using physical images of things that are familiar to the audience like planes and words, \"I got him,\" to create a new feeling for p"},{"id":8050300,"author":"dm2","month":"2014-07","story":"Thanks, HN: You helped discover a disease and save lives","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how tiny and difficult molecular processes are","quote":"","text":"[LINK] \"modern medicine\" isn't perfect, it's good and getting better every day, but we have a long way to go. Plus, we don't know for sure what modifying our DNA does. It might affect certain things, but it might also have unknown consequences. There is a ton of trial and error in science, and you don't want to have too many errors in your body, else it leads to cancer and other horrible things. Lots of mice have given their lives to ensure that we and our children live awesome healthy lives. Thank you lab-rats (and other animals), you have suffered for our benefit (though you didn't have much of a choice). Also, it's really really difficult and really tiny, [VIDEO] but there are several teams working on it and many other approaches."},{"id":24690341,"author":"modeless","month":"2020-10","story":"Cells are fast and crowded places (2011)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"videos like this where the amino acids arrive at the ribosome in order","text":"It always bothered me that in the standard explanations of how these processes work it always seemed that reaction ingredients would somehow magically seek each other out, such as in videos like this where the amino acids arrive at the ribosome in order: [VIDEO] The molecules really do move randomly, not magically, and it's just that they go so fast and are so numerous and collide so often that even random chance puts the right molecules in the right place many times per second. This article was a revelation when I first read it and I'm quite disappointed that this was never explained properly in my entire school life."}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1]]},"hgN8pCMLI2U":{"wy":"Recommended repeatedly as the best account of container history and how Zones built on and went beyond Jails.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this talk, talking about Jails and Zones.","i":41055476,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41055476,"author":"panick21_","month":"2024-07","story":"\"Doors\" in Solaris: Lightweight RPC Using File Descriptors (1996)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on how Zones built on and went beyond Jails","quote":"I highly recommend this talk, talking about Jails and Zones.","text":"I highly recommend this talk, talking about Jails and Zones. Jails was first, but Zones took a lot of lessens from it and went further. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12024590,"author":"ketralnis","month":"2016-07","story":"Running I3 Window Manager on Ubuntu for Windows","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cantrill talk on jails, zones and OS virtualization","quote":"Bryan Cantrill has a wonderful talk about exactly this in Illumos that I recommend at every opportunity","text":"Bryan Cantrill has a wonderful talk about exactly this in Illumos that I recommend at every opportunity [VIDEO]"},{"id":34768124,"author":"yakubin","month":"2023-02","story":"Sun: The Network Is the Computer","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on FreeBSD jails and Solaris zones","quote":"recommended talk about those topics","text":"FreeBSD jails were released in 2000 in FreeBSD 4.0. Solaris Zones in 2004/2005. In fact, the original Solaris Zones paper cites jails as inspiration. Jails: Confining the omnipotent root (2000): < [LINK] > Solaris Zones: Operating System Support for Consolidating Commercial Workloads (2004): < [LINK] > Originally, jails didn't virtualise the network stack. That was added later, in FreeBSD 8.0 with VNETs, which were buggy for a long time, but went through extensive testing and are enabled by default since FreeBSD 12.0 (quite recently). Solaris Zones went a step further than the original design, and run a separate network stack for each zone from the start, years before FreeBSD did. BTW, recommended talk about those topics: Papers We Love: Jails and Zones: < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":23560538,"author":"kohtatsu","month":"2020-06","story":"Gocker: Docker implemented in 1.3k lines of Go","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on history of OS-level virtualization","quote":"This talk by Bryan Cantrill on the history of OS-level virtualization is great.","text":"This talk by Bryan Cantrill on the history of OS-level virtualization is great. [VIDEO] Apparently the first version of Jails was a few hundred lines of code."},{"id":13918062,"author":"jsiepkes","month":"2017-03","story":"Comprehensive and biased comparison of OpenBSD and FreeBSD [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk starting with history of chroot, jails and zones","quote":"Bryan Cantrill has a nice talk which starts with a bit of history on containers","text":"Bryan Cantrill has a nice talk which starts with a bit of history on containers (chroot, FreeBSD jails, Solaris zones, etc.). [VIDEO]"},{"id":13349705,"author":"eternalban","month":"2017-01","story":"Memory Deduplication: The Curse that Keeps on Giving [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing Cantrill talk on the history of containers","quote":"this rather amusing talk by B. Cantrill discusses the history","text":"I am watching it now but he's talking about VMs (\"KVM\") and not \"containers\". As to why we're in this mess, this rather amusing talk by B. Cantrill discusses the history: [VIDEO] (Linux) Containers are not VMs: [LINK]"},{"id":43337775,"author":"TZubiri","month":"2025-03","story":"Kubernetes Home – what do you do if your ISP changes your IP addresses?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to support chroot as lightweight virtualization","quote":"","text":"1st of all yes, many people consider not only chroot to be virtualization (of the file system). Yes it is arguable as it is the birth of lightweight virtualization. But you were wrong in saying no one does. [LINK] [VIDEO] 2nd containers go farther and virtualize network, and other resources."},{"id":12569120,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2016-09","story":"Haiku Project","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I love Haiku -- and their inspiration in BeOS brings back my memories of deciding not to work for Be in 1996, and then later hitting jackpot at the Be Inc. bankruptcy auction.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12200816,"author":"Annatar","month":"2016-08","story":"Docker Storage: An Introduction","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":".. no? Because many of the grsecurity+pax improvements apply to any kernel that runs on a CPU (it provides active protections against certain forms of kernel vulnerabilities caused by bugs). This includes illumos, thus the question is valid. grsecurity is a set of patches for the GNU/Linux kernel. illumos is based on Solaris, so the entire argument about grsecurity is nonsense. illumos uses red zones to prevent buffer overflows, which grsecurity is attempting to address. grsecurity is a commercial product by the way. A single license costs $19,000 USD. Enhanced auditing and process control which grsecurity provides have been part of Solaris, and therefore illumos, since Solaris 10, some even earlier. GNU/Linux is still playing catch-up, and as long as people like myself, Bryan Cantrill, Adam Leventhal and the rest of the former Sun kernel engineers live, it will be playing catch-up forever. My impression about package management being a shit-show on other operating systems is that every single podcast or blog post I read about those operating systems is celebrating that \"package management is easy now with pkg\" -- while it's actually not IMO as good as certain GNU/Linux package managers. In order for that opinion of yours to actually mean anything, the question is: how many packaging formats do you know to produce packages for? Only then will you be in a position where you would actually be competent to make such a statement, and where your opinion would actually make a diffe"}],"r":[["ncHmEUmJZf4",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["oM6S7FEUfkU",1],["cfyWvJdsDRI",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["TrfD3pC0VSs",1],["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["4zG0uecYSMA",1],["c52QhiXsmyI",1],["EczW2IWdnOM",1],["rv-mFSoZOkE",1]]},"kYfNvmF0Bqw":{"wy":"Quoted in threads on motivation, agency and regret as an encouraging reminder that the world can be changed.","q":{"x":"the world is something you can change, not merely participate in.","i":25514997,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25514997,"author":"keiferski","month":"2020-12","story":"Things You're Allowed to Do","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs says the world can be changed because it was made by people no smarter than you.","quote":"the world is something you can change, not merely participate in.","text":"This reminds me of an old video of Steve Jobs. His idea is simple, but it's really powerful. In short: the world is something you can change, not merely participate in. When you grow up, you tend to get told that the world is the way it is and your life is just to live your life inside the world, try not to bash into the walls too much, try to have a nice family life, have fun, save a little money. That’s a very limited life. Life can be much broader, once you discover one simple fact, and that is that everything around you that you call life was made up by people that were no smarter than you. And you can change it, you can influence it, you can build your own things that other people can use. Once you learn that, you’ll never be the same again. And the minute that you understand that you can poke life and actually something will, you know if you push in, something will pop out the other side, that you can change it, you can mold it. That’s maybe the most important thing. It’s to shake off this erroneous notion that life is there and you’re just gonna live in it, versus embrace it, change it, improve it, make your mark upon it. I think that’s very important and however, you learn that, once you learn it, you’ll want to change life and make it better, cause it’s kind of messed up, in a lot of ways. Once you learn that, you’ll never be the same again. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7001489,"author":"dmak","month":"2014-01","story":"Ask HN: Which books changed your life?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"Here's a video of what I thought was really profound for me","text":"Steve Job's book was very inspiring and motivational for me. The way he saw the world was very enlightening. Here's a video of what I thought was really profound for me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23454744,"author":"bsldld","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: I implemented the life I designed: perfect but I feel lost. What now?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Here is some advice from a well know person","text":"The best thing according to me is to see smile on other people's faces. Atleast on those people's faces who have lost all hope. If I was in your position, I would concentrate on two things. Doing something to provide free(or atleast affordable) best quality - education to everyone, especially under priviledged kids e.g. from poor families and orphans. - healthcare to poor people. Atleast do these two things and the world will be a far better place. Take a break from your comfortable life and go travel to places like asia, africa and south america and you will find your calling. Here is some advice from a well know person: [VIDEO] Edit: typo"},{"id":8736442,"author":"Revex","month":"2014-12","story":"The Programmer's Dream (A Ramble)","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs says life is made by people no smarter than you, and you can shape it.","quote":"Everything in life is made up of people who are no smarter than you.","text":"This ramble made me think of an interview with Steve Jobs - [VIDEO] Basically - Everything in life is made up of people who are no smarter than you. And you can help shape reality and improve it."},{"id":5120571,"author":"thiagoperes","month":"2013-01","story":"Ask HN: How do you stop regretting?","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs on life, offered as encouragement.","quote":"Steve Jobs on Life","text":"1st - Change your username. 2nd - I'm positive that If you don't change your mind, the thing you'll regret the most in the coming years will be the way you're thinking right now. There's still a lot that can be done. Lots of people succeeded after their 30's. Hugh Hefner started at 26: [LINK] 2 videos for you: - 8 characteristics every sucessfull person has (based on a study) [LINK] - Steve Jobs on Life [VIDEO]"},{"id":3446783,"author":"denzil_correa","month":"2012-01","story":"Ask HN: “Stay Hungry. Stay Foolish.”","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Here's a 1-minute interview which embodies the same message","text":"I think by foolish he implies that all of us in this world were born naked. None of us are omniscient or above the other. Here's a 1-minute interview which embodies the same message [VIDEO]"},{"id":39726459,"author":"rramadass","month":"2024-03","story":"Great ideas in theoretical computer science","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"everything around you that you call life was made up by people no smarter than you","text":"Life can be much broader once you discover one simple fact. And that is, everything around you that you call life was made up by people no smarter than you (Steve Jobs) - [VIDEO] What one man can invent, another can discover. - Sir Arthur Conan Doyle (via Sherlock Holmes). There is no prison as strong and unbreakable as the mental prison you choose to build and stay in - Me :-)"},{"id":33564161,"author":"mch82","month":"2022-11","story":"The world is run by people no smarter than you","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs's 1994 remark that the world is run by people no smarter than you.","quote":"Here is the link to the original video posted by the Santa Clara Historical Society.","text":"The world is run by people. Those people are no smarter than you (person reading this comment right now). I believe this personally and I’m confident Steve Jobs meant this literally. The reason I’m confident is Jobs said this in 1994, when the conventional wisdom was that both he and Apple had failed. Toy Story wouldn’t be released until the next year. iPhone (the device that convinced people Apple was right about Human Centered Design) was 13 years away. NeXT was failing. Here is the link to the original video posted by the Santa Clara Historical Society. [VIDEO] Edit: fix video link"},{"id":32766453,"author":"nicbou","month":"2022-09","story":"Excuse me but why are you eating so many frogs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This guy who makes computers put it best","text":"I'm admittedly sitting on a winning lottery ticket, no doubt. But it came out of the many impulses I followed when I should have been eating frogs. Other impulses got me all sorts of connections, career boosts and other perks. Some frogs you just have to eat, but I don't think that anyone's denying that. The original article is questioning competitive frog-eating. In my experience, refusing to eat those frogs is how you end up finding other ways. This guy who makes computers put it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32076554,"author":"tim333","month":"2022-07","story":"Buddhism has found a new institutional home in the West: the corporation","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on changing the world.","quote":"A well known video of him for example on changing the world","text":"Jobs wasn't really like that as in \"zen and care for personal wellbeing\" at the start and then switching to \"angry and materialistic\". He seemed to combine a fairly consistent zen like focus, more on great objects like the iPhone rather than care for people, and a fairly consistent edge of angry materialism. It didn't really flip from one thing to the other. A well known video of him for example on changing the world [VIDEO] He was a complex character"},{"id":28718758,"author":"exnor","month":"2021-10","story":"I just don’t want to be busy anymore","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"As a serial entrepreneur, and therefore employer of many people, I'm pretty horrified to read this. My immediate thought is that it's a toxic attitude, but totally understandable in what is probably the rule rather than the exception, i.e. a 'regular' company. And that's sad. If you are really feeling as you do, I think it's time to reassess your employment and potentially your life. For instance, I think many startups are different to this, and not only because they are small. More meaning: the genuine opportunity to enact change, I think is the biggest differentiator, alongside better incentives and stock options that in some cases lead to life-changing financial outcomes. There is always risk with this, and it's a higher risk than a larger company, but that's just the mechanics of life, economics, and society. I work incredibly long hours, so i'm always 'busy', but for the most part I love what I do, like a passion project. This is because I'm transfixed by cause and effect. It's an amazing thing that I don't think our brains are wired to appreciate by default. It takes discovering this and then confirming it, illustrating that just one person can have disproportionate impact in the world. It seems the vast majority of us limit ourselves or feel a sense of imposter syndrome in our own skin. The more one realises this and manages to instrument some change, the more positive reinforcement one gets, until one can look back at varying chunks of time in one's life to see the im"},{"id":22920978,"author":"unstrafed","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: How do I overcome mental laziness?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've lived with ADHD for about half of my life. You may or may not have ADHD, but what you're describing sounds pretty similar. Here're a few things I do. 0. Rexamine physical health. In the past two years, I have been diagnosed with sleep apnea and low testosterone (I'm a guy). Having those two treated has been almost miraculous. The ADHD is still very real, but I find I can fight the inattentiveness if I've had a good night's sleep. (And, I guess not coincidentally, a good night's sleep for me is between 6 and 7 hours on a CPAP. I can rarely make it to 8 hours. Again, the ADHD is real.) I've also been examining my diet, my weight, my activity level and other physical changes I can make. 1. Be constructively self-critical and responsible for your actions, but not self-destructive. I shouldn't accept failure from myself due to lack of attentiveness/motivation, but that's not a license to tell myself that I'm an intrinsically, irredeemably bad person. This may be a cliche in SV, but with ADHD or the feelings you're describing, every failure really is a learning opportunity. Your feelings of lack of motivation are not anyone else's fault, but they're not yours either. However, only you can take the initiative to make the situation better. 2. Think through the activities you do where you actually do have quite a bit of clarity and focus. I'm guessing there's something you do that just makes time seem to melt away. See if there's a way to harness what interests you in that activi"}],"r":[["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["doaQC-S8de8",1],["S89BHnaxULo",1],["UWEjxkkB8Xs",1],["zDZFcDGpL4U",1]]},"DZCBRHOg3PQ":{"wy":"HN people cite it as insight into how Twitter thought about moderation, and as a source for Tim Pool's arguments about uneven enforcement.","q":{"x":"provided a lot of insight into how Twitter thought about moderation. It was a lot more nuanced than I expected.","i":38161066,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":20296615,"author":"davesmith1983","month":"2019-06","story":"Show HN: A Firefox extension to leave comments on any URL","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tim Pool shows Twitter terms of service are political","quote":"Tim Pool in this video manages to point out quite clearly how Twitter terms of service are political","text":"This comic routinely used as a \"Gotcha\". However it so over simplifies the position that we are in. Youtube now totally owns online video, there isn't any competitors that are even close. Facebook has almost half the world signed up to its platform. This isn't someone's online forum or a news site. These companies completely own their respective parts of the industry. The major social media sites like Youtube, Twitter, Facebook are so large they are considered to be a \"public private space\" (I forget the exact legal term). These platforms agree to DMCA safe harbour where they are considered to be a \"platform\" and not a \"publisher\". That means they shouldn't be censoring anyone on their political opinions and must be politically neutral (otherwise they are taking on the role of a publisher as they are acting as an editor), now IANAL but there is certainly an argument that can be made. Whether a judge would rule it so, who knows. This is worth watching: [VIDEO] Tim Pool in this video manages to point out quite clearly how Twitter terms of service are political and they don't even realise it."},{"id":38161066,"author":"Kye","month":"2023-11","story":"X Is Reportedly Selling Inactive Usernames for $50k","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced view of Twitter moderation","quote":"provided a lot of insight into how Twitter thought about moderation. It was a lot more nuanced than I expected.","text":"That's weird because I watched as left-leaning accounts got banned one after the other over the years. Maybe you think it's biased a certain way because of who you follow and listen to. I never thought I would recommend an episode of Joe Rogan, but the one where he had Vijaya Gadde on provided a lot of insight into how Twitter thought about moderation. It was a lot more nuanced than I expected. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23310614,"author":"generalpass","month":"2020-05","story":"Wikimedia enacts new standards to address harassment and promote inclusivity","stance":"rec","why":"Pool on inclusion policies excluding large populations","quote":"I encourage you to watch a Joe Rogan episode from last year","text":"I encourage you to watch a Joe Rogan episode from last year when he brought Jack Dorsey, a Twitter VP, and Tim Pool. [1] The reason this episode is relevant to your comment discussion is that Pool presents that there is this paradigm problem where certain policies intended to bring \"inclusion\" end up excluding something like half of the U.S. population. This paradigm Twitter management is stuck in prevents them from understanding how people outside their paradigm view their actions, and this results in effectively banning a enormous set of the population from popular discourse. It is hard to evaluate if this is exclusively an American issue because, really, there are so few other countries that speak English. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21402243,"author":"blotter_paper","month":"2019-10","story":"Twitter to ban political advertising","stance":"rec","why":"Dorsey interviews on Twitter's future","quote":"His two JRE interviews[0][1] and his Tales from the Crypto interview[2] are all worth checking out, IMO.","text":"I don't agree with every call Twitter makes, but I find Dorsey to be by far the most interesting public figure in the social media game. His two JRE interviews[0][1] and his Tales from the Crypto interview[2] are all worth checking out, IMO. He seems to think Twitter should use a blockchain in the future, and that small social circles should have a system for internally moderating content without needing it to be okay by Twitter's standards, but obviously they're still currently taking down content that breaches their policies in $CURRENT_YEAR. I'm really interested in seeing where Twitter goes, I think Dorsey plays realpolitik sometimes but I don't think he has the same goals as Zuckerberg. I could be wrong, and Dorsey could just be a next-level showman, but I'm hopeful that he might do something truly interesting with the platform. I've still never made a Twitter account. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":20055827,"author":"manfredo","month":"2019-05","story":"Gab will become a Mastodon fork","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into Twitter's trust and safety situation","quote":"This podcast with Jack Dorsey, Twitter's global lead for trust and safety, and Tim Pool gives some good insight into the situation","text":"> Why are free speech absolutists only supporting fascists? They aren't. You're totally free to advocate instituting a Marxists state, the execution of the bourgeoisen masse, and forming a communist society on Gab. Gab has the blanket policy that any legal speech is okay on their platform (and yes, calling for the execution of the bourgeois is legal in the US. Non-specific threats of violence are legal as per Brandenburg v. Ohio). There's nothing fascist about that in and of itself. The fact that Gab is filled with fascists is because of displacement. Fascists got banned from all the major platforms, so the only ones that don't ban them like Gab and 4chan are full of them. Free speech absolutists are disproportionately made of up right wingers these days, likely because the right wing is being subject to harsher censorship. I think it's unambiguously true that most big tech platforms are overtly biased in their policing. People are getting banned for tweeting \"learn to code\" to journalists (in mockery of how some suggested that laid off coal miners should learn to code). And by comparison, verified leftist Twitter users outright advocated for the doxxing and violence of the Covington high school students with no apparent repercussion. This podcast with Jack Dorsey, Twitter's global lead for trust and safety, and Tim Pool gives some good insight into the situation: [VIDEO] > Where are the people advocating for the destruction of intellectual property rights, for widespread dec"},{"id":19504395,"author":"dayvid","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook to ban white nationalist content","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into Twitter's moderation decisions","quote":"His debate with Tim Pool on the Joe Rogan podcast[0] especially provided insight into why they're making these type of decisions.","text":"I wish larger tech companies would follow Jack Dorsey's path and have more open communication about their policies. His debate with Tim Pool on the Joe Rogan podcast[0] especially provided insight into why they're making these type of decisions. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30905556,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-04","story":"Jack Dorsey has regrets about building Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"Pool advising Dorsey","quote":"Tim Pool told him exactly what he needed to do.","text":"He sat in a Joe Rogan podcast twice. Tim Pool told him exactly what he needed to do. [VIDEO] In the last 3 years, what exactly did he do? Nothing. The situation got significant worse. Worse yet, because of their size they are 'too big to fail' and so they flagrantly allowed to break the law and violate free speech? Section 230 liability protections limits their ability to censor. Twitter is certainly in violation of Section 230. They simply need to stop doing this. That's it. Fire any twitter employee who isn't a free speech absolutist. Eventually Twitter will hit the wrong topic to censor and they will collapse so rapidly just like digg and how many others. I am very surprised it hasn't happened to date. Hunter Biden's laptop is real now? Censored on Twitter because it's 'hacked' or illegitimate or whatever. How many Trudeau Tyranny trends have been censored for no valid reason. We are counting. Meanwhile, the givesendgo hacked information of freedom protest donors was not only allowed... it was bumped up into trending. CBC covered it widely. Government then used hacked data to seize people's bank accounts. I dont see parler/mastodon/gab as true alternative to twitter yet. Twitter can fix themselves. They can move into compliance with Section 230. They can return free speech to their platform. If someone comes along to hostile takeover twitter and makes this change... they are going to be worth a trillion $."},{"id":23835011,"author":"mikece","month":"2020-07","story":"Resignation Letter","stance":"reference","why":"Debate between Pool, Dorsey and Gadde on policy","quote":"was mostly just Rogan sitting back and watching Poole go back and forth with Dorsey and Gadde","text":"The episode -- linked below -- with Tim Poole, Jack Dorsey, and Vijaya Gadde (who manages Twitter policy) was mostly just Rogan sitting back and watching Poole go back and forth with Dorsey and Gadde, mainly only stepping in to deescalate when things started getting heated or to ask a question about which he was curious. I don't recall if it was in that episode (or a previous interview just with Tim Poole) that they debated Twitter's place in electoral politics given that all candidates are conducting direct-to-voters communication via the media and whether it would run afoul of Federal Election law to ban, limit, or even censure in any way the tweets of a candidate for office. I thought that was a pretty intense debate and one that has significant importance give it's election year. Link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23986824,"author":"technoplato","month":"2020-07","story":"CEOs of Amazon, Apple, Facebook, and Google prepare their antitrust defense","stance":"reference","why":"Example of substantive discussion with tech executives","quote":"","text":"> Ultimately, the hearing won't reveal any surprising facts about these companies and will likely feature some theatrics, as is often the case with antitrust hearings. The article says it best at the very end. Wherever you lie on the spectrum of blaming these tech companies for doing obvious things to continue to drive the bottom line to their investors, the problem here is that this is just a show. And everyone involved knows it. There are no mechanisms in place for making competent adjustments at this scale, ESPECIALLY in the United States government. If you’ve ever tried to watch any of these, it’s clear the legislatures either aren’t competent enough in the field to challenge these prepared talking points. There’s more of a chance of legitimate, competent discussion in a forum like Joe Rogans podcast where he gets people like [1] Jack Dorsey and Tim Pool in a room (albeit with the babysitter counsel) to have a back and forth. I think the key to moving forward as a decision making body of any kind is to merge the two. Clearly, nothing can come of the Joe Rogan show of note, but imagine: what if it could? What if there could be more transparency and accountability for these huge companies and we could objectively examine what they actually do rather than just hear their talking points and move on? I guess I’ll end my rant here. It’s just very discouraging that, despite the fact that this hearing is occurring, there’s a next to nothing chance that anything useful for the adv"},{"id":23354990,"author":"seesawtron","month":"2020-05","story":"Twitter hides Donald Trump tweet for “glorifying violence”","stance":"reference","why":"Dorsey describes manual review of reported tweets","quote":"As Jack Dorsey points out in this[0] Joe Rogan podcast","text":"I would imagine that accounts of \"important\" people are handled personally rather than by automated algorithm. As Jack Dorsey points out in this[0] Joe Rogan podcast, the reported tweets by public or algorithm are manually checked at some point. Approx. 4000 employees of Twitter all around the world. Every day 100k (edit: 100M) tweets are sent. The reports of tweets that violate the platform policy are (reported by public) enter a queue. These are then inspected by personnel hired by Twitter (number varies proportionally to the scale reports in the queue). The personnel then go through a series of steps to take an action such as making you verify again, delete those tweets, suspending the account, or in the last resort ban the user permanently. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22543929,"author":"gcc_programmer","month":"2020-03","story":"Twitter rewrites developer policy to better support research and ‘good’ bots","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as proof of left-wing policies at Twitter","quote":"for proof, watch this","text":"I know I will be downvoted, but I don't care: why are people using Twitter? To me it's the most vile and disgusting platform: no signal, just noise. On top of that, the people that run it are effectively coming up with arbitrary progressive left-wing policies on restricting free speech: for proof, watch this: [VIDEO] after seeing I realised Twitter/Facebook/Google/Apple are evil corporations taking away your freedom with you cheering to it. Why are you using Twitter's platform? For business? Do you want to make business from Twitter's users which are mind-numbed angry, twitching zombies? Are there no business opportunities elsewhere? Anyone who is doing any sort of business related to social media is wasting their time: it's a broken system that deserves to be boycotted. To anyone who would call me angry: I am saying this so you open your mind about the pointlessness of social media: I dont use it, and I seem to live a nice life, only time I get angry is when I think about social media, like in this post. Oh well, this is HN after all, born and bred in silicon valley where such blasphemy will not be tolerated. But lets see."},{"id":21265687,"author":"CapricornNoble","month":"2019-10","story":"Twitter says it will restrict retweeting world leaders who break rules","stance":"reference","why":"Pool challenges Dorsey on uneven enforcement with examples","quote":"Tim Pool demonstrated on the Joe Rogan Podcast when he ripped Jack Dorsey and Vijaya Gadde a new one, with examples.","text":">>>They're banning violations of their ToS. As long as that is performed equitably Except it isn't, as Tim Pool demonstrated on the Joe Rogan Podcast when he ripped Jack Dorsey and Vijaya Gadde a new one, with examples. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19715267,"author":"writepub","month":"2019-04","story":"China Bans the Word 'Leica' on Social Media","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as evidence of conservative speech suppression","quote":"","text":"I hope the average HN user sees that Silicon valley companies have whole heatedly adopted the exact same totalitarian formula to suppress and censor conservative speech [1] Tianamen:China :: Conservative-Speech:SV-Companies [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19756853,"author":"mikece","month":"2019-04","story":"Twitter won't ban Neo-Nazis because the algorithm might ban GOP politicians too","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Pool's point on bans as political speech restriction","quote":"Tim Pool raised an interesting point on the Joe Rogan interview with Jack Dorsey","text":"Tim Pool raised an interesting point on the Joe Rogan interview with Jack Dorsey: as Twitter becomes more powerful and important in shaping political discourse in the United States, banning someone from Twitter could be argued to be a restriction of political speech: [VIDEO] Personally, I think the mute and block options are all adults on twitter need if they encounter speech they don't like or makes them uncomfortable."},{"id":19328614,"author":"brodouevencode","month":"2019-03","story":"Titania McGrath: 'Queen of woke Twitter culture' sheds his online mask","stance":"reference","why":"Dorsey and Gadde interviewed about Twitter policy","quote":"recently was interviewed on Joe Rogan's podcast along with Twitter critic and journalist Tim Pool.","text":"Jack Dorsey (CEO) and Vijaya Gadde (global lead for legal, policy, and trust and safety) from Twitter recently was interviewed on Joe Rogan's podcast along with Twitter critic and journalist Tim Pool. Link to the stream here: [VIDEO] I have some criticisms of Twitter that tightly align with what Dr. Doyle experienced, what was discussed on Rogan's podcast, and my own experiences: * Twitter is not a free speech platform, regardless of what Mr. Dorsey and Ms. Gadde say. They have prior assumptions that guide their decision making especially when it comes to identifying what is called hate speech. * Twitter is largely guided and controlled by progressive ideals. The people that make decisions there admittedly live in a bubble. Tim Pool does a very good job of pointing this out. * Twitter is a giant echo chamber. The limited functionality of the platform make it that way. * Twitter cannot be completely free speech because it allows it's algorithms to run wild. We've already seen where ML can be viewed as racist ( [LINK] , [LINK] ). Presumably Twitter's algorithms can be shaped in such a way to allow extreme progressive ideals to make decisions. * Unlike Reddit, which has decentralized command over it's sub-reddits, Twitter has a centralized decision making body of which is too small to look closely at the nuance and detail of every report. * The suggested jury review system only re-enforces the echo chamber problem Twitter already has. It's different than American/English common "},{"id":19504436,"author":"wesammikhail","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook to ban white nationalist content","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Pool's argument on platform power","quote":"","text":"While I agree with you, Tim Pool (as well as many others) made a good point recently about the fact that when platforms are as powerful as being able to influence elections, maybe allowing them to define the structure within which we communicate might not be a good idea. I am a free market guy, so for me these companies ought to have the right to determine the parameters within which their users can operate on their service. However, with things such as EUCD and GDPR rules, introducing competitors to the established networks becomes harder and harder for each day, which only benefits the existing platforms. So when the platforms are biased and competition is hindered by red-tape and regulations, we cannot expect alternatives to popup that can compete with said entities. All of this leads me to believe that things will only get worse as I don´t see a way out of this clusterfuck we´re in. [VIDEO] Edit: why the downvotes? :S"},{"id":33378763,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-10","story":"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I highly recommend anyone who wants to know about Vijaya: [VIDEO] >Gadde did more for free speech on the internet than almost anyone else I can think of. Call an ambulance! Medical emergency! I'm dying right now of laughter."}],"r":[["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["_mP9OmOFxc4",1]]},"H9B5mYfBPlY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a thoughtful, honest look at the causes of addiction and depression, and as a deeper Mate interview than most.","q":{"x":"I recommend Tim Ferris’ interview with Gabor Mate for a great honest look into the causes and mechanisms of addiction.","i":20436875,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":16924161,"author":"dnc","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: Effective methods to fight depression?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful long interview on depression, ADHD, ayahuasca and wellbeing","quote":"It's rather a long interview (2h 23') that touches on many topics (Depression, ADHD, Ayahuasca), but IMO well worth time invested.","text":"I've recently watched Tim Ferris's pod-cast with Dr. Gabor Mate who is well known for his work with people who suffer from ADHD (including himself), Depression and various types of addiction: [VIDEO] . It's rather a long interview (2h 23') that touches on many topics (Depression, ADHD, Ayahuasca), but IMO well worth time invested. Very insightful as to the ways one might take when it comes to improving his/her own wellbeing. I had a rather milder form of depression few years ago (with symptoms such as low-self esteem, loss of motivation to work, eat, exercise and pretty much all standard symptoms except suicidal thoughts). One thing that helped a lot to break negative thought patters and to start pro-actively coping with my own issues was attending few Ayahuasca sessions. I'd recommend it only if you find really experienced, trustworthy, practitioner such as Dr. Gabor Mate and if you are healthy enough (health heart, you cannot use antidepressants and other, prescribed or not, psychoactive drugs and you should do specific diet few week prior to it). Some claim that it's worth 10 years of psychoanalysis. I don't not know how much truth is in that claim, but in my case it really gave me immediate relief, sense of a fresh start and valuable insights about why I'm the way I'm. Of course, a real work starts after it when it takes time and effort to integrate these insights into your everyday life (but I felt motivated to do so)."},{"id":34991270,"author":"gregwebs","month":"2023-03","story":"Early-life stress can disrupt maturation of brain’s reward circuits","stance":"rec","why":"Other Mate interviews give more depth than Rogan's","quote":"there’s a lot more depth that Mate has to give and some of it comes out in other interviews he has given","text":"I highly recommend Hungry Ghosts for anyone that wants to understand addiction (it’s an escape from pain that probably started in childhood ). You could also go straight to The Myth of Normal which has some of this information from his other works. The Joe Rogan interview was one of Rogans worst jobs interviewing- there’s a lot more depth that Mate has to give and some of it comes out in other interviews he has given. There’s a few on YouTube [VIDEO]"},{"id":28028755,"author":"litmus","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best non-technical talks of all time?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk worth rewatching","quote":"talks that I want to rewatch the moment they come to mind","text":"\"of all time\" is often too strong of a qualifier for me. I would say \"talks that I want to rewatch the moment they come to mind.\" Charlie Kaufman's Bafta Lecture [LINK] Gabor Mate, Prisoners of Childhood [VIDEO] I consider Bret Victor's inventing on principle a non-technical talk, so yeah that. [LINK] Quel Solaar, on creativity [LINK]"},{"id":27508031,"author":"RemingtonLak","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Things you wish you knew before having kids?","stance":"rec","why":"Short potent sound bites on raising kids","quote":"(just a few quick potent sound bites)","text":"[UPDATED] A fulltime father of a 2yr old. Few bullet points on what I wish I had from the beginning. 1. <5mons tons of skin to skin, lotsa of low calming talk to your baby, soothing music w/o words like works from Claude Debussy, smile/laugh at every chance. 2. <9mons while baby on their back, move their legs like army crawling, lotsa of stomach time. 3. >10mons 2 individuals who seem to contradict, but same messages: (just a few quick potent sound bites) - Jordan Peterson : [LINK] - *Gabor Mate\\ : [LINK] [VIDEO] 4. Your child will mimic your every move, talk, and reactions. They basically are your mini me. When they say, the are reflections of you they aren't kidding. The few times I talked aspirated or over excited happy or angry, I witnessed my child re-enacting it later including the few words he remembers. Kinda scary. So point #4: you need to change who you are completely. If you want to raise a calm, gentle, compassionate, humble, caring, empathetic child. You need to show that in every thing you do. If your child acts out or \"misbehaves\" or something you don't approve of. It's not them, it's you they are reflecting. Again, you need to show not tell. At this age and reasoning level, they have ZERO comprehension of your words, only your tone and body language. 5. Join a dad's group. Even if you don't receive \"support\" or advice, just by listening to others who will have gone through the same you have helps. 6. Although your child nor mine isn't quite that age yet, Super"},{"id":21161515,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2019-10","story":"Unsteady income in young adulthood linked to thinking problems in middle age","stance":"rec","why":"Mate on stress effects on children","quote":"I believe Gabor Mate talks in depth on this particular topic","text":">Not to mention knowing the effect of all this stress on your children and their developing minds. I believe Gabor Mate talks in depth on this particular topic: [VIDEO] is one interview, which references [LINK]"},{"id":20436875,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2019-07","story":"Portugal’s radical drugs policy is working (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Honest look at causes and mechanisms of addiction","quote":"I recommend Tim Ferris’ interview with Gabor Mate for a great honest look into the causes and mechanisms of addiction.","text":"An excellent way to push against addiction is to address the reasons for addiction. As Dr. Gabor Mate, who has spent a decade working with addiction and written extensively about it says: don’t ask why the addiction, ask why the pain. Alcohol and other drugs are the means of addiction and in no way the cause of it. I recommend Tim Ferris’ interview with Gabor Mate for a great honest look into the causes and mechanisms of addiction. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33527176,"author":"wsc981","month":"2022-11","story":"NightWare helps disrupt nightmares for those with PTSD","stance":"reference","why":"Mate interview on the topic","quote":"You can find quite a few interesting interviews with him online","text":"Gabor Maté [0] also discusses this topic often, particularly related to children. You can find quite a few interesting interviews with him online, for example with Joe Rogan [1] or Tim Feriss [2]. --- [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SWEts0rlezA",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1],["PY9DcIMGxMs",1],["o2_SZ4tfLns",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1],["AaTRHFaaPG8",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1]]},"5MgBikgcWnY":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard counterpoint to the 10,000-hour rule and as an encouraging recipe for starting new skills.","q":{"x":"the threshold for acquiring new skills for personal or professional use is somewhere around 20 hours.","i":7324943,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":34425897,"author":"santi_panti","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What are your best tips for quickly getting better at video games?","stance":"rec","why":"Four straightforward points for learning a skill in 20 hours.","quote":"The topic boils down to 4 points that seems fairly straight forward.","text":"I remember watching a video about learning a skill in 20 hours. The topic boils down to 4 points that seems fairly straight forward. I can't do it good justice myself so here is a link to those 4 points: [LINK] And here is the video itself: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32658343,"author":"vrajeshkanna","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: I want to be an expert in many things but my lifetime won't be enough","stance":"rec","why":"20 hours gets you to top 10 percent.","quote":"You can be a top 10% in any given field with this.","text":"The first 20 hours -- how to learn anything by Josh Kaufman - [VIDEO] You can be a top 10% in any given field with this. 20 hrs is worth the effort on skills you want to learn. My personal advice - Learn only if you put that skill into use immediatly for your good or for the greater good of the society around you. Growth for the sake of growth is the mindset of a virus"},{"id":20678796,"author":"Edmar","month":"2019-08","story":"Try Micromastery","stance":"rec","why":"Surprising how much you can learn in 20 hours.","quote":"It's surprising how much could you can learn in just 20 hours.","text":"A while ago I saw this video: [VIDEO] is about \"The first 20 hours\" and there is a book as well. It's surprising how much could you can learn in just 20 hours. You can't be an expert by doing so little but you can learn some tricks for fun."},{"id":11820746,"author":"kriro","month":"2016-06","story":"One Click to Be Pro: my list of the best resources for mastering a subject","stance":"rec","why":"Cool talk on learning a skill in 20 hours.","quote":"Link to a cool TED-talk (20 hours)","text":"It might be a good idea to add a meta section with resources on learning to learn. Books would be stuff like \"Pragmatic Thinking and Learning\", \"The first 20 hours\", \"The Talent Code\", \"Inner Game of Tennis\" etc. Link to a cool TED-talk (20 hours): [VIDEO] :)"},{"id":10662651,"author":"kriro","month":"2015-12","story":"The 100-Hour Rule","stance":"rec","why":"Kaufman's talk that prompted buying his book.","quote":"Here's a TED talk he gave which lead me to buy the book","text":"Reminds me of the book \"The First 20 Hours\" by Josh Kaufman. He gives examples of skills that you can learn fairly well in 20 hours. One of them is programming. I have some issues with the book but overall it's a fun read. Here's a TED talk he gave which lead me to buy the book: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8197569,"author":"Freeboots","month":"2014-08","story":"The 1,000-hour rule (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Contrasts 10k hours with 20 hours to proficiency.","quote":"Anyway, check the TED Talk above, 20 hours to proficiency.","text":"How about 20 hours? [VIDEO] The 10k hours thing is based on how long it took athletes to become the very top of their field. The very top of their highly specific, highly competitive field. It doesnt even mean becoming an 'expert' (whatever that means), let alone just being good at it. Anyway, check the TED Talk above, 20 hours to proficiency."},{"id":39086716,"author":"Syntonicles","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: How to be productive without making lists?","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the commenter's 20 hour practice idea.","quote":"I extended the 20 hour practice idea from Josh Kaufman [2]","text":"Here's an old album of the style meter I use [1]. I recommend a soothing blue & green for hobbies, I use red for client work. 20 Hours is the allocation for all skill learning. The tokens are part of a much larger whole as I've unified fun, skills, work, mental health and financial planning into a single system. I have programs that do most of it, but keeping it as a marker/paper UI is critical if you struggle with mental health or motivation, so that you can easily bootstrap yourself. I extended the 20 hour practice idea from Josh Kaufman [2] I highly recommend Sam Harris (Waking Up), Alan Watts, Laozi for mindfulness / meditation, as well as Iain McGilchrist (The Master & His Emissary), Mihály Csíkszentmihályi (Flow) and David Foster Wallace (Infinite Jest) for metaphorical inspiration. Learning to play at life in any moment helps to eliminate tedium & boredom from your vocabulary. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39207079,"author":"ljouhet","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: How would you approach mastering a skill ASAP?","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a progressive learning plan.","quote":"I would start with a first hour, then The first 20 hours","text":"I would start with a first hour, then The first 20 hours ( [VIDEO] ), then you just need 90 minutes. Every day"},{"id":14570935,"author":"i0nutzb","month":"2017-06","story":"Does it really take 10000 hours to master programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that getting started takes less than 10k hours.","quote":"it may take a bit less to get started [1]","text":"Actually those 10k hours are a (semi) myth: it may take a bit less to get started [1] and it's all about deliberate practice, not _just_ practice. The „secret” is hid in plain sight: just do stuff. The more - and diverse! - you do, the more you learn. You know more, you can do more, so you can learn more. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11019282,"author":"tinco","month":"2016-02","story":"Men Are Better at Maps Until Women Take This Course","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how long to become good enough at anything.","quote":"Here's a talk on how long it takes to become 'good enough' at anything","text":"I'm not an expert, but I researched this once. You can train any particular question that's asked at an IQ test, but someone can always think of new IQ test questions that you will fail if you're not smart enough (i.e. your brain adapts to new problems fast enough). There is a training program (I forgot the name) that trains you at solving IQ test problems sort of in general but it's unclear if it just trains people at solving the sort of questions that IQ test people tend to think of. The way I understand it the most reliable way of training for plasticity if it is possible at all is to learn as many different things as you can. That means: Learn to program, learn a language with a different root (Chinese), learn to dance, learn woodworking, learn to practice law, learn to do team sport, etc.. Basically have as many hobbies as you can. (Switching every X months) making sure they have as little in common as possible. Here's a talk on how long it takes to become 'good enough' at anything: [VIDEO] Have fun ;)"},{"id":7324943,"author":"joshkaufman","month":"2014-03","story":"Can 10,000 hours of practice make you an expert?","stance":"reference","why":"About 20 hours of practice yields practical competence.","quote":"the threshold for acquiring new skills for personal or professional use is somewhere around 20 hours.","text":"I recently wrote a book on this topic[1][2], and you're quite right - most people seem to get wrapped up in the status that surrounds the idea of expertise when the real value of this research is what it tells us about how to go about acquiring new skills for personal or professional use. As you mentioned, practicing to the point of enjoying the skill is the most effective strategy. If you can reach the point where practicing is fun, you're much more likely to continue leveling up. Adult learners typically find the first few hours of practicing a new skill extremely frustrating, and a very low percentage will complete even 2-3 hours of deliberate practice if the skill is complex, ambiguous, or challenging. Somewhere around hours 3-10, however, you start to see clear improvements, and pushing through frustration is much less of a factor. That's the point where you start seeing clear results. In my research/experience, the threshold for acquiring new skills for personal or professional use is somewhere around 20 hours. That's not \"expertise\" - it's being able to produce a desired result or enjoy the experience. In the vast majority of cases, you don't need to be an \"expert\" to derive value from practicing new skills. Since most people have a hard time pushing through the early frustration barrier, 10-20 hours of strategic, deliberate practice will also give you a surprising level of competence compared to other people. All it really takes is having a smart strategy that ensures"}],"r":[["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["rcwngbUrZNg",1]]},"JOLtS4VUcvQ":{"wy":"It is held up as a model of clear, well-paced educational film and of the old-era instructional style.","q":{"x":"The editing, pacing, and style were better.","i":19986127,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19986127,"author":"Animats","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Model of good educational film editing, pacing and demonstration.","quote":"The editing, pacing, and style were better.","text":"The editing, pacing, and style were better. Educational videos today tend to go to one of two extremes. Either they're a talking head with PowerPoint, like most of the \"massively online\" courses. Or they have way too many jump cuts, like theatrical movies which want your attention, not your understanding. There's also an annoying tendency to have distracting music and irrelevant graphics during narration. Here's an old Jam Handy film, \"Spinning Levers\", on how a transmission works.[1] There's a whole series of these Chevrolet films on the Internet Archive, covering major vehicle systems. Things to note: - There's a narrator and a demonstrator. We never see the narrator, and the demonstrator never talks. So the viewer can focus. - The demo models of parts are really good. They start with a simple version and add features until a full transmission has been built up. - There's some simple animation. Animation is used to point out how power flows through the gears. This is much clearer than someone using a pointer. - The editing and narration are very well synchronized. - There's an entertaining part at the beginning and end, so you don't feel like they're beating you over the head with the boring stuff. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42221768,"author":"_xerces_","month":"2024-11","story":"A 15-minute intro to involute gears","stance":"rec","why":"Training video that also treats gear teeth as levers.","quote":"This old training video is great too","text":"This old training video is great too, it also mentions the idea touched upon in the article that gear teeth are levers: Spinning Levers - How A Transmission Works (1936) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38519255,"author":"deaddodo","month":"2023-12","story":"1962 Military Innstructional Video on Magnetic Cores","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a list of good old government training films.","quote":"All of the old US gov't/mil funded education films are pretty good.","text":"All of the old US gov't/mil funded education films are pretty good. * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] Etc."},{"id":23587085,"author":"MaxBarraclough","month":"2020-06","story":"Strike a Solder Joint Behind Enemy Lines","stance":"rec","why":"Old video using physical models to show how transmissions work.","quote":"Both make great use of physical models of the mechanisms.","text":"A couple of other examples: an old US Army training video on the workings of various categories of firearms [0], and an old video on how transmissions work [1]. Both make great use of physical models of the mechanisms. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18346521,"author":"jolmg","month":"2018-10","story":"Why some things are darker when wet (1988)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains transmissions starting from levers and gears as spinning levers.","quote":"I ended up feeling like I could make a basic transmission","text":"Lately, because of a car situation, I've been watching car mechanic videos online. There's some old videos, like 1937 old, made by Chevrolet, that explain really well how different mechanics of a car work. One explains a transmission starting from levers[1], how they work to give mechanical advantage, and how gears are basically spinning levers. I ended up feeling like I could make a basic transmission if I only knew how to make metal pieces in the shapes I want. From there I've been looking at people disassembling automatic transmission and explaining the different parts. I gotta say, learning how these machines work mechanical advantage to something so convenient has really raised my interest in physics and mechanical engineering. I don't remember simple machines[2] being something so interesting back in physics class. I don't blame the teacher, 'cause she was always enthusiastic about physics. I wonder why this is more interesting now than then. It's probably just that, now that I'm older, I can understand complex applications of those fundamentals being taught at school. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":48456938,"author":"2143","month":"2026-06","story":"Apple WWDC 2026 Livestream","stance":"reference","why":"Example of plain, straightforward explanation from an older era.","quote":"See these old videos, where people talk in a straightforward way","text":"I think this “awesome”, “amazing”, “super exciting” phase came much later than the moon walking era. Remember it’s been over 50 years since humans walked on the moon. Much has changed. See these old videos, where people talk in a straightforward way: Car transmission [VIDEO] How to dial your phone [LINK] The dial comes to town [LINK] Now go watch that Apple video again and you’ll see what I’m talking about."},{"id":39703432,"author":"dTal","month":"2024-03","story":"Technical Manual TM 11-410: The Homing Pigeon (1945)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of straightforward instructional videos from that era.","quote":"","text":"I get a similar feeling looking at instructional videos from that era[0][1], or reading books like Stick and Rudder[2][3], or even the simple brilliance of the visual design of the London Underground[4]. There's a pervasive sense that despite the reams of progress that has been made in the science of human factors, simple straightforwardness is becoming an endangered species. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":36900291,"author":"bramhaag","month":"2023-07","story":"Hydraulic Steering – Principles of Operation (1956) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"One of several vintage car explainer videos.","quote":"Similar videos about cars","text":"Similar videos about cars: Chevrolet Suspension (1938): [LINK] How Differential Steering Works (1937): [LINK] How A Transmission Works (1936): [VIDEO] And as a bonus, an explanation about transistors from 1953: [LINK]"},{"id":22311538,"author":"dsfyu404ed","month":"2020-02","story":"Gears","stance":"reference","why":"A well-known video on how transmissions work.","quote":"Here's the spinning levers video you've all already seen a million times","text":"sigh If I don't post it someone else will. Here's the spinning levers video you've all already seen a million times: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["GqmsQeSzMdw",1],["t4vKPhjcMZg",1],["Ip3X9LOh2dk",1],["QKCK4lJLQHU",1],["Z4oYSByyRak",1],["yYAw79386WI",1]]},"ScEPu1cs4l0":{"wy":"Recommended as one of the best explanations of why Clojure and a functional, immutable approach to state exist.","q":{"x":"One of the best \"why Clojure\" talks that I've seen","i":27389178,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27389178,"author":"raspasov","month":"2021-06","story":"Tour of our 250k line Clojure codebase","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why-Clojure talk from OOP perspective","quote":"One of the best \"why Clojure\" talks that I've seen","text":"I personally started with this talk by Rich Hickey (person who made Clojure) [VIDEO] One of the best \"why Clojure\" talks that I've seen, especially for somebody coming from an OOP perspective, like myself."},{"id":48377453,"author":"onlyrealcuzzo","month":"2026-06","story":"My thoughts after using Clojure for about a month","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on limits of functional languages","quote":"I found this to be one of the more interesting talks I've watched.","text":"Rich Hickey's: Are We There Yet may be interesting to any one here: [VIDEO] I found this to be one of the more interesting talks I've watched. Like you (I think) - I love functional languages. But there's a problem I can't really figure out how to articulate where they reach a level where they stop \"just working\" imo. Maybe it's just me being too dumb."},{"id":30886317,"author":"joshlemer","month":"2022-04","story":"Two Vexing Problems in Functional Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey on modeling entities and information accumulation","quote":"Rich Hickey made a great talk on this in 2009","text":"Rich Hickey made a great talk on this in 2009 [VIDEO] An other way to think about it is that the programs we write do not literally simulate entities (users, other domain objects like books, students, professors, classes, etc). Our software simulates the record keeping devices that store /protect information regarding these entities. We are most of the time writing information processing systems, not simulation systems and information accumulates, rather than updates in places. Like, e.g. when there's a new president elected, we don't all go out with our erasers and remove all references to change sentences from \"The President is Donald Trump\" to say \"The President is Joe Biden\", instead we accumulate new facts without remembering the old."},{"id":30298409,"author":"simongray","month":"2022-02","story":"Case against OOP is understated, not overstated (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey's critique of OOP","quote":"I think \"Are We There Yet?\" is the most pertinent","text":"If you're going to reference a Rich Hickey take-down of OOP, I think \"Are We There Yet?\" is the most pertinent: [VIDEO] Of course, Simple Made Easy is excellent too, probably his most influential talk."},{"id":29945352,"author":"raspasov","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: New Programming Language?","stance":"rec","why":"Exploring Clojure for unified data handling","quote":"I highly recommend this talk","text":">> I am surprised that there are still no software development platforms where you use one language for all levels and do not have to translate between different ways of representing data and interacting with services. You've spotted a problem that I think many people are blind to. I agree 100%. If you'd like to explore more about Clojure, I highly recommend this talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":30884640,"author":"adamkl","month":"2022-04","story":"Two Vexing Problems in Functional Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's take on time and mutability","quote":"similar, interesting, and compelling take on time and mutability","text":"Late to the party here, but what you propose is not being cheeky. Rich Hickey (Clojure creator) himself has a similar, interesting, and compelling take on time and mutability [0]. This perspective is the underlying basis of Datomic. [0] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["HT_KdFCVEdc",1],["eMlx5fFNoYc",1]]},"hlrv7DIHllE":{"wy":"HN recommends it to engineers as a skeptical counterweight to technology boosterism, especially for those building AI and consumer tech features.","q":{"x":"I suggest the people working on those features watch Neil Postman's \"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\"","i":45294463,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45294463,"author":"jackdoe","month":"2025-09","story":"Chrome's New AI Features","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Suggested viewing for engineers building Chrome AI features.","quote":"I suggest the people working on those features watch Neil Postman's \"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\"","text":"I suggest the people working on those features watch Neil Postman's \"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\": [VIDEO] Part of me is actually excited for the acceleration of the end of the web, I even wrote [LINK] few days ago in frustration. The sooner it ends the better. I really don't want to see \"summarization\" tokens of other people's tokens."},{"id":37423031,"author":"blueridge","month":"2023-09","story":"Like privacy? Then smart devices are a dumb idea","stance":"rec","why":"Postman on technology solving problems no one has.","quote":"Here's Neil Postman on using technology to solve problems that no one has, linked to start at 20:03","text":"Yes, likewise. We have no smart devices, no TV in the house, no cameras or smart doorbells or Internet enabled appliances. People are always disoriented when they visit because there is no TV and no Echo-like device on the kitchen counter. Here's Neil Postman on using technology to solve problems that no one has, linked to start at 20:03: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22585886,"author":"zulgan","month":"2020-03","story":"Ted Kaczynski's Manifesto [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"For those questioning technology's value and societal impact.","quote":"if you enjoy questioning the value of technology and the impact on our society, check out Neil Postman's","text":"a bit off topic, but if you enjoy questioning the value of technology and the impact on our society, check out Neil Postman's \"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11996211,"author":"fkooman","month":"2016-06","story":"The Moral Economy of Tech","stance":"rec","why":"Postman on why wiring up schools to the information superhighway was misguided.","quote":"It specifically mentions education and how the \"wiring up to the information super highway\" was misguided.","text":"> Not necessarily more smarts, but better tech means more affordable connectivity and education, and better education too. This is not true. Neil Postman has something to say about this, already in 1996, in \"The Surrender of Culture to Technology\". It specifically mentions education and how the \"wiring up to the information super highway\" was misguided. [VIDEO] It is very interesting in reading/watching Maciej, Neil Postman and also Evgeny Morozov to put things in perspective."},{"id":7754646,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2014-05","story":"Neil Postman: Things We Need to Know About Technological Change (1998) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Postman lecture and Q&A on information not being the problem.","quote":"There's a related video from a March, 1997 lecture, in which Postman discusses the questions.","text":"There's a related video from a March, 1997 lecture, in which Postman discusses the questions. Though I agree with much of what he says, I also feel he suffered from a bit of failure of imagination in conceiving of what the possible utility of information might be. [VIDEO] Two bits, both in the Q&A, particularly stand out. First: the observation that as the hoi polloi are told they need to acquire technical skills, the ruling class retains its power by learning how to speak and write effectively. Second: \"I feel very strongly about this: what is distracting us from solving some of the problems that you mention is a kind of a world view that's being promoted now by computer technology, that the main reason we have problems in the world is that we have insufficient information. If only we could get more information, easier to access, and get it faster, then we can solve this problem, or any of the others you mention. And I think this is an awesome conceit, and a terrible mis-judgement. Look, if there are children starving anyplace, it's not because we have insufficient information. if there's crime rampant in the streets in New York, Detroit, and Chicago, it's not because we have insufficient information. If the ozone layer is being depleted and the rainforest is disappearing, it's not because we have insufficient information. And if you can't get along with your own relatives, it's not because you have insufficient information. But we have come to believe that that is the sourc"}],"r":[["-kFOXP026eE",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["HXAW2GseICs",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["VnvGwFegbC8",1],["jh8supIUj6c",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1]]},"8fHi36dvTdE":{"wy":"HN recommends it for a deep theory of why music sounds the way it does.","q":{"x":"Check out the Norton Lectures especially, where Bernstein analyzes musical structure with references to Chomsky.","i":35418826,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35418826,"author":"coolandsmartrr","month":"2023-04","story":"If Philip Glass Were a Film","stance":"rec","why":"Bernstein analyzes musical structure with references to Chomsky","quote":"Check out the Norton Lectures especially, where Bernstein analyzes musical structure with references to Chomsky.","text":"I feel that this video inherits the spirit of Leonard Bernstein's lectures, particularly the Young People's Concert and the Norton Lectures at Harvard. Bernstein likened music to linguistics, calling each movement of a symphony \"a musical sentence.\"[1] While Two Set Violin may have had facetious intentions, they have faithfully reenacted tensions within the music in their conversation. [1] Check out the Norton Lectures especially, where Bernstein analyzes musical structure with references to Chomsky. The maestro was really well learned. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28383916,"author":"sseagull","month":"2021-09","story":"Music Theory for the 21st-Century Classroom","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why notes an octave apart sound similar","quote":"This one has some good stuff about why notes an octave apart sound similar, etc.","text":"Leonard Bernstein has some lectures that might be helpful. This one has some good stuff about why notes an octave apart sound similar, etc. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24210695,"author":"sailfast","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: How to Become a Music Buff?","stance":"rec","why":"Bernstein lectures on the language of music for deeper appreciation","quote":"Bernstein did the Norton Lectures in the 70s around a language of music","text":"I agree 100% with this. While you can appreciate music it's hard to be a \"buff\" unless you can try to understand the effort or absolute virtuosity that goes into playing a really complex piece. Knowing that you like the way music sounds is great, but understanding _why_ a particular harmonic shift / change is unexpected, novel, or unique is even better. That said, understanding that sort of theory takes a lot of work and investment. If you can't make that investment, I'd say watching some videos about theory, or reading up on production, or technique on any instrument will help gain a deeper understanding. Bernstein did the Norton Lectures in the 70s around a language of music ( [VIDEO] ) but even watching somebody like Cory Wong or Jacob Collier go through their composition, playing and process (there are many other artists that do this stuff to) is great for appreciation / buffery."},{"id":21826881,"author":"sailfast","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: How Can I Learn Music Theory?","stance":"rec","why":"Harvard lectures on why things sound good to our ears","quote":"I've also enjoyed the Leonard Bernstein Norton lectures at Harvard in terms of overall theory of why things sound good to our ears","text":"I haven't watched the whole thing but this lesson from Barry Harris (and the whole Lincoln Center Jazz Academy) is pretty great for thinking about harmony, solos, and improvisation / voicing: [LINK] I've also enjoyed the Leonard Bernstein Norton lectures at Harvard in terms of overall theory of why things sound good to our ears: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17930837,"author":"beliu","month":"2018-09","story":"We know music is pleasurable, the question is why?","stance":"rec","why":"Bernstein lectures drawing analogies between music and linguistics","quote":"Draws analogies to concepts in linguistics and CFGs","text":"Leonard Bernstein gave his answer on the question in a series of lectures titled \"The Unanswered Question.\" Draws analogies to concepts in linguistics and CFGs: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xsSnOQynTHs",1]]},"C_r5UJrxcck":{"wy":"The go-to demonstration in car-safety debates that old cars were not better, showing crumple zones and a rigid passenger cell at work.","q":{"x":"I always liked this IIHS crash test: 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu It really shows the progress.","i":31497869,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38990631,"author":"xnx","month":"2024-01","story":"Cars suck, man","stance":"rec","why":"A one-minute demonstration of how much safer modern cars are.","quote":"Take one minute to watch","text":"Agreeing with the article. Car software sucks, but hardware is great. Cars have more horsepower, are more fuel efficient, are more reliable, and are most importantly much safer ( to their passengers at least) than they've ever been. Take one minute to watch \"1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu IIHS crash test\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32805166,"author":"Guest9081239812","month":"2022-09","story":"Heavier cars are safer for their drivers, but far deadlier for everyone else","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting crash test of an old car versus a modern one.","quote":"Here's an interesting crash test comparing one to a modern vehicle.","text":"Those old boats might be big, but they're not exactly safe or even that heavy. Here's an interesting crash test comparing one to a modern vehicle. [VIDEO] Both of those cars weigh about 3,500lbs. Now swap in a 5,000lbs Tesla, or that new 9,000lbs Hummer EV. That big old car will be nothing but a pile of twisted metal. In short, I don't think you can compare large cars of the past to ones that exist today."},{"id":32592964,"author":"dumpsterlid","month":"2022-08","story":"The end of the manual transmission is near?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows survivability difference between classic and modern car cabins.","quote":"you can get out and walk away from a crash in your ugly newer car","text":"\"Even for people in the car, some older car designs put the drivers legs under the engine block. In a head on collision at speed, the legs were the crumple zone (meanwhile the steering column was being pushed through your torso).\" Anytime anyone waxes nostalgic about how \"they dont make cars like they used\" I think about this video. They sure as hell don't make cars like they used too, you can get out and walk away from a crash in your ugly newer car that would brutally end your life in one of those beautiful classic cars. For anyone that is curious the key thing to pay attention to in survivability here (besides yah know... the airbag) is.. does the interior cab of the car change shape? The cab of the old car violently changes shape in a way that would destroy parts of your body whereas the newer car's cab doesnt seem to be affected at all. Even if getting crushed between the seats and dashboard didnt kill you immediately in the bel air, I struggle to imagine how emergency first responders would even get you out of that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31497869,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2022-05","story":"Why isn’t new technology making us more productive?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows safety progress from seatbelts, rigid cage and airbags.","quote":"I always liked this IIHS crash test: 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu It really shows the progress.","text":"[VIDEO] I always liked this IIHS crash test: 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu It really shows the progress. Seatbelts, the \"rigid cage\" to protect the driver, air bags, etc. etc. The 1959 Bel Air crash-dummy is clearly dead, bouncing around the cabin during the crash. The 2009 Malibu crash-dummy has a sprained neck and the face hurts from hitting the air-bag, but clearly survives."},{"id":18136055,"author":"Reelin","month":"2018-10","story":"Honda will use GM’s self-driving technology, invests in Cruise","stance":"rec","why":"Old versus new car crash test comparison.","quote":"Since this touches on the safety of old vs new cars","text":"Since this touches on the safety of old vs new cars, I'd just like to leave this link to an \"old vs new\" crash test video here for anyone who might not have seen it before. [VIDEO]"},{"id":49001075,"author":"simoncion","month":"2026-07","story":"OpenAI and Hugging Face partner to address security incident","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy between old car safety and LLM safety.","quote":"As an aside: you may have seen this video","text":"> LLMs are impossible to make safe in the same sense that humans cannot be made safe. No. LLMs are impossible to make safe in the same sense that a car designed as if it was the ~1940's would be impossible to make safe for its passengers during an at-speed collision. There's only so much you can do if you're committed to using plate glass, rigid steel everything, and leaving out occupant safety belts because they're unpopular and spoil the lines of the cabin. [0] Back in the day, \"the people in the cabin are the crumple zone\" was state of the art, but we've learned an awful lot about how to make much, much safer personal vehicles in the ~75 years since then. It'd be massively irresponsible to design and sell a car today that ignored the safety and engineering lessons we've learned since then. \"Funnily\" enough, the major LLM providers have designed and are selling access to systems that they very much want to be used in situations where you need a reliable, safe tool... but they've -somehow- ignored one of the most fundamental lessons we've learned about the design of safe software systems that are intended to be used in the presence of attacker-controlled inputs. [1] What they've done is no less irresponsible than designing and selling a new car that conforms to the very latest safety regs of the 1940's... AFAIK, it's so irresponsible to design and sell such a car commercially that -in the US- it's a violation of federal law to do so. As an aside: you may have seen this video"},{"id":36865424,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2023-07","story":"Autoenshittification. How the computer killed capitalism. – by Cory Doctorow","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that modern cars are far safer.","quote":"You better believe that modern cars are better","text":"[VIDEO] Malibu vs Bel Air crash test. You better believe that modern cars are better, its incredible how _BAD_ cars were before we cared about safety. And that's just safety. Today's cars have better MPG, go faster, have more power, easier to maintain, consistently hit 150,000 miles, etc. etc."},{"id":34742384,"author":"beamgirl","month":"2023-02","story":"The $20 an hour Cessna 172 experiment","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks rigid-box myth by comparing crumple behavior.","quote":"They both crumple, but the 2009 only crumples in the crumple zones.","text":"So the whole rigid box thing is kinda a \"they dont make em like this anymore\" myth. Here's a 1959 Chevy crashed into a 2009 Chevy [VIDEO] . They both crumple, but the 2009 only crumples in the crumple zones. The crumpling on the 1959 impala is more spread out and you can see includes more of the interior"},{"id":33775485,"author":"Karellen","month":"2022-11","story":"The factory that only builds white Toyota Land Cruisers","stance":"reference","why":"Shows decades of safety advances matter.","quote":"there were plenty of other advances in the 22 years between then and when that Malibu was made in 2009","text":"1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu IIHS crash test: [VIDEO] Sure, a lot of advances in safety were made in the 28 years between 1959 and 1987, so your car won't fare as badly as the Bel Air, but there were plenty of other advances in the 22 years between then and when that Malibu was made in 2009, and likely quite a few in the 13 years since. It's a free country, so you can take your chances if you want. I wouldn't rate them that highly though, FWIW. ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯"},{"id":32112002,"author":"drewzero1","month":"2022-07","story":"BMW Makes Heated Seats an $18/Month Subscription Service – Again","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates crumple zones absorbing impact.","quote":"Crumple zones do a lot to absorb forces of impact and prevent intrusion into the passenger compartment.","text":"That is certainly a factor. Crumple zones do a lot to absorb forces of impact and prevent intrusion into the passenger compartment. See also: [0] We're getting to the point where a lot of cars that could be called \"classic\" still have the modern safety features without all of the connected bs. Most late 90s-early 2000s cars have multiple airbags, crumple zones, active seatbelts etc. without also needing to have subscriptions, telemetry, or really any connectedness at all. Granted, subscription-based safety features aren't the point here, but they're certainly a future possibility (as discussed here last year with a motorcycle safety accessory[1]). [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":31295242,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2022-05","story":"Shaving is an example of how consumer products extract more money","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates modern cars being safer than old ones.","quote":"they're much safer now, much better gas mileage...","text":"This reads like something my 88-year-old father would write. \"Everything used to be better, and hamburgers should cost a nickle!\" Look, as consumers, we make choices. You can still get a safety-razor on Amazon if you want. The trouble is... man, if you don't know how to use them right, you can get some nasty cuts. For me... modern idiot-proof razors that this article rants against... well they're just bliss. I can't remember the last time I cut myself shaving. My Dad said something like, \"They sure don't make cars like they used to!\" And I said, \"No, they don't... they're much safer now, much better gas mileage...\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30767656,"author":"judge2020","month":"2022-03","story":"Mercedes to accept legal responsibility for a vehicle when Drive Pilot is active","stance":"reference","why":"Shows newer cars are far safer than old ones.","quote":"newer cars are magnitudes safer than anything 20+ years old.","text":"Nowadays it depends on how new the car is, as newer cars are magnitudes safer than anything 20+ years old. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26027745,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2021-02","story":"$1,944 for a coronavirus test? Readers helped us spot an unusual trend","stance":"reference","why":"Debunks idea that old things were better using crash test.","quote":"Check out the IIHS crashing a 60s bel air into a modern malibu.","text":"It's also not even remotely true. Check out the IIHS crashing a 60s bel air into a modern malibu. \"They don't make them like they used to\" has always been a nostalgic crock of shit, like the people who think the NES was the golden age of video games, despite the vast majority of the 700 or so releases being utter trash for $60 that sometimes didn't even work! [VIDEO]"},{"id":26052625,"author":"judge2020","month":"2021-02","story":"Tesla: Recalled touchscreens were meant to only last 5-6 years","stance":"reference","why":"Most extreme old-versus-new crash comparison.","quote":"the most extreme comparison - 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu","text":"The biggest problem with old[er] cars is that they don't compare to modern safety systems like large crumple zones and advanced airbags. There are many examples where even a 1-year gap in make year will greatly increase the safety of passengers: [LINK] (2015 Nissan Tsuru vs. 2016 Nissan Versa) [LINK] (2015 and 2019 Jeep Cherokee driver-side small overlap IIHS crash test comparison) [LINK] (2016 and 2019 Honda HR-V driver-side small overlap comparison) [VIDEO] (the most extreme comparison - 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu)"},{"id":11589337,"author":"noir_lord","month":"2016-04","story":"How Cheap Can Electric Vehicles Get?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows chassis structure matters more than weight in crashes.","quote":"I'd sooner be in the light car with a chassis that doesn't get crushed into the cabin than a heavy car which does.","text":"Absolutely, also if you get hit from the side there is a lot more to it than just the weight of the car. I'd sooner be in the light car with a chassis that doesn't get crushed into the cabin than a heavy car which does. [VIDEO] I know which one Id like to be in.."},{"id":7959848,"author":"LnxPrgr3","month":"2014-06","story":"Drinking behind 1 in 10 deaths of working-age adults","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how much safer modern cars are in a crash.","quote":"Modern cars have made it a lot safer to be in a crash.","text":"Modern cars have made it a lot safer to be in a crash. I'd hate to have been driving the 1959 Bel Air in this video: [VIDEO] Still, since not much else is likely to kill the average American 20-something, car crashes rank relatively high, and alcohol remains involved in about a third of all fatal crashes in the US."}],"r":[["5AVO1IyfA9M",1],["hJP5GqnTrNo",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["ySuUZEjARPY",1]]},"rzQEIRRJ2T0":{"wy":"HN users share it as the quick demo that shows what Magit can do, for people unfamiliar with it.","q":{"x":"I always share this demo with people to show off just a tiny portion of what it's capable of doing","i":23107920,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23107920,"author":"giggles_giggles","month":"2020-05","story":"Making Emacs Popular Again","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Demo of a small portion of Magit's capabilities","quote":"I always share this demo with people to show off just a tiny portion of what it's capable of doing","text":"I cannot possibly encourage you more to check out magit. It is unbelievably good. I always share this demo with people to show off just a tiny portion of what it's capable of doing [VIDEO] My skills with git have gotten better after using magit, too, because it brings the power of git to the surface. It's a really fantastic git porcelain and the fact that it is deeply integrated into Emacs just makes it absolutely second to none. Magit is easily one of my favorite pieces of software and I will leap at the chance to sing its praises."},{"id":17689383,"author":"nickbarnwell","month":"2018-08","story":"Show HN: I made a tool that made me faster at Git","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short screencast showing Magit in action","quote":"It's easier to see it in action than explain it.","text":"If you're an emacs user, I can't recommend magit [1] enough. I was a diehard CLI user and had flags and aliases out the wazoo, and it was still a step change in usabilty and power for me. Staging hunks, rebasing, and stashing are all vastly easier. Amending or editing a commit is a breeze, and it's tied in to all of the other emacs tools you already use, e.g. org-mode to boot! It's easier to see it in action than explain it. If you've two minutes to spare, check out this emacsrocks screencast [2], or Howard Abram's longer presentation from the PDX Emacs Hackers meetup [3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":24441831,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2020-09","story":"Magit – A Git Porcelain inside Emacs","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of hunk- and line-level staging in Magit","quote":"This demo is pretty good I think","text":"Not sure about other editors, but Magit's hunk- and line-level staging/unstaging is hard to beat, IMO. For hunk-level staging, you put your cursor on the hunk you want to add and press \"s\". If you want to split that and only stage a few lines, you just select those lines and press \"s\". This demo is pretty good I think: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20374487,"author":"d0mine","month":"2019-07","story":"Hub: Use GitHub from the Command Line","stance":"rec","why":"Intro screencast for those unfamiliar with Magit","quote":"If you are not familar with Magit, there is a screencast","text":"There is Magit Forge which is not limited to Github [LINK] If you are not familar with Magit, there is a screencast: Emacs Rocks! Episode 17: Magit [VIDEO]"},{"id":15376926,"author":"tarsius","month":"2017-10","story":"Git, Graphs and Software Engineering","stance":"reference","why":"User-made Magit screencasts","quote":"You might also want to check out some of the screencasts that were created by users","text":"The video is indeed... not so great. I would go as far as to say that this might be the Kickstarter campaign with the crappiest video that ever succeeded. But at some point I just had to launch instead of continuing to hope I would eventually get around to launching the perfect campaign. I did however make an effort to give non-Emacs a better glimpse into how Magit works using a few articles listed at [1]. You might also want to check out some of the screencasts that were created by users [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18727299,"author":"d0mine","month":"2018-12","story":"Work with Git Forges inside Emacs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me (I hear it in the voice of the narrator)","text":"> It’s really that good It reminds me (I hear it in the voice of the narrator) \"Emacs Rocks! Episode 17: Magit\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["B9ly7JjqEb0",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["GlXNVnftaNs",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["0g9BcZvQbXU",1],["PriwCi6SzLo",1]]},"XDM3MqHln8U":{"wy":"Recommended as a first-hand account from NSA whistleblowers, often linked in surveillance and Snowden threads.","q":{"x":"You may be interested in watching this talk from 29c3 where Drake and Binney told their stories in their own words","i":7170448,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":14357255,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-05","story":"Chelsea Manning Is Expected to Leave Prison, 28 Years Early","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thomas Drake's talk; other panelists also worth watching","quote":"the other talks by Jesselyn Radack and William Binney in the same video are also worth watching","text":"> Especially impressed by Thomas. If you haven't seen it, I suggest watching Thomas Drake's talk[1] at 29c3. [1] [VIDEO] (although I've linked to Thomas Drake's talk, the other talks by Jesselyn Radack and William Binney in the same video are also worth watching)"},{"id":7170448,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2014-02","story":"US Media Blacks Out Snowden Interview","stance":"rec","why":"Drake and Binney tell their stories in their own words","quote":"You may be interested in watching this talk from 29c3 where Drake and Binney told their stories in their own words","text":"[LINK] The EFF has been supporting these former NSA agents' whistleblowing efforts for a while now. Also, Ellsberg - author of the Pentagon Papers - has signed it in support. While it's not a character reference, it is worth observing that the rather dramatic efforts the NSA has gone though to threaten and prosecute these whistleblowers. You may be interested in watching this talk from 29c3 where Drake and Binney told their stories in their own words: [VIDEO] And yes, it's a hell of an accusation. It's also the one that finally made this NSA mess make sense, with their description of THINTHREAD being dropped because it was too inexpensive . It's all about moving money to their contractor friends."},{"id":6972706,"author":"PavlovsCat","month":"2013-12","story":"Glenn Greenwald keynote at Chaos Communication Congress [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Panel on what happens when telling truth about secret power becomes a crime","quote":"Also from last year's 29c3: Jesselyn Radack, Thomas Drake & William Binney","text":"Also from last year's 29c3: Jesselyn Radack, Thomas Drake & William Binney, \"Enemies of the State: What Happens When Telling the Truth about Secret US Government Power Becomes a Crime\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":5000759,"author":"contingencies","month":"2013-01","story":"There's no avoiding Google+","stance":"rec","why":"Whistleblower panel that motivated the commenter to block Google domains","quote":"after watching the 29C3 keynote ( ) and whistleblower panel ( ) a couple of days ago I blocked all the main google domains","text":"Google's growing G+ surveillance is a worry. This is why after watching the 29C3 keynote ( [LINK] ) and whistleblower panel ( [VIDEO] ) a couple of days ago I blocked all the main google domains (mail, maps, G+, accounts, picasa, etc.) via /etc/hosts and committed to only accessing Gmail via Tor ( [LINK] ). Facebook I already deleted and block via AdBlockPlus (though I now recommend [LINK] ), but G+ is harder and more insidious to stay free of. After a few days of use, I am quite happy with my new strategy: keep email (or anything personally identifying, like banking) in a different and anonymized browser. Keep everything else in a general browser that never sees you identified. Edit: add URLs, TrueBlockPlus reference."},{"id":6186725,"author":"krek","month":"2013-08","story":"NSA revelations could hurt collaboration with 'betrayed' hackers","stance":"reference","why":"Binney's technical talk on ThinThread discarding private data","quote":"See his 29C3 technical talk where he goes over it.","text":"> his own \"ThinThread\" system was designed to do exactly that, but with better technical controls over who could view the data. That's plainly false. His system was specifically designed to throw-out private data, that is, never to store it. There is no data to view if it's not stored. See his 29C3 technical talk where he goes over it. [1] >The notion that Binney is a staunch opponent of PRISM-style surveillance is revisionist. This ignores nearly everything Binney has actually said when asked about why he came forward to blow the whistle on NSA's spying activities. Also, see above. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5516284,"author":"epoxyhockey","month":"2013-04","story":"Show HN: Hide Secret Messages In Facebook Photos With My Chrome Extension","stance":"reference","why":"Panel mentions Facebook at 56:07 as evidence surveillance is not secret","quote":"(note that Facebook was specifically mentioned @ 56:07)","text":"God you guys need to start wearing tinfoil hats It's not really a secret: [VIDEO] (note that Facebook was specifically mentioned @ 56:07)"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"RQv5CVELG3U":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear debunking of the retrocausality interpretation of the quantum eraser.","q":{"x":"Later Sabine Hossenfelder did a video debunking the proposition that DCQE somehow showed that the past was being rewritten.","i":43197087,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48986679,"author":"TxFkdZ","month":"2026-07","story":"You probably misunderstand the double slit experiment [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Nice explanation of the delayed-choice quantum eraser","quote":"She has a nice video on delayed-choice quantum eraser","text":">The delayed-choice quantum eraser wasn’t carried out experimentally until 1999, so I think it’s a bit misleading to say this is what makes the double-slit experiment (first carried out with matter in 1927) so weird. I think Sabine is right, the weirdest thing in the quantum eraser experiment is still the FTL update of the wavefunction. She has a nice video on delayed-choice quantum eraser: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43383111,"author":"wruza","month":"2025-03","story":"Did the Particle Go Through the Two Slits, or Did the Wave Function?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the two-cluster picture in popsci videos is misleading","quote":"you don't get \"two clusters\" actually. They are both statistical parts of a single [non-]interference pattern.","text":"I think Sabine explained this social effect few years ago. I know she's a little controversial, but the key thing in the video (as opposed to all other videos about DSE on the internet) was that you don't get \"two clusters\" actually. They are both statistical parts of a single [non-]interference pattern. \"||\" is a lie. I'm not in a physics rebel camp and don't prefer Sabine either, but after that I sort of lost trust in the interpretations that can't even get the resulting picture right. I even suspect that showing dumbed results amplifies \"wow\" effect and monetizes better. [VIDEO] This is the video if you're interested. Again, I'm no physicist and don't know if explanations are legit or statistically correct. But that little || trick that all other popsci videos play on you, that's a true concern."},{"id":43197087,"author":"calny","month":"2025-02","story":"Time Warp: Delayed-choice quantum erasure","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the claim that delayed-choice eraser rewrites the past","quote":"Later Sabine Hossenfelder did a video debunking the proposition that DCQE somehow showed that the past was being rewritten.","text":"PBS Spacetime did an interesting video on DCQE, but it tripped me up trying to fully understand what was happening: [LINK] ... Later Sabine Hossenfelder did a video debunking the proposition that DCQE somehow showed that the past was being rewritten. [VIDEO] And Matt from PBS Spacetime acknowledged she was right in this respectful comment: > Sabine, this is amazing. You are, as usual, 100% right. The delayed choice quantum eraser is a prime example of over-mystification of quantum mechanics, even WITHIN the field of quantum mechanics! I (Matt) was guilty of embracing the quantum woo in that episode 5 years ago. Since then I've obsessed over this family of experiments and my thinking shifted quite a bit."},{"id":43197794,"author":"anotherpaulg","month":"2025-02","story":"Time Warp: Delayed-choice quantum erasure","stance":"rec","why":"High quality discussion demystifying eraser experiments","quote":"Two other high quality discussions of eraser experiments are by Sean Caroll [0] and Sabine Hossenfelder [1].","text":"This is a very interesting, novel take on explaining the delayed choice quantum eraser. I think I can summarize it as follows: The signal photon hits the screen, which is a measurement. The entangled idler's wave function is thereby constrained by that measurement, influencing the probabilities of later detecting it at each of the D1-4 detectors. It's not that the fate/erasing of the idler changes the already committed path(s) of the signal. It's that the measurement of the signal constrains the subsequent detection of the idler. Two other high quality discussions of eraser experiments are by Sean Caroll [0] and Sabine Hossenfelder [1]. Like the OP, both Sabine and Sean demystify/debunk these experiments. These three discussions all use different language to explain the outcome, which is clearly predicted by the QM math. Sean's article includes the gist of the math. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29628619,"author":"rafaelero","month":"2021-12","story":"Does superdeterminism save quantum mechanics?","stance":"rec","why":"Sabine's video explaining the quantum eraser experiment","quote":"Perhaps you will be interested on another video from Sabine.","text":"Ok, that's definitely not what OP said. Of course the particle still can interfere with itself after the slit, it's just that when the measurement is made, the pattern of two waves interfering gets much weaker/non-existent. Also, the experiment you are alluding to does not say what you think it says. Perhaps you will be interested on another video from Sabine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44771066,"author":"renox","month":"2025-08","story":"Double-slit experiment holds up when stripped to its quantum essentials","stance":"reference","why":"Argues delayed choice is a poor interpretation of the data","quote":"As for the 'delayed choices' IMHO it is a poor interpretation of the data","text":"> I am more interested in its explanation, now that the theory has been proven correct again and again. What do you call an explanation? An interpretation of QM? There are dozens but none are especially satisfying.. As for the 'delayed choices' IMHO it is a poor interpretation of the data: see [VIDEO] for example."}],"r":[["tP-Ipsat90c",1]]},"RTB5XhjbgZA":{"wy":"Recommended as a meditative, geeky tour of alternative display designs.","q":{"x":"here is a meditative video on alternative segmented displays.","i":47768007,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47768007,"author":"gherkinnn","month":"2026-04","story":"Air Powered Segment Display? [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Meditative tour of alternative segmented displays.","quote":"here is a meditative video on alternative segmented displays.","text":"If you liked the pneumatic segmented display like I did, here is a meditative video on alternative segmented displays. [VIDEO] No affiliation, just one of my favourite videos."},{"id":35676952,"author":"sph","month":"2023-04","story":"Four-Byte Burger","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good starting point for Posy's geeky explorations of displays.","quote":"Recommended if you want to see deep, geeky and fun explorations of the world of liquid crystal displays","text":"A similar channel in style, thoroughness and love for the 80s and 90s is Posy. [LINK] Recommended if you want to see deep, geeky and fun explorations of the world of liquid crystal displays, Hi-Fi stereos and anything that tickles his fancy. A good starting point: \"Segmented Displays\" — [VIDEO]"},{"id":46032239,"author":"runroader","month":"2025-11","story":"Fran Sans – font inspired by San Francisco light rail displays","stance":"rec","why":"Investigates many weird kinds of segmented displays.","quote":"Posy's YouTube video where he investigates a bunch of weird options for these","text":"The segmented type site that lets you see a bunch of different options reminded me of Posy's YouTube video where he investigates a bunch of weird options for these: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["A-fxij3zM7g",1]]},"5QwOpRh-IfI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a striking visual breakdown of how the song was constructed.","q":{"x":"This video is a great visualization on how it's composed. Absolutely incredible.","i":46476526,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46476526,"author":"alun","month":"2026-01","story":"Daft Punk Easter Egg in the BPM Tempo of Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizes how One More Time is built from a disco sample","quote":"This video is a great visualization on how it's composed. Absolutely incredible.","text":"Daft Punk are very clever in the way they make their music. Their song \"One More Time\" is a simple three-part sample from a 70s disco song. This video is a great visualization on how it's composed. Absolutely incredible. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43592394,"author":"phrotoma","month":"2025-04","story":"Recreating Daft Punk's Something About Us","stance":"rec","why":"Channel with many sampling recreations including this one","quote":"This youtube channel has a whole pile of sampling recreations.","text":"This youtube channel has a whole pile of sampling recreations. Daft Punk: Discovery [LINK] Daft Punk: One More Time [VIDEO] Mos Def: Mathematics [LINK] Pogo: Alice [LINK] Fatboy Slim: Rockafeller Skank [LINK] And this 30 minute compilation that spans four decades is utterly mesmerizing [LINK]"},{"id":26507620,"author":"modeless","month":"2021-03","story":"All Futurama Opening Quotes","stance":"rec","why":"Visual breakdown of samples behind a Daft Punk track","quote":"Wow, what an amazing breakdown video visualization.","text":"Wow, what an amazing breakdown video visualization. I see there are a bunch of these, for e.g. Daft Punk [VIDEO] , Britney Spears [LINK] , Fatboy Slim [LINK]"}],"r":[["jHP942Livy0",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["3uiEyEKji0M",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["hM_h0UA7upI",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1]]},"IHZwWFHWa-w":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a very accessible way to understand the math behind machine learning and LLMs.","q":{"x":"3Blue1Brown which explains math in very accessible way","i":15689936,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15689936,"author":"hal9000xp","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: Best way to get started with AI?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accessible math explanation that removed fear of ML.","quote":"3Blue1Brown which explains math in very accessible way","text":"I'm in the same boat. For long time, I was interested in AI but at the same time intimidated by math. I'm relatively comfortable with discrete mathematics and classical algorithms and at the same time calculus and linear algebra is completely foreign to me. Also, I do not accept way to learn ML without good understanding of core principles behind it. So math is a must. A few months ago, I stumbled upon very amazing YouTube Channel 3Blue1Brown which explains math in very accessible way and at the same time I got feeling that I finally started understanding core ideas behind linear algebra and calculus. Just recently he published 4 videos about deep neural networks: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] So my fear of ML was gone away and I'm very excited to explore whole new world for neural networks and other things like support vector machines etc"},{"id":41926020,"author":"superkuh","month":"2024-10","story":"LLMs Aren't Thinking, They're Just Counting Votes","stance":"rec","why":"Explains gradient descent, helpful for understanding LLMs.","quote":"I suggest checking out 3blue1brown's videos on gradient descent","text":"> when an LLM tells us that the sun rises in the east, it's not because it truly understands astronomy or the solar system. Rather, it has seen the phrase \"sun rises in the east\" repeated so many times in its training data that this becomes the highest probability answer. It's less about understanding and more about pattern recognition. This is not too different from most human person's experience with basic education ( [LINK] ). Though it is obvious that humans can do more than that too. And while \"voting\" that may be a close-enough metaphor for the multi-layer perceptron layers, it is not what is happening in the attention layers. I suggest checking out 3blue1brown's videos on gradient descent [VIDEO] and \"How might LLMs store facts\" [LINK] versus how attention works [LINK]"},{"id":23085192,"author":"miklosme","month":"2020-05","story":"Humans Not Invited","stance":"rec","why":"Quick answer on how neural networks learn.","quote":"For a quick answer, watch this segment","text":"If you want an extensive answer, I recommend the neural network playlist on 3Blue1Brown: [LINK] For a quick answer, watch this segment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15625863,"author":"nouveaux","month":"2017-11","story":"What is backpropagation and what is it doing? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a video series for learning neural networks.","quote":"here are the first few videos of the series","text":"This was very timely for me and for anyone else learning, here are the first few videos of the series: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] (Original video) [LINK]"},{"id":15627132,"author":"afarrell","month":"2017-11","story":"What is backpropagation and what is it doing? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A bit of a side-note, but I think it is an interesting piece of marketing that Amplify Partners decided to sponsor[1] the previous video in this series. I wonder (and hope) we'll see more VCs sponsoring open educational content relevant to their focus. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aircAruvnKk",4],["Ilg3gGewQ5U",2],["tIeHLnjs5U8",2],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["7J7X7aZvMXQ",1],["eMlx5fFNoYc",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1]]},"ImoQqNyRL8Y":{"wy":"Recommended in launch threads as an immersive way to hear what a Falcon Heavy launch actually sounds like.","q":{"x":"the sonic booms of the boosters coming into land are amazing, listen with headphones!","i":19664607,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19664607,"author":"mywacaday","month":"2019-04","story":"F-1 Thrust Chamber","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sonic booms of landing boosters in immersive audio","quote":"the sonic booms of the boosters coming into land are amazing, listen with headphones!","text":"Thanks for that, great video that led me to this, [VIDEO] , sound recording organised by Dustin from SmarterEveryDay of the latest SpaceX launch, the sonic booms of the boosters coming into land are amazing, listen with headphones!"},{"id":33421631,"author":"MontagFTB","month":"2022-11","story":"Falcon Heavy Launch [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the audio phenomenon of a Falcon Heavy launch","quote":"SmarterEveryDay has a great video explaining this audio phenomenon","text":"SmarterEveryDay has a great video explaining this audio phenomenon from a previous Falcon Heavy launch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19642615,"author":"mikejb","month":"2019-04","story":"Arabsat-6A mission [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Acoustical representation of launch with good explanations","quote":"gives you a somewhat acoustical representation and a lot of good explanations","text":"For those not lucky enough to explore this in person, Destin Sandlin from Smarter Every Day made a video [1] (from the test launch last year) that gives you a somewhat acoustical representation and a lot of good explanations. I haven't seen a Falcon Heavy launch, but I've been lucky enough to see a 'normal' Falcon9 launch. There's 2 things that I know I didn't expect: The sound is just beyond anything. And I think the video above brings that across at least to some extend. The other part that surprised me when seeing a launch with my own eyes: The exhaust is bright . It gets lost quite a bit through cameras, so I was surprised how bright those flames are - even 30-45 seconds in, when the rocket is dozens of miles away. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19667365,"author":"sbr464","month":"2019-04","story":"What It's Like to View a SpaceX Falcon Heavy Launch","stance":"rec","why":"Immersive audio of the facilities and launch, liftoff at 3:15","quote":"pretty amazing binaural (immersive) audio recording of the facilities and launch","text":"This youtube channel[1] has a pretty amazing binaural (immersive) audio recording of the facilities and launch. Liftoff is @ 3:15 [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16991214,"author":"chrononaut","month":"2018-05","story":"Why NASA's Next Mars Lander Will Launch from California Instead of Florida","stance":"rec","why":"Binaural recording conveying how a Falcon Heavy launch sounds","quote":"a binaural recording of the SpaceX Falcon Heavy launch","text":"Related, someone submitted a binaural recording of the SpaceX Falcon Heavy launch to SmarterEveryDay: [VIDEO] Conveys your sentiment regarding how it really sounds to be there. It unfortunately cannot convey the actual feeling of being there, and the impact of the launch on the body."}],"r":[["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["hVimVzgtD6w",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1],["j56s46e97Lo",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1]]},"ZecQ64l-gKM":{"wy":"HN points to it in origin-of-life and complexity threads as a fascinating long-form introduction to assembly theory.","q":{"x":"It really changed my naive perspective of life, and the universe.","i":32281776,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32281776,"author":"nomel","month":"2022-07","story":"Scientists discover new “origins of life” chemical reactions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lee Cronin on origins of life and complexity theory.","quote":"It really changed my naive perspective of life, and the universe.","text":"I highly recommend the Lex Fridman podcast with Lee Cronin (#269), about the origins of life and complexity theory [1]. The discussion is really incredible. It really changed my naive perspective of life, and the universe. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31659508,"author":"nomel","month":"2022-06","story":"Amino acids found in asteroid samples collected by Japan's Hayabusa2 probe","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast on Assembly Theory by Lee Cronin.","quote":"I thought it was fascinating.","text":"There’s a great Lex Fridman podcast related to this, about “Assembly Theory”, by Lee Cronin. I thought it was fascinating. Youtube: [VIDEO] Spotify: [LINK]"},{"id":31154470,"author":"egman_ekki","month":"2022-04","story":"Do the same mechanisms that create complex life also create complex technology?","stance":"rec","why":"Lex Fridman interview with Lee Cronin on complexity.","quote":"I found this Lex Fridman's interview with Lee Cronin fascinating and thought-provoking","text":"On the topic of complexity of life created structures, I found this Lex Fridman's interview with Lee Cronin fascinating and thought-provoking: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31741089,"author":"Barrera","month":"2022-06","story":"Japan asteroid probe finds 23 amino acids, researchers confirm","stance":"reference","why":"Lee Cronin podcast related to origin-of-life debate.","quote":"","text":"> A total of 23 types of amino acids were found in asteroid samples brought back by Japan's Hayabusa2 space probe, according to new studies published in the journal Science and elsewhere, shedding further light on the origins of life on Earth. This intersects with a raging debate in Origins of Life research. The Miller-Urey experiment demonstrated the production of amino acids from simple gasses thought the be present in the early Earth atmosphere and energy. The problem is that you can add amino acids into a flask, stir, and not much will happen. You can pump in all the energy you want and all you'll get it tar. Before you can even start to make proteins, which is what makes life, you need enzymes. The trouble with enzymes is that they're, well, proteins. So how do you get the proteins that make proteins in the first place? One line of investigation says that you don't. What happens instead are stepping-stone chemical processes that turn energy from the environment into self-reinforcing cycles that build complex molecules. Over time chemical evolution takes place to produce self-reinforcing systems that make something that's very close to amino acids and/or proteins. We don't know what these processes are, but we can deduce their necessity given the above conundrum. Chemical evolution is a process we observe on the macro scale when, for example, viruses evade vaccine defenses. In other words, finding amino acids just says that the processes that produce amino acids are ubiqu"},{"id":30683677,"author":"ssl232","month":"2022-03","story":"Identifying biosignatures with assembly theory and mass spectrometry","stance":"reference","why":"Lee Cronin on detecting biotic molecules via mass spectrometry.","quote":"This has implications for the detection of life on other planets.","text":"This was recently discussed in an interview with Lee Cronin on the Lex Fridman podcast [1]. The idea is that using only mass spectroscopy and a (hard to compute, but not impossible) look-up table, one can determine with vanishingly low uncertainty whether a complex molecule came from a biotic or abiotic process. This has implications for the detection of life on other planets. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["iwcYp-XT7UI",1],["_L3gNaAVjQ4",1]]},"bC-8y2R6IxI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining deep dive on CRT versus LCD rendering of retro games and the case for CRT shaders.","q":{"x":"This is an excellent deep dive on the issues I spoke of","i":48287320,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46204552,"author":"komali2","month":"2025-12","story":"The Joy of Playing Grandia, on Sega Saturn","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Decent video on CRT display simulation for retro games.","quote":"I strongly recommend anyone getting into retro gaming, try some CRT shaders (or lcd ones for portables)!","text":"I've been getting more and more into retro gaming lately, and something that really made it click for me is leveraging shaders (or overlays) to simulate period-accurate displays. For a Sega Saturn, that'd be some kind of CRT. The art direction in these games are designed to take advantage of the quirks of the CRT, and often look significantly better on a CRT. Noodle just did a decent video on it: [VIDEO] I strongly recommend anyone getting into retro gaming, try some CRT shaders (or lcd ones for portables)!"},{"id":48287320,"author":"dleslie","month":"2026-05","story":"Earthion: A New Mega Drive-Style Shoot-Em-Up","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent deep dive on CRT versus LCD display issues.","quote":"This is an excellent deep dive on the issues I spoke of","text":"The video I linked is taken of a CRT and is not a screenshot of an LCD. This is an excellent deep dive on the issues I spoke of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47533989,"author":"user68858788","month":"2026-03","story":"Light on Glass: Why do you start making a game engine?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining comparison of retro games on CRT versus LCD.","quote":"Here’s an entertaining video showing the difference in retro games on crt and lcd screens.","text":"Interesting article. I hope their engine gets to the point where it actually looks like CRT instead of the blocky filters we see nowadays. Here’s an entertaining video showing the difference in retro games on crt and lcd screens. It’s pretty incredible if you aren’t aware. Games back then were designed on CRTs and can look awful on LCDs in comparison. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xR7XMkjDGw0",1],["8An2SxNFvmU",1]]},"hpAMbpQ8J7g":{"wy":"Frequently linked in threads on philanthropy, ethical consumption and billionaire giving as a short, approachable critique of charity.","q":{"x":"well worth a watch (10 minutes) and approachable by those without philosophical backgrounds","i":9370062,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9370062,"author":"mmanfrin","month":"2015-04","story":"The Noble Edge Effect","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short animated Zizek talk on charity as part of consumerism","quote":"well worth a watch (10 minutes) and approachable by those without philosophical backgrounds","text":"This charity-as-part-of-consumerism is the topic of Zizek's talk First as Tragedy, Then as Farce ; well worth a watch (10 minutes) and approachable by those without philosophical backgrounds: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34179840,"author":"rsj_hn","month":"2022-12","story":"On ‘Plant-Based’","stance":"rec","why":"Beautifully illustrated Zizek talk on ethical consumerism","quote":"in a beautifully illustrated talk here","text":"Slavoj Zizek discusses this idea of \"ethical consumerism\" in a beautifully illustrated talk here: [VIDEO] Needless to say, the idea that you are \"supporting\" something by voluntary exchange (how do you know that they aren't supporting you ?) is a relatively recent development, and is of course completely unworkable. It's just another form of retail therapy."},{"id":18132954,"author":"sithadmin","month":"2018-10","story":"The ugly truth about the ugly produce movement","stance":"rec","why":"Quick Zizek talk on feel-good products and consumer guilt","quote":"Good, quick talk by Zizek on this phenomenon","text":">I know many people who buy into these 'good feelings' products. Good, quick talk by Zizek on this phenomenon: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20755763,"author":"jeanlucas","month":"2019-08","story":"Amazon fires: Record number burning in Brazil rainforest","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek on billionaires giving with one hand what they destroyed with the other","quote":"One interesting point is made by Slavoj Zizek on our society[0]","text":"One interesting point is made by Slavoj Zizek on our society[0] about the \"good\" billionaires that give with one hand what they destroyed with the other. This what it feels like about all of what is happening over there, 1st world countries give \"aid\" to cover the problem themselves did over there. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17980091,"author":"iooi","month":"2018-09","story":"Amazon's Bezos Launches $2B Fund to Help the Homeless","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek argues charitable giving perpetuates the problem","quote":"another interesting take on this is by Slavoj Zizek, who argues that charitable giving perpetuates the problem","text":"I don't necessarily agree with you, but another interesting take on this is by Slavoj Zizek, who argues that charitable giving perpetuates the problem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3770445,"author":"napoleoncomplex","month":"2012-03","story":"Batman's Real Story","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek's attack on charity as sustaining the system that causes problems","quote":"Slavoj Zizek's fiery verbal attack on charity[1]","text":"Slightly off-topic, but your post reminded me of Slavoj Zizek's fiery verbal attack on charity[1], especially the line at the very end: \"It's horrible to see a child whose life is ruined because of an operation that costs 20$. .. (but) If you operate the child, they will live a little bit better, but in the same situation that produced them\" Just an interesting side-note on this debate. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2777648,"author":"napierzaza","month":"2011-07","story":"Apple deals massive patent blow to HTC, Android in serious trouble","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek on charity and consumerism, linked to the Toms shoes point","quote":"","text":"The manufactures that put Android on their devices are not mom and pop electronics firms. So the implication that this is somehow shifting money to poorer people is kind of ridiculous. Unless you mean the top 1% richest people in those countries. Because I don't think the manufacturers will go to their labourers and pay them any more money just because some hardware was designed in house (hardware design doesn't employ many people either). It's not Tom's shoes. In fact I find it hard to believe that even Tom's shoes are Tom's shoes. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Kzpv47o80eg",1],["aT7mnSstKGs",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1],["db-7J5OaSag",1],["iIpPuJ_r8Xg",1]]},"0vJJlVBVTFg":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a level-headed, practical take on design patterns without Java-style verbosity.","q":{"x":"I found Alex Martinelli's talk on design patterns very level-headed and instructive","i":12851370,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12851370,"author":"omginternets","month":"2016-11","story":"“Design Patterns” Aren't (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Level-headed and instructive talk on design patterns","quote":"I found Alex Martinelli's talk on design patterns very level-headed and instructive","text":"In a similar vein, I found Alex Martinelli's talk on design patterns very level-headed and instructive: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3755321,"author":"irahul","month":"2012-03","story":"Books Programmers Don't Really Read But Recommend","stance":"rec","why":"Practical design patterns talk without Java-style verbosity","quote":"I recommend Alex Martelli's talks","text":"Among the books \"programmers have read\", I have read K&R, Programming Pearls and bits and pieces of Effective Java and C++. I thoroughly enjoyed these books. The other books on the \"have read\" list are on my \"don't bother reading\" list. Code Complete, Pragmatic Programmer, Mythical Man Month et al aren't my kind of books. As far as design patterns go, I believe if you code in Python/Ruby/Perl etc, books covering them in Java/C++ are needlessly verbose and boring. I recommend Alex Martelli's talks [VIDEO] [LINK] And this book which covers design patterns using Ruby. [LINK] Both approaches actually show you practical examples of design patterns in the standard library or real world problems, and shuns patterns which aren't applicable to the language or are too trivial. These talks aren't the end-all, but they will give you a good overall knowledge, and will make further exploration easy. And from the \"haven't read\" list, CLRS is a good book and isn't as dense as it is generally taken to be. It does have sections on proofs, but you can skip it and jump directly to the data structure/algorithm. The pseudo code used in the book is extremely clear, and will convert to simple python in most of the cases. Use it as a reference, or read first few hundred pages. If the book isn't for you, you will know it after you have read the first 100 pages. Another book I recommend is Sedgewick's book on Algorithms. I have read the C version. It mostly covers basic data structures and some accompa"},{"id":3399581,"author":"irahul","month":"2011-12","story":"Design Patterns in Python (Free Book)","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on Python-idiomatic design patterns","quote":"this talk by Alex Martelli is a good starting point","text":"I looked at couple of them. Some of the patterns has a note mentioning it is a port of C#/Java/C++ implementation in the Wikipedia article. Python is drastically different from C#/Java/C++, and a direct port of classical patterns won't make much sense. It will work, but it will the equivalent of Fortran programmers writing Frotran in Python. If anyone is interested, this talk by Alex Martelli is a good starting point: [VIDEO] I think Bruce Eckel was writing a book on \"Design Patterns\" in Python, but that's not completed. However, there is a book using Ruby, which shows the classical approach for implementing design patters, then shows how it is done in Ruby: [LINK]"},{"id":22542400,"author":"andrix","month":"2020-03","story":"Design Patterns in Dynamic Languages (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Remembered talk on Python design patterns at Google","quote":"I still remember the talk from Alex Martinelli at Google","text":"Cool. I'll take a look at the book. I was specifically interested in Python design patterns. I still remember the talk from Alex Martinelli at Google: [VIDEO] , and I just saw there is an updated version: [LINK]"},{"id":4782038,"author":"irahul","month":"2012-11","story":"OOP Isn't a Fundamental Particle of Computing","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in an argument about patterns in dynamic languages","quote":"","text":">> I use lists and strings and arrays with no concern about how many elements they contain or where the memory comes from. > You should worry. If you don't want your server or your app to run slow, you should worry about these things. I am pretty sure he is saying he doesn't have to manually allocate and free memory and worry about off by one errors when iterating. He is comparing equivalents; he isn't saying since I am using Python I can shove 1 billion numbers in a list. > Can you imagine going into a programming interview and saying something like this? The only occasion when programming interviews have any significance is when you are seeking a job. Using them as a benchmark for anything else is flawed. > It's pretty difficult to implement reliable, readable and proven design patterns if you're just passing around dictionaries of dictionaries and lists. Translating proven, classical design patterns to Python or Ruby will result in shitty code. The proven design patterns don't have to be the same for all languages. It's idiomatic to use dictionaries/list in place of classes where applicable. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["Unzc731iCUY",1],["ZhuHCtR3xq8",1]]},"3DtUzH3zoFo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clearest account of the CSP lineage behind Go's goroutines and channels.","q":{"x":"Check out this video by Pike","i":19020539,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19020539,"author":"why-el","month":"2019-01","story":"The Story of Squeak, a Practical Smalltalk Written in Itself (1997)","stance":"rec","why":"Traces concurrency ideas from Squeak to Newsqueak to Go.","quote":"Check out this video by Pike","text":"Squeak was the inspiration for Newsqueak by Rob Pike, co-author of Golang. A lot of the concurrency ideas can be traced to this timeline, some fascinating stuff there. Check out this video by Pike: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":8380879,"author":"bishop_mandible","month":"2014-09","story":"Rob Pike: Reflections on Window Systems (2008) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on CSP-inspired languages from Occam to Go.","quote":"A similarly interesting talk by him on CSP-inspired languages","text":"The Blit video he mentions: [LINK] A similarly interesting talk by him on CSP-inspired languages (Occam, Erlang, Squeak, Newsqueak, Limbo, Alef, Go): [VIDEO] Slides (not shown in the video): [LINK] Interesting as well: From parellel to concurrent (on Sawzall and Go): [LINK]"},{"id":6417758,"author":"canthonytucci","month":"2013-09","story":"Less is exponentially more (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Great hour-long Go talk by Rob Pike.","quote":"He has given some really great Go talks which are available on youtube","text":"For those who don't know who Rob Pike is, he's pretty awesome, helped create UTF-8 among other things, and now works on Go. [LINK] He has given some really great Go talks which are available on youtube, about an hour each: Origins of Go Concurrency Style - [VIDEO] Lexical Scanning in Go - [LINK] Go Concurrency Patterns (One of my favorites) - [LINK]"},{"id":44620995,"author":"Kranar","month":"2025-07","story":"The borrowchecker is what I like the least about Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Pike's answer comparing Go and Erlang at 32 minutes.","quote":"It's at the 32 minute mark","text":"I can't substantiate your claim about Erlang or weird syntax, is that either a proper quote or some kind of paraphrasing because nothing remotely close to it comes up. There are numerous interviews with Rob Pike about the design of Go from when Go was still being developed, and Erlang doesn't come up in anything that I can find other than this interview from 2010 where someone asks Rob Pike a question involving Erlang and Rob replies by saying he thinks the two languages have a different approach to are fairly different: [VIDEO] It's at the 32 minute mark, but once again this is in response to someone asking a question. Here are other interviews about Go, and once again in almost every interview I'd say Rob kind of insinuates he was motivated by a dislike of using C++ within Google to write highly parallel services, but not once is Erlang ever mentioned: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":17617472,"author":"mseepgood","month":"2018-07","story":"Go: building on the shoulders of giants and stepping on a few toes [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on origins of Go concurrency.","quote":"","text":"Intersting talk. Here are some other talks about the history and origins of Go: Evolution of Go (Griesemer, GopherCon 2015) Video: [LINK] Slides: [LINK] Origins of Go Concurrency (Rob Pike, OSCON 2010) Video: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK]"},{"id":5203773,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2013-02","story":"Google Go: The Good, the Bad, and the Meh","stance":"reference","why":"Pike on how Hoare's CSP paper inspired Go.","quote":"Rob Pike saying how mind blowing and inspiring Tony Hoare's CSP paper is","text":"I'm not sure who \"they\" are, but certainly not the core development team, and Samuel Tesla does not represent them. See for example Rob Pike saying how mind blowing and inspiring Tony Hoare's CSP paper is, how everyone should read it and how it inspired Go[1]. Another example is how Go's regexp implementation has a solid mathematical foundation[2]. Now, those are two examples of quite old programming research being used to good effect in Go, so there is an opening for criticism there. But in general I'm pretty sure the core go devs don't have have anything against programming research nor try to create this false dichotomy. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["f6kdp27TYZs",1],["dX649lnKAU0",1],["tfAnxaWiSeE",1]]},"4AfRAVcThyA":{"wy":"Praised as the best talk of CppCon 2017 and the key introduction to metaclasses and generative C++.","q":{"x":"Herb Sutter's talk about it is by far the best talk I've seen from from CppCon17.","i":15548269,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16259949,"author":"xaedes","month":"2018-01","story":"Outperforming LAPACK with C++ metaprogramming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reflection proposal and meta classes in C++.","quote":"The talk about the reflection proposal was amazing. Meta classes are also very cool.","text":"The talk about the reflection proposal was amazing. Meta classes are also very cool. We can only hope it comes fast. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15548269,"author":"slavik81","month":"2017-10","story":"C++17 Is Here: Interview with Herb Sutter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sutter on generative C++ and metaclasses; best CppCon17 talk.","quote":"Herb Sutter's talk about it is by far the best talk I've seen from from CppCon17.","text":"Herb Sutter's talk about it is by far the best talk I've seen from from CppCon17. \"Meta: Thoughts on generative C++\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":25323515,"author":"tylerhou","month":"2020-12","story":"Is the preprocessor still needed in C++? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Herb Sutter proposed Python-like metaclasses in C++ through compile-time code generation.","text":"A few years ago Herb Sutter proposed Python-like metaclasses in C++ through compile-time code generation. Not sure if anything has been proposed for general use. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25323613,"author":"tylerhou","month":"2020-12","story":"Is the preprocessor still needed in C++? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"To a degree, but templates and constexpr don't support a bunch of features like compile-time field enumeration and introspection + code generation. For example, let's say I have a bunch of structs: struct GeoCoordinate { int lat, long; }; struct GeoArea { std::vector<GeoCoordinate> perimeter; }; struct Place { std::string name; std::string contact_number; GeoArea area; }; Now I need to serialize these structs into a format to be sent over the wire. Currently, I have a few choices: 1. Use an off-the-shelf library like protobuf (disclaimer: I work for Google). Then I have to convert my code to a protobuf definition and rely on its code generator to perform [de]serialization. I also have to hope that my library supports all the field definitions I need. 2. Write macros to define each field in each structure. These macros perform some arcane magicks that somehow create the necessary [de]serialization functions. These macros are difficult to write and maintain (or I find a library). 3. Manually define the methods myself. This is tedious, hard to maintain, and error prone. What if I could write some code in C++ which could read the structure and generate the appropriate serialization code? Something like (syntax hypothetical): Serializable(Class) { std::string serialize() { std::string output; for (auto member : Class.members()) { // loop unrolled at compile time if (member.type == int) { output.append(std::format(\"{:10}\"), member.get()) } else if (member.type == std::string) { ..."}],"r":[["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["PorfLSr3DDI",1],["lkgszkPnV8g",1],["fHNmRkzxHWs",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1]]},"9syvZr-9xwk":{"wy":"HN recommends Sipser's free lectures as the companion to the standard theory of computation textbook and a path to understanding how computers fundamentally work.","q":{"x":"Sipser also has corresponding lectures that are available for free on YouTube here","i":40797438,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":44923345,"author":"skydhash","month":"2025-08","story":"Traps to Developers","stance":"rec","why":"One-hour lecture covering finite automata behind regular expressions","quote":"the instructor also explains Finite Automata which is the basic mechanism that does all the stuff","text":"It's half a chapter in most books I know. Or a subset of this 1h MIT videos [0], but the instructor also explains Finite Automata which is the basic mechanism that does all the stuff. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44163397,"author":"eterm","month":"2025-06","story":"The Princeton INTERCAL Compiler's source code","stance":"rec","why":"Course teaches the formal definition of regular languages","quote":"As someone who has only recently learned the formal definition of regular language","text":"> if you require your parens to be balanced, that's no longer a regular language. TIL: [LINK] As someone who has only recently learned the formal definition of regular language ( Thanks to [VIDEO] as mentioned on this board recently ), I'm interested in the formal proof for this? It feels intuitively true, but I haven't finished the course yet and therefore haven't come across it and can't yet reason well about it. Is the problem then in trying to \"encode\" whether brackets are balanced, that we would need infinite \"levels\" of how many more brackets we've opened rather than closed, (i.e a stack) and that violates the finite nature of finite automota? So I've googled it now, and I've found the result is due to the \"Pumping lemma\": [LINK]"},{"id":40797438,"author":"sky2224","month":"2024-06","story":"Recommended Books on Computational Complexity Theory","stance":"rec","why":"Free Sipser lectures matching the standard intro textbook","quote":"Sipser also has corresponding lectures that are available for free on YouTube here","text":"As someone else said already: Theory of Computation by Sipser is the go to intro book for this. I'd recommend really reading the book front to back honestly. It helps to have a complete picture of everything that leads up to getting into complexity theory. Sipser also has corresponding lectures that are available for free on YouTube here: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":40814623,"author":"trod123","month":"2024-06","story":"Making AI better at math tutoring","stance":"rec","why":"MIT course giving real understanding of how computers work","quote":"it provides a true understanding of how computers actually work which is sorely lacking in the youth of today","text":"AI won't ever be able to teach math because AI cannot reason. It handles information very differently than humans do. It is at best a flawed oracle that may lie through omission without you being able to tell. This is supported by the fact that there are hard limits to computation, in terms of both Computability Theory, and Complexity Theory that are often disregarded by the novice and magical thinker alike despite being largely solved fields by experts in Computer Science, at least in terms of Computability Theory. Most of this work was completed in the 1950s. This is not a difficult a subject, but most people online simply are unwilling to do the work to rationally learn this and instead choose to try and hide their own ignorance because they feel inadequate, or perhaps they are engaging in something more malevolent. The reasoning (false justification) doesn't really matter. It is, however; quite telling when expert's have validated these things, and yet any mention contrary to a narrative provokes downvotes in a public forum to remove it from view. A perfect example of intentional actions done by third-parties trying to misinform others about the risks (by those parties actions removing legitimate and valid information). For those with the cajones to actually learn this stuff. Here is a link. It seems very jaron laden but it provides a true understanding of how computers actually work which is sorely lacking in the youth of today. MIT 18.404J Theory of Computation [VIDEO] "},{"id":28823791,"author":"kjhughes","month":"2021-10","story":"MIT 18.404J – Theory of Computation [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Lecturer states course prerequisites at this point","quote":"He addresses prerequisites specifically in the first lecture at this timestamp","text":"He addresses prerequisites specifically in the first lecture at this timestamp: [VIDEO] (You're expected to be comfortable understanding mathematical theorems and producing proofs.)"}],"r":[["9yjZpBq1XBE",1],["YCjNT9qGjh4",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1],["XA2WjJbmmoM",1]]},"Ag1AKIl_2GM":{"wy":"HN uses it constantly as the reference for the argument that either the user controls the software or the software controls the user, and it is recommended as a basic introduction.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend this[1] talk by Richard Stallman, it's pretty basic, but it gets the idea across well.","i":9153418,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":36257152,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2023-06","story":"Linux Is Free If You Don't Value Your Time","stance":"rec","why":"Case for free software giving users control over computing","quote":"Linux, BSDs, and free / open source operating systems give the user freedom and control over their computing experience","text":"Windows is closed source, full of ads and telemetry, and increasingly user hostile. MacOS is a mostly closed source, expensive, user-condescending walled garden. Linux, BSDs, and free / open source operating systems give the user freedom and control over their computing experience: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36390844,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2023-06","story":"We need more of Richard Stallman, not less","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the four freedoms and who controls your computer","quote":"Are you in control of your computer, or does your computer control you?","text":"The four freedoms that free software guarantee are essential for people to be in control of their own computing. Are you in control of your computer, or does your computer control you? [VIDEO] People get angry and frustrated when a big tech company makes a decision that is user-hostile, but benefits the tech company's profits. This is to be expected because in almost all cases, the big tech company has no obligation other than to maximize profits. The four freedoms help to put a check on the power of big tech companies by providing alternatives that keep power in the hands of the user. That does not mean that free software and big tech companies cannot coexist or even thrive together. Red Hat was built on free software. System76 sells hardware that runs mostly free software."},{"id":24855063,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2020-10","story":"Some Thoughts on Windows 10","stance":"rec","why":"Food for thought on who controls your computer","quote":"Food for thought about who controls your computer","text":"Sounds very frustrating. I don't use Windows on any of the computers I own. I prefer to have more control over my computer so I use Linux. I still have to use Windows at work, but I don't admin those machines so I don't have a lot of control / choice in the matter. Food for thought about who controls your computer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9153418,"author":"arfar","month":"2015-03","story":"VMWare Taken to Court Over GPL Violation","stance":"rec","why":"Basic talk conveying the free software idea well","quote":"I'd recommend this[1] talk by Richard Stallman, it's pretty basic, but it gets the idea across well.","text":"You and all of the \"Free Software\" world have different definitions on free. I'd recommend this[1] talk by Richard Stallman, it's pretty basic, but it gets the idea across well. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44923024,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2025-08","story":"Microsoft keeps adding stuff into Windows we don't need","stance":"reference","why":"Features reflect vendor wants, not user wants","quote":"Copilot and Recall are not what most users want, but they are what Microsoft wants","text":">> Microsoft keeps adding stuff into Windows we don't want Of course they are. Copilot and Recall are not what most users want, but they are what Microsoft wants: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39756742,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2024-03","story":"USB hubs, printers, Java, and more seemingly broken by macOS 14.4 update","stance":"reference","why":"Argues software behavior is intentional, not malfunction","quote":"It did exactly what it was programmed to do.","text":">> Your computer didn’t do what you expected it to. Hell, you’re very justified to go beyond that and say that it behaved in a way that it shouldn’t. It did exactly what it was programmed to do. It was not a malfunction, it was intentional: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39194075,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2024-01","story":"The disastrous communication from Apple to apps developers","stance":"reference","why":"Argues Apple controls your phone","quote":"Because Apple controls your phone","text":">> the more relevant point is that it already is my phone. I paid Apple for it. Why can't I install anything I want on it? Because Apple controls your phone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37267516,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2023-08","story":"Windows popup to change competing browser search engine to Bing anticompetitive?","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates software doing what its makers programmed","quote":"It is doing what it was programmed to do","text":"It is doing what it was programmed to do: [VIDEO] Sadly, this kind of user-hostile behavior is increasingly common and a form of enshitification (see [LINK] ) of the operating system."},{"id":36936438,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2023-07","story":"The Right to Lie and Google’s “Web Environment Integrity”","stance":"reference","why":"Whether you control your computer or someone else does","quote":"This fundamentally comes down to \"do you really control your computer, or does someone else?\"","text":"\"If your computer can’t lie to other computers, then it’s not yours.\" This fundamentally comes down to \"do you really control your computer, or does someone else?\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":35243165,"author":"valleyer","month":"2023-03","story":"Richard Stallman's Ted talk in Steve Jobs' style/voice (elevenlabs.io/ChatGPT)","stance":"reference","why":"Original talk behind a Steve Jobs style remake","quote":"Here's the original, I think","text":"This is remarkably good, both the (I assume) human-generated slides and text and the computer-generated voice. Here's the original, I think: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33583186,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2022-11","story":"Tell HN: Siri provides Apple Music link when sharing current song on Spotify","stance":"reference","why":"Argues Siri serves Apple, not the user","quote":"Siri is Apple's tool, not yours despite appearences","text":">> Digital assistants do not exist to serve you or your interests. You should assume that your every interaction with one will be exploited in some way for someone else's benefit, or leaked and/or sold to some third party without your knowledge, or both. Correct. Siri is Apple's tool, not yours despite appearences: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33720492,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2022-11","story":"Mercedes locks faster acceleration behind a $1,200 annual paywall","stance":"reference","why":"Argues you do not own a vehicle if others control its software","quote":"It's not fully \"your vehicle\" if they control the software","text":"It's not fully \"your vehicle\" if they control the software: [VIDEO] You can pay a recurring subscription fee to access features that they own."},{"id":33738413,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2022-11","story":"Intel Officially Introduces Pay-as-You-Go Chip Licensing","stance":"reference","why":"Argues pay-to-unlock chips mean you do not own the processor","quote":"It's not your processor if they control the firmware","text":"\"Intel's upcoming Intel's 4th Generation Xeon Scalable Sapphire Rapids processors are equipped with various special-purpose accelerators and security technologies that all customers do not need at all times. To offer such end-users additional flexibility regarding investments, Intel will deliver them to buy its CPUs with those capabilities disabled but turn them on if they are needed at some point. The Software Defined Silicon (SDSi) technology will also allow Intel to sell fewer CPU models and then enable its clients or partners to activate certain features if needed (to use them on-prem or offer them as a service).\" Apparently Intel believes that subscription-driven microprocessors will be a great revenue stream. It's not your processor if they control the firmware: [VIDEO] You can pay a subscription fee to access features that they own. I will not be buying any new Intel processors."},{"id":30389841,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2022-02","story":"Outlook just asked me if I want to upgrade to bigger ads","stance":"reference","why":"Stallman quote on user control","quote":"Either the user controls the software, or the software controls the users\"","text":"Microsoft is showing that they are really the ones who are in control. \"Either the user controls the software, or the software controls the users\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":29581870,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-12","story":"Windows 11 Officially Shuts Down Firefox’s Default Browser Workaround","stance":"reference","why":"Stallman quote on user control","quote":"Either the user controls the software, or the software controls the users\"","text":"Microsoft is showing that they are really the ones who are in control. \"Either the user controls the software, or the software controls the users\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":28896926,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-10","story":"Netflix's new player breaks the ability to modify the seeking of a playing video","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Stallman quote about software controlling users","quote":"Either the user controls the software, or the software controls the users\"","text":"\"Who controls your computer? Is it you?\" \"Either the user controls the software, or the software controls the users\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":27663969,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Could Microsoft restrict access to file backups via Microsoft Account?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why free software exists","quote":"Possible issues like these are one reason why free and open source software exist","text":">> if Microsoft were to ban my account [1], could they then stop me from opening my backup files on a new machine, even if I made a new account? If your documents are tagged with the banned account info, it certainly seems possible. It seems like a really dirty thing to do, but there are lots of HN users who are against the Microsoft account requirement for Windows 11: [LINK] Possible issues like these are one reason why free and open source software exist: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25845319,"author":"augustk","month":"2021-01","story":"Can't uninstall Adobe apps without an account (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Stallman quote on user control","quote":"either the user controls the program or the program controls the user.","text":"As Richard Stallman puts it \"either the user controls the program or the program controls the user.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":25321639,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2020-12","story":"Macs with T2, M1 chips cannot be restored to factory state without Apple","stance":"reference","why":"Stallman on who controls your computer","quote":"Who really controls your computer","text":"Apple may sell you a computer, but ultimately you do not control it. Who really controls your computer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8711039,"author":"rian","month":"2014-12","story":"The JavaScript Trap","stance":"reference","why":"Stallman on open source versus free software distinction","quote":"Stallman's own words on this distinction in this video","text":"\"Open Source\" terminology emphasizes its technical superiority as a development method. \"Free Software\" terminology emphasizes its ethical necessity to maintain a free society. One is technical, one is political/ideological. Open source advocates don't care about politics, Free software advocates think technical superiority is unimportant in a non-free society. Stallman's own words on this distinction in this video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1]]},"AyORieDhpkg":{"wy":"HN commenters suggest it as atmospheric, near-ambient music for programming and focus.","q":{"x":"still in the spirit of heavy, nearly ambient","i":31194595,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31194595,"author":"maicro","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Heavy Metal Music for Programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Heavy, nearly ambient jazz suggested for programming.","quote":"still in the spirit of heavy, nearly ambient","text":"Another second here. There's a YouTube channel with full stoner metal albums (Stoned Meadow of Doom: [LINK] ; been around for quite a while, though I have a vague fear of them disappearing due to the obvious copyright infringement...). As for specific bands (though I don't think on the stoner metal spectrum, but ones I love): Baroness, Sub Rosa, King Woman, Bell Witch, Mastodon. And for something a bit farther off from metal, but still in the spirit of heavy, nearly ambient - The Kilimanjaro Darkjazz Ensemble ( [VIDEO] ) / The Mount Fuji Doomjazz Corporation (which is apparently the live version of \"The Kilimanjaro...\")."},{"id":14629874,"author":"badosu","month":"2017-06","story":"The slow death of the electric guitar","stance":"rec","why":"Dark electronic jazz in an eclectic music selection.","quote":"dark electronic jazz","text":"Made a selection with an eclectic variety of modern electronic sounds, enjoy! - [LINK] (a gem of prog trance) - [VIDEO] (dark electronic jazz) - [LINK] (black metal + breakbeat + classical) - [LINK] (amazing psy chill album) - [LINK] To illustrate how Electronic Music enables you to achieve a wide range of possibilities I've chosen these 2 songs from the same band: - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":11151799,"author":"ulrikrasmussen","month":"2016-02","story":"AI generated music to improve focus, relaxation and sleep","stance":"rec","why":"Album suggested among ambient and soundscape recommendations.","quote":"The Kilimanjaro Darkjazz Ensemble - Here Be Dragons","text":"I'm glad you like it :). I think ambience and soundscapes should still have interesting musical elements. If it only works as background noise, then I get bored by it. I've also listened to Isis quite a lot, mostly \"Oceanic\", which is great. There is a pretty good remix album of \"Oceanic\" that opens with a remix by Fennesz. Let me just throw a few more out there: Antenne (two albums, just called #1 and #2): [LINK] Jóhann Jóhannsson - Englabörn: [LINK] The Kilimanjaro Darkjazz Ensemble - Here Be Dragons: [VIDEO] Kreng - Grimoire: [LINK] La Grande Bellezza - Original Sound Track (sans disco tracks): [LINK]"}],"r":[["s-CTkbHnpNQ",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["PbmJkMhmrVI",1]]},"K_nnUbX7uuQ":{"wy":"Recommended as a rare look at the unmarked buildings and infrastructure where undersea cables come ashore.","q":{"x":"Documentary about the cable landing stations: The Secrets of Cornwall","i":28819607,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":33119468,"author":"132RRN96","month":"2022-10","story":"Interactive Submarine Cable Map","stance":"rec","why":"Local tracks down cable landing sites around Cornwall","quote":"there is a youtube video[1] of a local tracking these down around the Cornwall area.","text":"A lot of the time these landing sites / buildings are a bit further inland. At the beach there is usually only a buried armoured cable leading to a concrete slab or something where it connects to underground ductwork. If you're interested in that and have an hour of time there is a youtube video[1] of a local tracking these down around the Cornwall area. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28819607,"author":"b0tzzzzzzman","month":"2021-10","story":"Incenser, or how NSA and GCHQ are tapping internet cables (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary about cable landing stations","quote":"Documentary about the cable landing stations: The Secrets of Cornwall","text":"Great write up. I would recommend the checking out: Documentary about the cable landing stations: The Secrets of Cornwall [VIDEO]"},{"id":24119873,"author":"the-dude","month":"2020-08","story":"Secret Landscape: America's cold war infrastructure","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on unmarked buildings housing cable landing equipment in Cornwall","quote":"All kinds of bland looking, unmarked buildings housing telecom equipment for the onshoring fibres.","text":"Reminds me of 'The Secrets Of Cornwall - Part 1 - Communications' : [VIDEO] All kinds of bland looking, unmarked buildings housing telecom equipment for the onshoring fibres."}],"r":[["tiJVJ5QRRUE",1],["0sHCQWjTrJ8",1],["la-sGpTpkxE",1]]},"OINAk2xl8Bc":{"wy":"Recommended as a sourced, nuanced corrective whenever the Japanese justice system's conviction rate is raised.","q":{"x":"I've seen this Japanese person explaining the situation informally but thoroughly.","i":22346592,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23253370,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-05","story":"U.S. arrests former Green Beret, son, in connection with Ghosn escape from Japan","stance":"rec","why":"Nuanced view of Japanese justice system","quote":"You might want to hear what Yuta has to say about this[1].","text":"You might want to hear what Yuta has to say about this[1]. It's not as black and white, basically. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22729638,"author":"jdtang13","month":"2020-03","story":"Problems with Japan's Covid-19 reports","stance":"rec","why":"Referenced explanation of the 99% conviction figure","quote":"Here is a video, with references, from a relatively trustworthy source","text":"This 99% number does not mean what people think it means. The Japanese prosecution system is a pipeline that strongly filters out unlikely-to-be-convicted cases from the very beginning. Long before you are convicted, you might not even be arrested, or your case gets dismissed earlier in the pipeline. Here is a video, with references, from a relatively trustworthy source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22346592,"author":"diegoperini","month":"2020-02","story":"Japan’s Lost-and-Found System","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough explanation of 99% conviction rate context","quote":"I've seen this Japanese person explaining the situation informally but thoroughly.","text":"I'm not Japanese, but I've seen this Japanese person explaining the situation informally but thoroughly. [VIDEO] The premise is, yes 99% percent is a measured rate but also has no utility in a discussion if the other parts of the big picture is ignored."},{"id":22346214,"author":"jbay808","month":"2020-02","story":"Japan’s Lost-and-Found System","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the high conviction rate","quote":"The high conviction rate seems to be because cases are dropped unless the prosecution's confidence is very high","text":"The high conviction rate seems to be because cases are dropped unless the prosecution's confidence is very high, rather than letting it go to trial. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["24OXzIRIiMQ",1],["iY57ErBkFFE",1],["p_iZr2-Coqc",1]]},"VYhAGnsnO7w":{"wy":"Cited as the go-to demo that full microcontrollers now cost about three cents, replacing things like the 555 timer.","q":{"x":"Dave Jones' EEVBlog recently posted a video on cheap microcontrollers (<0.03USD)","i":18198584,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":31312021,"author":"michaelt","month":"2022-05","story":"Everything with a battery should have an off switch","stance":"rec","why":"About a roughly 3 cent microcontroller, one-time-programmable and basic.","quote":"You might enjoy [1] about a microcontroller that's ~3 cents, in quantity 10.","text":"> Yeah I wish we could get those prices. You might enjoy [1] about a microcontroller that's ~3 cents, in quantity 10. Of course it's only one-time-programmable, and generally pretty basic. And that's a pre-supply-chain-crisis price - these days prices have risen to 9 cents [2]. Still very cheap though! [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27563423,"author":"cartoonworld","month":"2021-06","story":"U.S. senators propose 25% tax credit for semiconductor manufacturing","stance":"rec","why":"Covers dirt cheap Chinese microcontrollers.","quote":"You can also check out Dave from EEVblog's 3-cent microcontroller[0] video about the same series of dirt cheap micros.","text":">The countries that are going to be hurt by these US semiconductor subsidies are going be Taiwan, South Korea, Japan ... not China. I think I may agree here, and it gets complicated -- fast. I suspect you might be referring to larger parts like CPUs, but you should take this excellent opportunity to investigate china's semi industry a bit. China has a very interesting local manufacturing economy of parts and suppliers that is mostly invisible to outside observers. It's fascinating that in China, there is a totally divergent supply chain to support the cheap consumer electronics industry. Granted, it isn't CPUs. However, they sure are cranking out semis, its just that they never leave China but for inside of another product. The datasheets are all in Chinese, they aren't exported, they serve to support China's unique manufacturing culture. Here's a previous submission that delves into this: The “terrible” 3 cent MCU – a short survey of sub $0.10 microcontrollers [LINK] You can also check out Dave from EEVblog's 3-cent microcontroller[0] video about the same series of dirt cheap micros. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18198584,"author":"mrob","month":"2018-10","story":"The 555 and How It Got That Way","stance":"rec","why":"Cheap microcontrollers under three cents as 555 timer replacements.","quote":"Dave Jones' EEVBlog recently posted a video on cheap microcontrollers (<0.03USD)","text":"Dave Jones' EEVBlog recently posted a video on cheap microcontrollers (<0.03USD): [VIDEO] Replacing a 555 timer is a very common job for this type of part. It will typically have an internal oscillator, so it won't need the additional components the 555 needs, reducing cost further. Even if you use something more expensive like an ATtiny (cut down version of the microcontroller found in Arduinos, so very easy to develop for) it might still be competitive because of the reduced parts count and board space."},{"id":33847616,"author":"ajsnigrutin","month":"2022-12","story":"Complete working transistor-scale replica of the classic MOS6502 microprocessor","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence microcontrollers cost about 3 cents.","quote":"you can get whole microcontrollers for ~3 cents a piece [0]","text":"Most of the components are leds and transistors (and some resistors) and those are (still) dirt cheap... you can get whole microcontrollers for ~3 cents a piece [0] (if you buy a few thousands). PCBs are comparatively cheap, few tens of dollars, depending on the process, and with pick-and-place, assembly is also relatively cheap. so yeah.. maybe they're making them in some more expensive country or use some exotic components. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27135621,"author":"yitchelle","month":"2021-05","story":"Reverse Engineering an Unknown Microcontroller","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what is possible with a 3 cent part.","quote":"EEVblog has a video of what could be possible. He is talking about 3c part.","text":"EEVblog has a video of what could be possible. He is talking about 3c part. With direct negotiation at massive volumes, sub 1c part is definitely possible. [VIDEO] What has your experience been with the very cheap parts?"}],"r":[["MtxA20Q-Uss",1],["wcBEOcPtlYk",1],["KEoGrbKAyKE",1],["oYp5XuGYqqY",1],["PGv5BqNL164",1],["TMbl_ofF3AM",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["S4O5voOCqAQ",1]]},"cQq2pKNDgIs":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it for Hanson's grabby aliens reasoning as an unusual framework for thinking about UFO behavior and intelligent life in the cosmos.","q":{"x":"Grabby Aliens concept by Robin Hanson is an interesting new take on UFOs.","i":37535484,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38424286,"author":"ricksunny","month":"2023-11","story":"The most powerful cosmic ray since the Oh-My-God particle puzzles scientists","stance":"rec","why":"Hanson on long-lived stars raising odds of intelligent life.","quote":"significantly updated my priors on the likelihood of intelligent, non-human life roaming about the cosmos","text":"To the casual HN reader - my recommendation to you is to neithet laugh at nor dismiss out of hand analyses such as this (even as logic intrinsic to them merits as much picking apart as any other analysis one sees everyday on HN). Robin Hanson's description of long-lived stars (usually strongest in infrared - i.e. the 3 stars our eyes lack the spectral response to see for every 1 star that we do see when looking into the night sky) significantly updated my priors on the likelihood of intelligent, non-human life roaming about the cosmos. [VIDEO] (timestamped) tl;dr: Red-dwarf stars have been around -- with all that goes with that.. -- for much, much longer than our sun."},{"id":37535484,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-09","story":"NASA clears the air: No evidence that UFOs are aliens","stance":"rec","why":"Hanson's grabby aliens reasoning about UFO behavior.","quote":"Grabby Aliens concept by Robin Hanson is an interesting new take on UFOs.","text":"Here are some boring and obvious thought ending dismissals. Grabby Aliens concept by Robin Hanson is an interesting new take on UFOs. Start with “if they are UFOs, what could we reason about them from their behaviour?”. See [VIDEO] “It might be unlikely but it’s not crazy unlikely”."},{"id":36687478,"author":"Simon_O_Rourke","month":"2023-07","story":"Laniakea Supercluster","stance":"rec","why":"Robin Hanson's model of why visitors might appear only fleetingly.","quote":"Take a few minutes to listen to Prof. Robin Hanson talk on this very thing.","text":"> Every human has a camera 24/7 now and no one has documented visitors. Take a few minutes to listen to Prof. Robin Hanson talk on this very thing. [VIDEO] The jist of what he says, is that there's very much weird stuff seen in the sky, like the McMinnville photos [1], but there's nobody as yet landing a craft on the White House lawn and posing for the cameras. He puts forward a model for this kind of scenario, it's worth a listen, that any visitors would quite rightly be far in advance of the societies we currently live in, and may only show themselves fleetingly so we gain an acceptance of their presence. [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1]]},"gQnmP7UD_zk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a highly informative look at micro-etching glass and the smallest Tesla valves.","q":{"x":"The video on micro etching glass with HF is highly informative on this post...","i":39800716,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39800716,"author":"samstave","month":"2024-03","story":"High-speed microscale 3D printing","stance":"rec","why":"Informative on micro etching glass with HF","quote":"The video on micro etching glass with HF is highly informative on this post...","text":"[LINK] Seems like it would work beautifully. What would be cool is to make a logic-gate-tesla-valve-micro-hydraulic computer? -- The video on micro etching glass with HF is highly informative on this post... he talks about etching tesla valves at the end... [LINK] <-- 30 microns long with channel width of 4 microns via etched glass... [LINK] [VIDEO] -- So I wonder what resolution would be possible via the 3d version. What would be really interesting to see with this method would be to make a blend of a spiral-screw with the tesla valves to climb or control vertical rates of flow. --- Since this process can print in ceramics, I wonder if the can effectively make a \"super-conductive-nano-wire with spiraling interconnected bucky-ball-shaped lattice of particles\""},{"id":32575748,"author":"thrown_22","month":"2022-08","story":"Perfluorocubane is (as you would expect) weird","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"on the bright side I've found something I have not been desensitized to no matter how many time I've watched it.","text":"I read that, then I saw this guys youtube channel: [VIDEO] on the bright side I've found something I have not been desensitized to no matter how many time I've watched it."},{"id":28157494,"author":"osamagirl69","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla valve","stance":"rec","why":"Attempt at a microscopic Tesla valve in etched glass","quote":"There is an youtube video[1] by the excellent channel Huygens Optics","text":"There is an youtube video[1] by the excellent channel Huygens Optics which goes through trying to make a microscopic tesla valve. The valve does not work as a checkvalve (it is sufficiently small that surface tension and all that work differently than in a macroscopic system), but the author argues that it did satisfy the real goal of being a tesla valve -- which is to look sexy and high tech. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28158365,"author":"showerst","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla valve","stance":"rec","why":"Huygens Optics Tesla valve video","quote":"Also seconding the Huygen's optics video","text":"For a fun visual demonstration, check out nighthawkinlight's video where he runs a flamefront through one -- [LINK] Also seconding the Huygen's optics video osamagirl69 already posted below -- [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XI5frPV58tY",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["VI_riscmviI",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["tcV1EYSUQME",1],["TSZMtSPX3iE",1]]},"ihkRwArnc1k":{"wy":"Recommended in documentary and semiconductor-history threads as a detailed look at Japan's chip industry.","q":{"x":"There is a four-part documentary I saw on YouTube that went into much more (fascinating) detail.","i":32174881,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32174881,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2022-07","story":"The Rise and Peak of Japanese Semiconductors","stance":"rec","why":"Four-part documentary with much more fascinating detail.","quote":"There is a four-part documentary I saw on YouTube that went into much more (fascinating) detail.","text":"There is a four-part documentary I saw on YouTube that went into much more (fascinating) detail. Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK] Part 3: [LINK] Part 4: [LINK]"},{"id":23506739,"author":"rmrfstar","month":"2020-06","story":"Officer of China’s People’s Liberation Army Arrested at LAX","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent NHK documentary on the Japanese semiconductor industry.","quote":"Japanese semiconductor industry, for which there is an excellent NHK documentary","text":"This is bad read on the history of innovation. I offer two counter-examples, though there are many more: (1) Japanese semiconductor industry, for which there is an excellent NHK documentary [1]. (2) After WW2, the British invited Soviet engineers to tour jet engine factories as a token of good faith. The engineers documented the factory layout, and wore adhesive on their shoes to gather metal filings for later analysis. A few years later, Mig-15's were shooting American planes out of the sky over Korea. A similar example: the British transferred huge bodies of knowledge to US aircraft engine manufacturers during WW2. This laid the foundation for the industry's future success. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18088104,"author":"newman8r","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Four-part documentary on the history of Japan's electronics industry.","quote":"I wouldn't call it the best documentary, but I think people here will like it","text":"I wouldn't call it the best documentary, but I think people here will like it, it's a 4 part documentary: \"A Video History of Japan's Electronic Industry\" I. Birth of the transformer: [VIDEO] II. Circuits in stone: [LINK] III. Calculator wars: [LINK] IV. Tech Giant: [LINK]"}],"r":[["JQymn5flcek",1],["VsHc91IhzdI",1]]},"vs41JrnGaxc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible way to understand why people across the political spectrum disagree and to better understand opposing views.","q":{"x":"Jonathon Haidt has actually done some great scientific work on the real differences between liberals and conservatives.","i":18248181,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18248181,"author":"jerf","month":"2018-10","story":"Ways people trying to do good accidentally do harm instead and how to avoid them","stance":"rec","why":"Short version of Haidt's science on liberal-conservative differences","quote":"Jonathon Haidt has actually done some great scientific work on the real differences between liberals and conservatives.","text":"Jonathon Haidt has actually done some great scientific work on the real differences between liberals and conservatives. This is a shortish version: [VIDEO] There's also some more detailed and longer versions if you poke around on YouTube a bit, plus a book you can buy if you're really interested. I don't think your characterization of either side is particularly accurate. Neither side is utilitarian, and both sides are plenty deontological, just with different rule sets. Both sides are rationalizing deeply-held instinctual beliefs, or the lack thereof."},{"id":16638971,"author":"mistermann","month":"2018-03","story":"Facebook Sued by Investors Over Voter-Profile Harvesting","stance":"rec","why":"Explains public behavior through moral roots of political divide","quote":"Jonathan Haidt has studied this psychological phenomenon quite extensively","text":"Fair enough, but it wouldn't have made any difference. Disagreement with this particular aspect of the local culture will get you a downvote, not a discussion. Any discussion, if you're lucky to get one, would be better described as a re-education lecture, to teach you the \"facts\" of \"how things are\". Jonathan Haidt has studied this psychological phenomenon quite extensively and in my opinion it does a good job of explaining the recent unusual behavior of the public, it's a shame no one's interested. The moral roots of liberals and conservatives [VIDEO]"},{"id":12703523,"author":"carsongross","month":"2016-10","story":"Whether You’re a Democrat or Republican, Your Social Media Is an Echo Chamber","stance":"rec","why":"Haidt's work on liberal-conservative divide","quote":"Jonathan Haidt has done a lot of work on this problem","text":"Jonathan Haidt has done a lot of work on this problem: [VIDEO] I would like to see peaceful political secessionism happen, so that as we sort ourselves out ideologically we can also sort ourselves out physically and sovereign-ly."},{"id":15174976,"author":"tchaffee","month":"2017-09","story":"At liberal tech companies, those who disagree on politics say they’re isolated","stance":"reference","why":"Haidt's moral foundations theory helped understand conservatives","quote":"I was able to better understand where conservatives are coming from, and even became a lot better at persuading.","text":"I think employees could use some training on how liberals and conservatives think very differently. When I became familiar with Jonathan Haidt and his moral foundations theory, I was able to better understand where conservatives are coming from, and even became a lot better at persuading. For example, when talking about confederate statues coming down, a conservative will respond to arguments around why it's traditional and patriotic to remove those statues (along with why it's fair) whereas a liberal will concentrate only on why it's fair. Make an argument that focuses only on the fairness aspect and the conservative will remain unconvinced and the liberal will think the conservative is a horrible person, because the liberal heavily values fairness. Liberals are most sensitive to the Care and Fairness moral foundations and conservatives are equally sensitive to all the first five foundations. I think the sixth foundation was added for libertarians. - Care: cherishing and protecting others; opposite of harm. - Fairness or proportionality: rendering justice according to shared rules; opposite of cheating. - Loyalty or ingroup: standing with your group, family, nation; opposite of betrayal. - Authority or respect: submitting to tradition and legitimate authority; opposite of subversion. - Sanctity or purity: abhorrence for disgusting things, foods, actions; opposite of degradation. A sixth foundation, Liberty or oppression, was theorized by Jonathan Haidt in The Righteous Mind "}],"r":[["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"Cfe5UQ-1L9Q":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a patient, full-length walkthrough of how a raymarched shader is built from scratch.","q":{"x":"is fantastic, yes it's five hours but worth it.","i":31638811,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31638811,"author":"jwatzman","month":"2022-06","story":"Source code for 4kb demoscene production “Elevated” released (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Five-hour live coding of a 3D shader","quote":"is fantastic, yes it's five hours but worth it.","text":"Indeed his videos are amazing . My recommendation though would be to start with the \"beginner\" video -- which is completely 2D but at least gets some ideas across. Then watch [LINK] which isn't his but introduces how you get 3D out of fragment shaders. Finally just drink from the firehose -- [VIDEO] is fantastic, yes it's five hours but worth it. (Definitely come into that familiar with the idea of raymarching though... the first few minutes start off super basic but it goes to eleven real quick.)"},{"id":24846178,"author":"trollied","month":"2020-10","story":"Raymarching with Fennel and LÖVE","stance":"rec","why":"Six-hour step-by-step creation of a raymarched shader","quote":"And the video that goes through its creation step by step","text":"If you have 6 hours to kill, check this shadertoy that uses ray marching: [LINK] And the video that goes through its creation step by step: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25707909,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-01","story":"Raymarching Distance Fields (2008-2020 compilation)","stance":"reference","why":"Making-of and math explanations for Happy Jumping shader","quote":"Making-of and related math/shader/art explanations (6 hours","text":"Inigo Quilez's procedural raymarching masterpiece, \"Surfer Boy\": [LINK] // Created by inigo quilez - iq/2018 // License Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported License. // A surfer boy in the beach, disney/pixar style. I had to be very careful // with the complexity, otherwise the browser would crash during shader // compilation. I also had to split the boy in two layers because of that, // see below (the single pass version is smaller of course but takes 3 extra // seconds to compile, and renders a bit more slowly) // // This is totally painted to camera, it won't work from others perspectives. // // Common - contains some basic stuff // Buffer A - renders the brackground - not super polished really // Buffer B - blurs the background // Buffer C - paints the boy, except the hand and the board // Image - paints the hand and the board, and does some minimal postpro // // If you have a slow PC, you can watch it here: [LINK] And Happy Jumping: [LINK] // Created by inigo quilez - iq/2019 // License Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported License. // // // An animation test - a happy and blobby creature jumping and // looking around. It gets off-model very often, but it looks // good enough I think. // // Making-of and related math/shader/art explanations (6 hours // long): [VIDEO] // // Video capture: [LINK] // // You can buy a metal print of this shader here: [LINK]"}],"r":[["wupToqz1e2g",1],["5eo8nz_niiM",1]]},"bB340S0tGf8":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny parody that resembles agile as practiced at large companies.","q":{"x":"A funny enough YouTube video (comedy)","i":32504973,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36724347,"author":"EarthIsHome","month":"2023-07","story":"Interview with Senior Rust Developer in 2023 (Parody) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Same creator's agile coach parody.","quote":"parody video is one of the best","text":"Their \"Interview with an Agile Coach\" [0] parody video is one of the best I've seen. Take a look if you enjoyed this one! [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32504973,"author":"shrubble","month":"2022-08","story":"Agile projects have become waterfall projects with sprints","stance":"rec","why":"Comedy parody resembling agile at large companies.","quote":"A funny enough YouTube video (comedy)","text":"A funny enough YouTube video (comedy) and really, it kind of reminds me of the agile methodology in the large company I worked at ... [VIDEO]"},{"id":38602970,"author":"DyslexicAtheist","month":"2023-12","story":"Ego killed the empowered product team","stance":"reference","why":"Satire of agile coaches preaching to the choir.","quote":"","text":"all the digital and agile transformations I've seen had 1 thing in common: they missed to get buy in from middle management. Instead there are agile coaches preaching[1] to the choir while the rest of the org is betting against their success. [1] Art imitating life in that regard: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["faHQA_0d9Mo",1]]},"rwhUpu9MfZ0":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny but serious critique of the show's portrayal of authority and policing.","q":{"x":"It’s a superb video essay and absolutely worth the watch!","i":34162765,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34162765,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2022-12","story":"They Called 911 for Help. Police Used Junk Science to Decide They Were Liars","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Comedic yet serious video essay on Paw Patrol's problematic portrayals.","quote":"It’s a superb video essay and absolutely worth the watch!","text":"I think you will enjoy this video breakdown from Skip Intro: [VIDEO] It’s a superb video essay and absolutely worth the watch! But to be clear, it’s also playing into the meme that paw patrol is copaganda, so take it as a mix of comedy and serious analysis :) The gist of the video is that, while paw patrol is not copaganda, it is problematic for parallel reasons. It’s also a terrible tv show as you noted."},{"id":37887272,"author":"Quinzel","month":"2023-10","story":"The fascist state of Paw Patrol (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Emphasizes the issues with Paw Patrol well.","quote":"this YouTube video really emphasises the issues quite well","text":"Paw Patrol is so shit, I had to study it in my undergrad forensic psychology course: [LINK] And if you can’t be bothered reading an academic journal about it, this YouTube video really emphasises the issues quite well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40886508,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2024-07","story":"Map of Pontypandy in Fireman Sam","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of what vision of policing PAW Patrol suggests","quote":"PAW Patrol is a TV show for kids. It's very popular, so we might as well ask","text":"Related: PAW Patrol's Dark Secret, Explained - Skip Intro > PAW Patrol is a TV show for kids. It's very popular, so we might as well ask: \"What vision of policing does it suggest?\" PAW Patrol is also a very bad TV show. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1]]},"yYTSdlOdkn0":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a well-researched overview of decaf processes and quality, and cite it for the claim that decaf stales faster.","q":{"x":"his video about decaf sold me on the idea","i":46176478,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46176478,"author":"erikrothoff","month":"2025-12","story":"Adenosine on the common path of rapid antidepressant action: The coffee paradox","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced commenter that decaf drinkers love coffee for taste","quote":"his video about decaf sold me on the idea","text":"I went to the US recently and was fully prepared to drink caffeine on the trip because of all the cool coffee shops and roasters. But I was amazed that decaf was basically a first-class citizen there. The hotel breakfast had one giant brewer for regular and the same giant brewer for decaf. It was amazing. And it’s pretty important to realize that well-made decaf doesn’t have to taste worse than regular coffee. James Hoffmanns decaf project proved this for me, and his video about decaf sold me on the idea: decaf drinkers are the OG coffee drinker, drinking it purely for the taste, even without the drug-induced high that caffeine gives you. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39430547,"author":"gruez","month":"2024-02","story":"The case against caffeine","stance":"rec","why":"Hoffmann explains decaf processes and why decaf stales faster","quote":"James Hoffmann did a video on decaff coffee recently[1].","text":"James Hoffmann did a video on decaff coffee recently[1]. In it he basically says the \"scary chemicals\" aspect of other caffeine removal processes is basically overblown, and the reason why decaf coffee sucks has less to do with the solvents used and more to do with how the decaffination process makes the beans more prone to staling. In short, there's nothing really special about the swiss water process in particular and there are more important factors to worry about. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39431916,"author":"rubslopes","month":"2024-02","story":"The case against caffeine","stance":"rec","why":"Hoffmann on decaf quality and roasting","quote":"James Hoffmann made a great video about this recently","text":"> Decaf can taste very good. I've certainly had decaf that was better than most caffeinated coffee I've had. But it's harder to find serious attempts at decaf roasts, sadly. James Hoffmann made a great video about this recently: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40839681,"author":"gruez","month":"2024-06","story":"Coffee helped the Union in the Civil War","stance":"reference","why":"Source for decaf staling claim","quote":"source for both claims","text":"That's one way of making it, but it's by no means the only way. Also, the reason why it tastes bad isn't because it's \"same chemicals used to do dry cleaning\", it's that the decaffination process changes the physical structure of the beans, making it easier to stale. source for both claims: [VIDEO] , [LINK]"}]},"5eqRuVp65eY":{"wy":"Recommended as a well-made, digestible introduction to scaling laws and why models cluster along a similar efficiency frontier.","q":{"x":"see eg for a well made/easily digestable intro","i":41844225,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":44765223,"author":"mdaniel","month":"2025-08","story":"Break the quadratic wall of Transformer attention: WERSA, paper+code open source","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting video on AI hitting a scaling limit.","quote":"it's title reminded me of an interesting video I saw recently","text":"I'm not deep enough in the ecosystem to ingest that paper but it's title reminded me of an interesting video I saw recently: \"AI can't cross this line and we don't know why\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":41844225,"author":"kla-s","month":"2024-10","story":"Zamba2-7B","stance":"rec","why":"Well-made, accessible introduction to scaling laws.","quote":"see eg for a well made/easily digestable intro","text":"Please someone correct me if I’m wrong, but my understanding of ML/LLMs is that this kind of hand crafting has been tried, but it is easier to train/less finicky to let behavior like this emerge from more data, see [1] “Bitter Lesson” and [2] “Scaling Laws”. MAMBA as an architecture claims to have some significant gains performance wise, but to my knowledge there haven't been any really large models (>~100B params) with open weights/leaked MAMBA architecture been disclosed other than this (7B). As mentioned by other comments, another dimension not to forget is the training data quality. Not only quantity but also quality really matters, is what we are learning more and more with LLMs.. [1] [LINK] [2] see eg [VIDEO] for a well made/easily digestable intro"},{"id":45105790,"author":"general1465","month":"2025-09","story":"Anthropic raises $13B Series F","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the efficient compute frontier limitation.","quote":"There is a 1 year old video on YouTube, which describes this limitation","text":"Yep we do. There is a 1 year old video on YouTube, which describes this limitation [VIDEO] Called efficient compute frontier"},{"id":44829191,"author":"general1726","month":"2025-08","story":"GPT-5","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the compute efficient frontier that models cluster toward.","quote":"all models will eventually cluster to similar CEF as was described in this video 10 months ago","text":"Because they are hitting Compute Efficient Frontier. Models can't be much bigger, there is no more original data on the internet, so all models will eventually cluster to similar CEF as was described in this video 10 months ago [VIDEO]"},{"id":42981356,"author":"gsf_emergency_2","month":"2025-02","story":"Value-Based Deep RL Scales Predictably","stance":"reference","why":"Offers a different perspective on error versus compute.","quote":"For a different perspective, error vs compute, see and comments","text":"My attempt at a summary: authors characterize the data-compute pareto front (aka how bitter is the lesson, exactly?) For a different perspective, error vs compute, see [VIDEO] and comments (I particularly liked the one about string theorists rediscovering a fundamental theorem in GR decades too late-- rediscovering how to integrate happens in every field, it's nothing to be ashamed of :)"}],"r":[["xoxhDk-hwuo",1],["LIIBRr31DIU",1]]},"5tu32CCA_Ig":{"wy":"Frequently linked as a compact summary of the Princeton study when commenters argue Congress ignores public opinion.","q":{"x":"It's pretty compelling and well made.","i":14113618,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26282370,"author":"auslegung","month":"2021-02","story":"How prepared are we for the next giant solar flare?","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended as a discouraging-then-encouraging pair of videos","quote":"I think this video will encourage you.","text":"I think this video will encourage you. Well actually maybe it will discourage you, but the follow up video will encourage you :D Corruption is Legal in America: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14113618,"author":"rudimental","month":"2017-04","story":"Richard Posner: “The Real Corruption Is the Ownership of Congress by the Rich”","stance":"rec","why":"Well-made explainer on how money influences legislation","quote":"It's pretty compelling and well made.","text":"Today somebody on Reddit linked to this video which talks about the process of money influencing politics [VIDEO] It doesn't discuss the role of the judicial system. It's all about the legislative aspects of US government. It's pretty compelling and well made. Hopefully a bipartisan movement can have some impact. The video was made by [LINK]"},{"id":33964251,"author":"NaturalPhallacy","month":"2022-12","story":"SBF Arrested by Bahamian Authorities","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the claim Congress doesn't care what you think","quote":"","text":"But would she have been if he didn't? You don't have to like him to acknowledge that the world's richest man with more followers than Joe Rogan has listeners (the most popular podcast period) has power. It's not inconceivable. When presidents do this they call it the \"bully pulpit\" because he uses his reach to the people to have them put pressure on their reps, which I wish we saw more of, because \"Congress doesn't care what you think\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":31744662,"author":"ihumanable","month":"2022-06","story":"Coinbase lays off around 1,100 employees","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes Princeton study on who shapes policy","quote":"Here's a nice video on the topic that summarizes a Princeton study","text":"Power and office are two different things. Someone can exercise a great deal of power in advancing or killing legislation while never holding office and allowing all the office holders to call them names on TV. What matters is what policies are enacted. Here's a nice video on the topic that summarizes a Princeton study [VIDEO]"},{"id":29680617,"author":"cupcake-unicorn","month":"2021-12","story":"Be curious, not judgmental","stance":"reference","why":"Background on laws failing to align corporations with the public","quote":"","text":"It's a tough issue, for sure, because the playing field is just so unequal. To even be able to approach these issues in a more equal way you'd probably have to start a lawsuit and go through a process of discovery because the corporation is bound by all these bizarre legal, financial, and political motives that don't apply when people are interacting with each other. This leads to corporations responding to such requests in a way that's inherently against the interests of the public answering these questions. Without a system in place that further aligns and incentivizes companies interests with their users the only way you can really approach this in a more equal manner is something like a lawsuit. Many times the problem is incredibly complex and while some people, even C level people may be all for providing this transparency it breaks down at scale and the company is at the end of the day beholden to its own interests and perpetuating itself. I recently saw this video on YouTube about some of the shadiness going on with Roblox: [LINK] The official answers they are giving is basically meaningless legalese and that's becoming more and more common. As I said before since Roblox isn't entering into this conversation in good faith the only way to force that conversation would be legal actions. Any kinds of laws further aligning the needs of clients/the general populace against the needs of big corporations/political elite have a really hard time passing because the system is se"},{"id":29697012,"author":"kilna","month":"2021-12","story":"The carpetbagger’s guide to home ownership","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim that politicians serve capitalists","quote":"","text":"Capitalism inherently exploits economically unequal systems, and thus incentivizes them in both the private and public sectors (especially in the US where politicians are basically owned outright[1]). So, yes, it is \"companies\"... or more specifically, large wealth-owned profit-takers, that are the root cause of the supply-demand curves AND the zoning AND all of the other contributing factors. The fact that there are different sets of capitalists (rent and investment exploiters versus labor exploiters) who have varying influence on politicians is secondary to the fact that the politicians are primarily if not solely doing the bidding of capitalists. Attempts at corporate externalizing of risk happen in multiple contradictory directions, thus causing inconsistent public policy. Regardless, privatized-profit motivated externalization of public risk is the core problem. GDP-centric increases in productivity mean precisely fuckall for working class quality of life until we solve for the fact that the entirety of increased productivity gains have gone to the wealthy in the past 5 decades[2]. I do think as a society we should build more affordable housing in densely populated areas, but allowing capitalists to hold the reins for that would accelerate all of the problems of late stage capitalism. Lastly, Japan has succeeded where others have failed because of a cultural difference which highly values new builds over old homes. They dispose of their homes and rebuild, as often as the"},{"id":25284875,"author":"JMTQp8lwXL","month":"2020-12","story":"U.S. states plan to sue Facebook next week: sources","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that ordinary people barely influence legislation","quote":"","text":"It comes down to power. Studios don't own theatres because they aren't powerful enough to lobby for that, but if were more profitable for studios and theatres to be vertically integrated (spoiler alert: it would), then you'd bet it'd happen. Maybe Disney will take the lead on that one. Look, I don't like it either, but common people have statistically insignificant impact on whether or not legislation is passed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25241274,"author":"kmonsen","month":"2020-11","story":"Most Americans Object to Government Tracking Their Activities Through Cellphones","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim that Congress ignores public opinion","quote":"","text":"Lol, not. Congress doesn't care much what the american people want, but rather disctract them with abortions and talks about scary socialism that might give them what they actually want and probably not what they fear. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19399671,"author":"mtgx","month":"2019-03","story":"Amazon Lobbied More Government Entities Than Any Other US Company Last Year","stance":"reference","why":"Shows public opinion has no correlation with policy outcomes","quote":"what the People support has ZERO correlation with how politicians will vote for something.","text":"Sure, they may not get their way 100% of the time, but studies have shown that corporate lobbying highly correlates with what U.S. politicians support or don't support. Meanwhile, what the People support has ZERO correlation with how politicians will vote for something. [VIDEO] Also, the U.S. system of \"vote transparency\" (votes are anonymous in almost all other cases, and for good reason) in Congress means that the lobbyists know which politician voted for what, and the politicians also know that the companies donating tens of thousand of dollars to them will know how they voted, too - so they will vote \"accordingly\" if they want that income stream to continue in the future. This is a rotten/corrupt relationship/dependency that should not exist in a democracy that's supposed to listen to the \"voice of the people\"."},{"id":18187102,"author":"Finbel","month":"2018-10","story":"Leaked Google briefing, admitting a “shift towards censorship”","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that lobbying is a plutocracy","quote":"","text":"> On all sides. Goldman Sachs has their man in Washington, as does Planned Parenthood. I might be misinterpreting you here but it sounds as if you're saying that all is well and good since all lobby organisations are represented in Washington. The problem to me with this idea would be that not all of the people are represented by all of the lobby organisations. Which makes the system not even a mob rule, but simply a plutocracy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16775448,"author":"prolikewhoa","month":"2018-04","story":"Free speech cannot be sacrificed to strike fake news","stance":"reference","why":"Sourced evidence that government rarely acts in citizens' interest","quote":"","text":"I agree with your second point. Who controls what's considered fake news? Very little is done in the interest of the citizens of the USA anymore [1], so why would we trust our government or media to show us what's in our best interest? [1] [VIDEO] (Fully Sourced in Description)"},{"id":16905807,"author":"zyxzevn","month":"2018-04","story":"Underestimating the mind-warping potential of fake video","stance":"reference","why":"Cited tangentially in long comment on media manipulation","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of an old science-fiction where all events on the media were computer-generated fakes. There has been no \"underestimating\" of that potential. \"Mind-warping\" has been researched and is being research extensively by the CIA for some time. Most of that research is still top-secret, but well known are the extreme psychological manipulations of subjects during the MK-Ultra experiments. I will focus on the CIA and it's influence in the past, as this is more controversial and influential than that of other agencies/countries. The influence of MI5/6 or Mossad is great, but a lot of it is still hidden. The influence of the KGB was different, as the citizens did not really believe much in them to begin with. The Chinese are going a whole different way again. The CIA also did experiments involving the manipulation of the control of subjects in social media, like facebook. But it did not stop there. After the peace-protests during the Vietnam war, the CIA has infiltrated into the press and media and anti-war movements. They now use that influence to bend war and destruction into a peace-giving action. So we had \"Freedom fighters\" in Afghanistan etc. Which was portrait in the Rambo movie. And the US supported Saddam fighting Iran with US made chemical weapons. Later the US fights the parties that it created and armed. Some strange thing that keeps repeating in the US history. At the same time the CIA has the Phoenix program and other society destructive programs. They worked to"},{"id":15910634,"author":"lowbloodsugar","month":"2017-12","story":"A list of ways to “Break the Internet” for the 48 hours before the FCC vote","stance":"reference","why":"Princeton analysis showing Congress represents the rich","quote":"Princeton University did the analysis to prove it","text":"Oh happy friend! There is only one choice. All our congresspersons represent the rich and Princeton University did the analysis to prove it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14991843,"author":"Chardok","month":"2017-08","story":"Almost All of FCC’s New Advisory Panel Works for Telecoms","stance":"reference","why":"Data shows representatives serve campaign funders","quote":"","text":"\"if you are invested in an issue, find a way to get involved (reach out to you representative etc.) and have a voice in the discussions, rather than waiting idly by and demonstrating against the result.\" [VIDEO] The data seems to show that simply isn't true. It isn't malice, but only representing the interests of those who finance your campaign and ensure you have a job for the next election, which is entirely not the point of our federal government."},{"id":14119940,"author":"guilamu","month":"2017-04","story":"Princeton’s Ad-Blocker May Put an End to the Ad-Blocking Arms Race","stance":"reference","why":"Five minute explainer on how laws are made","quote":"here's a 5 minutes video on how laws are made in America","text":"For anyone doubting the absolute veracity of this comment, here's a 5 minutes video on how laws are made in America: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13789265,"author":"odbol","month":"2017-03","story":"How Uber Used Secret “Greyball” Tool to Deceive Authorities Worldwide","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim that voter preferences rarely affect laws","quote":"Laws pass 30% of the time, whether the voters love it or hate it","text":"You don't really have a say in the US, at least. Laws pass 30% of the time, whether the voters love it or hate it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12906958,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2016-11","story":"Trump Nears Victory","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as support for claim that elections don't matter","quote":"","text":"The people who were funding Trump/Hillary were funding them equally. Is doesn't matter who won, they banks, oil companies and large media conglomerates still control the country like they have for the past several decades. I see in our future ... the exact same presidency. More US led wars. More predator drones. More CIA guns and money to fund revolutions that we then fight with out predator drones. Just remember the election is like Whose Line is it Anyway, the points are all made up and none of it matters. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11514877,"author":"kefka","month":"2016-04","story":"Top 100 Users on Hacker News by H-Index","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in passing about buy-in from the rich","quote":"","text":"Those two issues are orthogonal to each other. On some plane of understanding, business executives understand that lack of money makes commerce grind to a halt. It's a form of tragedy of the commons, in monetary philosophy. Henry Ford understood this concept; it's why he paid his people well to make cars... which his workers bought. > there is no discourse on how to handle everyone when nobody needs to work. I was in haste in that sentence. Things like UBI are being discussed. However, the flip-side is the actual actions that grind down the worker to a 'nothing': robotic/computer work is ideal for every job, to the minds of the Silicon Valley exec. UBI/Minimum Income: It's been given a many great names through the years. But it's understood as a way for everyone to receive the basic necessities of life, plus a bit more. But this idea requires buy-in of the population at large. Or at least, buy in from the \"rich\" ( [VIDEO] ) The problem with Silicon Valley is that they have an assumption this either this already exists somehow, or it will magically appear. What instead happens, is that people lose jobs, wages are forced yet lower and lower, and the human is removed carte blanche in the favor of unpaid robotic labor. On an individual company level, this helps the bottom line of the company (race to the bottom). However, when this idea is expanded to wider and wider areas, it gentrifies the owner class away from everyone else. For in that eventuality, not even upper class jobs e"},{"id":11165655,"author":"citizensixteen","month":"2016-02","story":"Bill and Melinda Gates 2016 Annual Letter","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of corruption in America","quote":"","text":"Corruption is a world-wide problem and that includes all nations. Corruption is Legal in America [Video] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10077331,"author":"itburnswheniit","month":"2015-08","story":"Decertifying the worst voting machine in the US","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes Princeton study on voter influence","quote":"Princeton confirmed it in a study earlier this year.","text":"Most voters aren't relevant anymore. Princeton confirmed it in a study earlier this year. It's neatly summed up here: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lhe286ky-9A",1]]},"N2jZryt607U":{"wy":"Recommended in threads about airbag vests as a solid overview when shopping for a motorcycle airbag.","q":{"x":"If you're in the market for a Motorcycle Airbag, this is worth a watch.","i":27055693,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28601092,"author":"kristofferR","month":"2021-09","story":"How to Ride a Motorcycle – 1942 British Military Training Film [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Helite Turtle and motorcycle airbags in general.","quote":"This is a great video about the Helite Turtle","text":"I think he was talking mostly about good motorcycle airbag vests like the Helite Turtle and e-Turtle. \"Good\" is an important qualifier word though, the worst airbags offer little protection and don't limit head movement. This is a great video about the Helite Turtle (it came out before the electronic e-Turtle) and motorcycle airbags in general. [VIDEO] I'll definitively grab a Helite e-Turtle when I start riding next year."},{"id":27055117,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-05","story":"Motorcycle airbag vest will stop working if you miss a payment","stance":"rec","why":"Survey of motorcycle airbag tech, including the Helite Turtle.","quote":"Fortnine did a great video surverying motorcycle airbag tech","text":"Note that you have the option of purchasing the vest outright, and there's also a purchase option if you subscribe for 3 years, similar to a lease. In conclusion, buy a Helite Turtle. Fully offline, no electronics, and user-refillable with simple CO2 cartridges. Fortnine did a great video surverying motorcycle airbag tech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27055693,"author":"jowsie","month":"2021-05","story":"Motorcycle airbag vest will stop working if you miss a payment","stance":"rec","why":"Useful when shopping for a motorcycle airbag.","quote":"If you're in the market for a Motorcycle Airbag, this is worth a watch.","text":"If you're in the market for a Motorcycle Airbag, this is worth a watch. [VIDEO] If you don't wanna watch, the Helite Turtle range seem to be the best way to go."},{"id":29545589,"author":"peatmoss","month":"2021-12","story":"Motorcycle Airbag Vest Will Stop Working If You Miss a Payment","stance":"reference","why":"Rationale for tethered airbag vests matches commenter's reasons.","quote":"Fortnine has a video whose rationale matches mine","text":"I wear a Helite Turtle 2 airbag vest when I go motorcycling. I wear it for three reasons: - There is a mechanical tether. It is a reliable mechanism that is not subject to subscriptions or software of any sort. If I’m clipped in, I’m protected. - Its air pockets are bigger than the “cool” airbag vests that are designed to fit under your clothing. Bigger air pockets really do translate into better impact protection. AND it has CE armor in the back as well, just in case. Below a certain threshold of air displacement, you’re not much better off than armor by itself. - The air channels run over my shoulders and are designed to immobilize my head and neck in the event of a crash. Fortnine has a video whose rationale matches mine: [VIDEO] The downside of my airbag style is that if I low-side and stay with the bike, the airbag won’t deploy. That said, in this kind of crash, I’m much more worried about abrasions (which is also why I wear a one-piece Aerostich suit)."}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["DlTq8DbRs4k",1],["usSfgHGEGxQ",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["AdKNCBrkZQ4",1],["a0atL1HSwi8",1]]},"PHiyQID7SBs":{"wy":"Recommended as a good visual walkthrough of the Standard Model equation for people who want to understand its terms.","q":{"x":"PBS Spacetime has a very good video[0] that helps visualize what's going on in the equation, recent too!","i":33402061,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":39987508,"author":"hughesjj","month":"2024-04","story":"The deconstructed Standard Model equation","stance":"rec","why":"More comprehensive breakdown of the Standard Model equation.","quote":"There's been more comprehensive breakdowns since","text":"Yup. That said it seems like a quick blog post from 2016. There's been more comprehensive breakdowns since [VIDEO]"},{"id":33402061,"author":"irjustin","month":"2022-10","story":"Why is symmetry so important in particle physics?","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes what is going on in the Standard Model equation.","quote":"PBS Spacetime has a very good video[0] that helps visualize what's going on in the equation, recent too!","text":"PBS Spacetime has a very good video[0] that helps visualize what's going on in the equation, recent too! [VIDEO]"},{"id":38070344,"author":"ben_w","month":"2023-10","story":"How deep is the brain? The shallow brain hypothesis","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It means that there is no guarantee that, given a non-continuous function function f(x), there exists an NN that approximates it over its entire domain withing some precision p. And why is this important? > Do you know of a universal algorithm that can take a continuous function and a target precision, and return an NN architecture (number of layers, number of neurons per layer) and a starting set of weights for an NN, and a training set, such that training the NN will reach the final state? > All I'm claiming is that there is no known algorithm of this kind, and also that the existence of such an algorithm is not guaranteed by any known theorem. I think so: the construction proof of the claim that they are universal function approximators seems to meet those requirements. Even better: it just goes direct to giving you the weights and biases. > My point was relevant because we are discussing whether an NN might be equivalent to the human brain, and using the Universal Approximation Theorem to try to decide this. So what I'm saying is that even if \"knowning English\" were a continuous function and \"knowing French\" were a continuous function, so by the theorem we know there are NNs that can approximate either one, there is no guarantee that there exists a single NN which can approximate both. There might or might not be one, but the theorem doesn't promise one must exist. I still don't understand your point. It still doesn't seem to matter? If any organic brain can't do $thing"}],"r":[["TwKpj2ISQAc",1],["9crggox5rbc",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1]]},"SZazYFchLRI":{"wy":"HN uses it as an example of radically different musical notation and as a favorite, if challenging, modernist piece that anticipates sounds like the THX Deep Note.","q":{"x":"look at the notation for this piece by Iannis Xenakis.","i":2026663,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":31821446,"author":"nirse","month":"2022-06","story":"‘The War on Music’ Review: Songs Without Listeners","stance":"rec","why":"One of the better known post-war modernist pieces.","quote":"here are some of the better known 'inhumane' pieces, all from the post-war European modernists, which I all like a lot","text":"here are some of the better known 'inhumane' pieces, all from the post-war European modernists, which I all like a lot: [LINK] (Klavierstueck ix by Stockhausen) [LINK] (Pli selon pli by Boulez) [LINK] (Athmosphere by Ligeti) [LINK] (Sinfonia by Berio) [VIDEO] (Metastasis by Xenakis) And a more recent work: [LINK] (La commedia by Andriessen)"},{"id":7538437,"author":"computer","month":"2014-04","story":"Recreating the THX Deep Note","stance":"rec","why":"1953 orchestral piece anticipating the THX Deep Note effect.","quote":"It's one of my favorite songs, mostly for when I want to be left alone in a room.","text":"Iannis Xenakis' song Metastasis got there first as far as I know, in 1953 with a full orchestra. It has a slightly different (less harmonic) end: [VIDEO] (mostly the first minute) It's one of my favorite songs, mostly for when I want to be left alone in a room."},{"id":2026663,"author":"ohyes","month":"2010-12","story":"The Semicolon Wars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Xenakis score as example of evolving, radically different musical notation.","quote":"look at the notation for this piece by Iannis Xenakis.","text":"Western musical notation has been continuously evolving. It is far from being 'largely unchanged for hundreds of years'. For example, look at the notation for this piece by Iannis Xenakis. (Shows the sheet music in the video). [VIDEO] Compare to this gregorian chant: [LINK] Or to this piece by his teacher oliver messiaen [LINK] There are some important things that are the same between the chant and the piece by Messian, but the notations are very very different, and have accumulated some really important stuff over the years. Saying that musical notation has been largely unchanged for hundreds of years is kind of like saying that English has remained largely unchanged for hundreds of years.... in that it is incorrect."},{"id":1364101,"author":"bd","month":"2010-05","story":"Iannis Xenakis - Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It seems like math and music is not always a winning combination","text":"This made me curious to check his actual work. It seems like math and music is not always a winning combination: [VIDEO] (Metastasis) [LINK] (Psappha) [LINK] (Terretektorh)"}],"r":[["-hCimLnIsDA",1]]},"VNTQ-M_uSo8":{"wy":"Cited as an argument for runtime specs as an alternative to static typing, including a comparison of types, tests and spec.","q":{"x":"He offers a chart that shows the strengths and weaknesses of strict typing versus unit tests versus the run-time checks offered by Spec. It's worth a look.","i":13051063,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":30770837,"author":"Jonovono","month":"2022-03","story":"Clojure 1.11 is now available","stance":"rec","why":"Clojure spec as a powerful alternative to static typing.","quote":"clojure has one of the most powerful ways to 'type' your language?","text":"..... you realise clojure has one of the most powerful ways to 'type' your language? Like 100x more powerful than the typescript or whatever type language you are using now ;p [VIDEO]"},{"id":13051063,"author":"susan_hall","month":"2016-11","story":"The Unsound Playground: Java and Scala's Type Systems are Unsound","stance":"rec","why":"Compares strict typing, unit tests and runtime checks.","quote":"He offers a chart that shows the strengths and weaknesses of strict typing versus unit tests versus the run-time checks offered by Spec. It's worth a look.","text":"There have been some fantastic conversations on Hacker News about type systems. I've learned a lot about the different arguments for dynamic typing, versus strict typing, versus gradual typing. Java's type system may or may not have represented new thinking when it was under development in the early 1990s, but it certainly seems a bit dated in the year 2016. The limits of the Java type system have been discussed both here on HackerNews and also elsewhere, many times. For those who want to consider arguments against Java's style of strict typing, 2 things I would recommend include \"Agility & Robustness: Clojure spec, by Stuart Halloway\": [VIDEO] He offers a chart that shows the strengths and weaknesses of strict typing versus unit tests versus the run-time checks offered by Spec. It's worth a look. The discussions around gradual typing have been interesting, but to see how far the limits of this can be pushed, I would suggest everyone check out the Qi/Shen programming language: [LINK] \"Qi makes use of the logical notation of sequent calculus to define types. This type notation, under Qi’s interpretation, is actually a Turing complete language in its own right. This notation allows Qi to assign extensible type systems to Common Lisp libraries and is thought of as an extremely powerful feature of the language.\" This next quote is from someone who has spent a long time experimenting with different Lisps: \"Qi (and its successor Shen) really push the limits of what we might call a "},{"id":13478310,"author":"lkrubner","month":"2017-01","story":"Making the move from Scala to Go","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Since they cite me (and my essay from 2014) as part of their decision making process, I want to throw in 2 cents here. They ended up deciding on Go, whereas I have ended up preferring Clojure, yet I agree with a lot of what they say, so I'll try to clarify why I ended up with a different decision than what they made. I understand what they mean when they write: I think the first time I appreciated the positive aspects of having a strong type system was with Scala. Personally, coming from a myriad of PHP silent errors and whimsical behavior, it felt quite empowering to have the confidence that, supported by type-checking and a few well-thought-out tests, my code was doing what it was meant to. There are times when I appreciate the strict type-checking that happens in Java. I do get what they mean. But there are also a lot of times when I hate strict type-checking (in particular, when dealing with anything outside of the control of my code, such as whimsical, changing 3rd party APIs that I have to consume (for some business reason), or even 1st party APIs that feel like 3rd party APIs because they are developed by another team (within the same company) or for some reason we can not fix the broken aspects of some old API that was developed in-house 6 years ago.) Because of this, I have become a proponent of gradual typing. If I am facing a problem that I have never faced before, I like to start off without any types in my code, and then, as I understand the problem more, I like "}],"r":[["1bNOO3xxMc0",1]]},"XRXwWbo_mX0":{"wy":"Treated as a favorite hack and a go-to example in threads about machines playing music.","q":{"x":"if you haven't heard an F1 car play God Save The Queen [0] it's a great watch","i":24685544,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24685544,"author":"wastedhours","month":"2020-10","story":"Honda quits F1, invests in carbon-free tech instead","stance":"rec","why":"F1 car playing a tune, showing how controllable the engine is","quote":"if you haven't heard an F1 car play God Save The Queen [0] it's a great watch","text":"I once walked the footbridge over the hillclimb at Goodwood during the Festival of Speed as a V10 F1 car went underneath. Was something else entirely. Seeing the musical demos the Renault team used to do was always interesting too - if you haven't heard an F1 car play God Save The Queen [0] it's a great watch (and a reminder about how controllable they are). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9287366,"author":"tdicola","month":"2015-03","story":"Dot matrix printer playing “Eye of the Tiger” [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Renault F1 engine playing God Save The Queen via RPM control","quote":"My favorite version of this hack is Renault changing the RPM of their F1 engine to play God Save The Queen","text":"My favorite version of this hack is Renault changing the RPM of their F1 engine to play God Save The Queen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41626421,"author":"cromulent","month":"2024-09","story":"Let your mower sing Happy Birthday for your loved ones","stance":"reference","why":"Renault F1 engine playing tunes through RPM","quote":"The Renault F1 engine could famously play tunes this way.","text":"Yeah I am guessing it is from the motor RPM pitch but I would like to find out. The Renault F1 engine could famously play tunes this way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38423714,"author":"NextHendrix","month":"2023-11","story":"Tesla Normal Operating Sounds","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an engine playing a tune","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Maybe this is what you're referring to. There are similar examples where people do this with old disk drives and printers too."},{"id":31729836,"author":"qbasic_forever","month":"2022-06","story":"The Floppotron 3.0","stance":"reference","why":"Renault demonstrating drivetrain control by playing music","quote":"Renault did it some years ago to demonstrate how much control they have on their F1 engine drivetrain","text":"> Next trick...turn a Tesla into a musical instrument via the motor drives. Renault did it some years ago to demonstrate how much control they have on their F1 engine drivetrain: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19518698,"author":"paddy_m","month":"2019-03","story":"Tone.js – A framework for making interactive music in the browser","stance":"reference","why":"F1 engine playing a tune, sounds atrocious but cool","quote":"Here is an f1 car engine playing God Save the Queen [1], sounds atrocious, but still cool.","text":"The examples were a lot of fun to play with. Nice work. I was thinking earlier today of creating an F1 engine note simulator. I would want to play with the number of cylinders, v angle, crank offset (v8 normal crank/180 crank) and maybe some kind of exhaust system variables (tuned length, crossover pipe), and of course RPM. I don't know where to start, could you point me at the type of programming I would want to look at to figure out how to do this? Here is an f1 car engine playing God Save the Queen [1], sounds atrocious, but still cool. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ON0A1dsQOV0",1],["E8kUJL04ELA",1],["ILNs1GChGDk",1]]},"ed2FWNWwE3I":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an insider-style look at quants and high-frequency trading and their effect on markets.","q":{"x":"just to get an insider look into quant traders and the effect HFT can have on the market, I also recommend these two short documentaries","i":6628049,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":25972963,"author":"kratom_sandwich","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Best Resources on (Computational) Finance","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on quants featuring Paul Wilmott.","quote":"Documentary on \"quants\" featuring him","text":"Check out the works of Paul Wilmott, which include textbooks and a magazine of the same name which is (or at least used to be) the most expensive magazine subscription there is. Wikipedia: [LINK] Documentary on \"quants\" featuring him: [VIDEO] For finance in general, I recommend \"Corporate Finance\" by Berk & de Marzo or the CFA study material, which is quite accessible (at least for Level 1)."},{"id":6628049,"author":"chinmoy","month":"2013-10","story":"How to Get a Job at a High Frequency Trading Firm","stance":"rec","why":"Insider look at quant traders and HFT's market effects.","quote":"just to get an insider look into quant traders and the effect HFT can have on the market, I also recommend these two short documentaries","text":"I'm happy to see this pop up on HN since HFT is something I've been looking into lately. If you're interested to dig deeper into HFT and algorithmic trading, 'Inside the Black Box' will be a good read. Link: www.amazon.com/Inside-Black-Box-Quantitative-Frequency/dp/1118362411 If you want to take your reading further, there are some great recommendations on the review section. And just to get an insider look into quant traders and the effect HFT can have on the market, I also recommend these two short documentaries made by the dutch national TV: 1. Quants: The Alchemists of Wall Street [VIDEO] 2. Money & Speed: Inside the Black Box [LINK]"},{"id":24309938,"author":"emrehan","month":"2020-08","story":"Ethereum Is a Dark Forest","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant to risks present in conventional finance.","quote":"Similar risks have been present in conventional finance and economy too. Relevant","text":"Good point but it won’t stop DeFi’s growth. In my opinion, such risks shouldn’t stop DeFi’s growth neither. Similar risks have been present in conventional finance and economy too. Relevant: [VIDEO] Instead of fearing from the risks we should quantify and analyze them."}],"r":[["_o7qjN3KF8U",1],["nq_SpRBXRmE",1]]},"h67k9eEw9AY":{"wy":"Cited whenever euphemisms appear in corporate, product or news language, as the classic comedic critique.","q":{"x":"I refer you to George Carlin [1], who explained it better than I could.","i":6645304,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":8800435,"author":"7Figures2Commas","month":"2014-12","story":"“Employee” and other words that make me cringe","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin skit on soft language, suggested to an author uneasy with the word employee.","quote":"the author should watch George Carlin's skit on \"soft language\"[1] and reflect.","text":"Humble suggestion: the author should watch George Carlin's skit on \"soft language\"[1] and reflect. A mature individual can contribute to a company he or she didn't start and still feel a sense of responsibility and ownership without being coddled by language that pretends employees aren't employees, teams have no leaders, and everybody is equally responsible for the success or failure of the business. Frankly, I would probably avoid a company run by a founder who appears to have hang-ups about being an employer and seems reluctant to accept the responsibilities and rewards of leadership. This is about as appealing as going for a ride in a car without a driver. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6645304,"author":"chill1","month":"2013-10","story":"California woman ticketed for wearing Google Glass while driving","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin explains how softened language removes humanity from serious subjects.","quote":"I refer you to George Carlin [1], who explained it better than I could.","text":"For starters, we could all stop calling them car \"accidents\"... The language we use has a direct affect on the emotions we feel while talking about subjects such as this. Softening our language takes the humanity out of life. I refer you to George Carlin [1], who explained it better than I could. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38985079,"author":"fasterik","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: Who else is working on nothing?","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on soft language obscuring uncomfortable facts, especially aging and death.","quote":"His criticism of soft language about aging and death starts around the 5 minute mark","text":"I am frequently reminded of George Carlin's bit about \"soft language\" and how it obscures facts of life that we're uncomfortable about: [VIDEO] His criticism of soft language about aging and death starts around the 5 minute mark"},{"id":31481834,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2022-05","story":"Klarna – Company announcement from CEO regarding layoffs","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin bit on euphemistic language, applied to corporate layoff wording.","quote":"George Carlin had a bit[1] about this.","text":"Can we all agree that \"impacted\" is worse than both of those? It provides no clear information about what is actually happening, relying on the entire audience to make the \"aha, we're being laid off\" connection themselves. \"Impacted\" appeared five times, \"Affected\" once, but it was left to the recipients' imaginations to figure out what they were actually being impacted by. Finally, they get around to framing it as \"not being offered a role in the new organization\"--another euphemism. I mean, it's one thing to soften the message a little when bearing bad news, but \"impacted\" is softened to the point of uselessness. George Carlin had a bit[1] about this. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21179278,"author":"coldtea","month":"2019-10","story":"Monty Python at 50","stance":"reference","why":"Links Carlin as a source for a quote while questioning comedians' pretensions.","quote":"George Carlin also said this","text":"George Carlin also said this: [LINK] [VIDEO] It's also not always clear cut who is the real underdog and who the gentrified upper middle class finger-pointing comedian who pretends to be the greater person..."},{"id":14503982,"author":"calcifer","month":"2017-06","story":"Certified Pre-owned Teslas for Less Than $40K USD","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Carlin's soft language bit for the term pre-owned.","quote":"The soft language [0] version of \"secondhand\".","text":"Ah, \"pre-owned\"... The soft language [0] version of \"secondhand\". You see, poor people buy secondhand cars and ewww that's icky and definitely not meant for the temporarily embarrassed millionaires looking for a Tesla. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"pq1XqP4-qOo":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on Linux desktop application distribution as a clear treatment of why binary compatibility and static linking are hard.","q":{"x":"Here's good video about this problem space btw","i":39332793,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39332793,"author":"Cloudef","month":"2024-02","story":"Building the DirectX shader compiler better than Microsoft?","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on the problem space of Linux binary stability.","quote":"Here's good video about this problem space btw","text":"That mesa issue reminded me of wayland issue thats been open for 3 years now where apps crash if theres even a slight stall due to the wayland socket becoming full. Instead of providing quick fix for actual app devs and users who are affected most, they have been designing this perfect solution for apps and compositor to agree on the socket size for 3 years now. Its similar crap with the server size decorations where they finally had to admit people want them and merged the protocol but don't implement it in gnome compositor :) I'd say wayland really was barebones when it was being pushed and major contributions to actually get it usable on desktop was from outsiders. I'd say win32/flatpak/libretro are the only sane way for games to target linux right now. The fact that linux doesn't have real \"runtime\" and major components required by games link against libc is what makes linux really unstable for anything that needs to open window, draw stuff using GPU and play audio. It's possible to create static binaries for linux that work for eternity, thanks to kernel being actually stable. But link against something in /usr/lib and it all goes to hell. If the GPU drivers and libs that provided basic window / audio did not depend on libc and were standalone, the situation would be much better. Here's good video about this problem space btw [VIDEO]"},{"id":29480317,"author":"ndesaulniers","month":"2021-12","story":"A proposal to start “llvm-libc” (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting point about hard-coded PT_INTERP.","quote":"Andrew Kelley makes an interesting point in","text":"Andrew Kelley makes an interesting point in: [VIDEO] about the PT_INTERP being hard-coded. That seems like a long ago assumption baked that binaries should only have one program interpreter per system. I don't know enough about configuring a system and the historical decisions around PT_INTERP, but I suspect Andrew is on to something."},{"id":42554946,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2024-12","story":"Why Linux is not ready for the desktop, the final edition","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on stable graphics ABI and static binaries.","quote":"Related","text":"> Linux is not an operating system It is though. Go ahead and find me any distribution of Linux on which this binary of the Zig compiler does not work: [LINK] You can't, because it's a static executable whose only runtime dependencies are a file system. And if kernel devs would bless us with a stable graphics ABI, then we could do this for games, too. Unfortunately, the way drivers work currently relies on dynamic linking in a way that drags in a dependency on differences in distributions, which is completely unnecessary and maddening. Related: [VIDEO] P.S. Just bought my wife a steam deck for christmas and she loves it."},{"id":40929379,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2024-07","story":"Zed on Linux Is Here","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by commenter on Linux application installation culture.","quote":"Some talks I have given on this topic","text":"My interest is not in using this text editor as a consumer, but in guiding software development culture in general, particularly when it comes to installation of Linux applications. Some talks I have given on this topic: [VIDEO] [LINK] So, I think \"entitled\" is the wrong insult. \"arrogant\" would be more accurate."},{"id":35694508,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2023-04","story":"Fedora 38 LLVM vs. Team Fortress 2","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on shipping static Linux executables that do graphics.","quote":"this talk that I made 2 years ago about an experiment to ship static executables on linux that could do graphics","text":"related: this talk that I made 2 years ago about an experiment to ship static executables on linux that could do graphics: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YEBfamv-_do",1]]},"ZxYOEwM6Wbk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the explanation that makes complex exponentials click, especially for understanding the Fourier transform.","q":{"x":"I was finally able to understand the role of complex numbers in the Fourier Transform.","i":34389650,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34389650,"author":"rectang","month":"2023-01","story":"The Fourier Transform, explained in one sentence","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reframes e^x, unlocking understanding of complex numbers in Fourier","quote":"I was finally able to understand the role of complex numbers in the Fourier Transform.","text":"The key insight for me on this topic also came from 3Blue1Brown, but in a different video: it’s that e^x is NOT best thought of as repeated multiplication, but instead as the function exp(x) = 1 + x + x^2/2 + X^3/6 + x^4/24 + … After being relieved of the burden of that misconception, I was finally able to understand the role of complex numbers in the Fourier Transform. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34389926,"author":"rectang","month":"2023-01","story":"The Fourier Transform, explained in one sentence","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes how the exponential produces rotation","quote":"Later in that video, we see a visualization of the rotation.","text":"People told me that over and over but it didn’t help — because it didn’t make sense why repeated multiplication would cause rotation! Later in that video, we see a visualization of the rotation. I was able to grasp how the exp function could yield rotation where I’d never been able to understand why e*e*e*e… did. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27392575,"author":"beforeolives","month":"2021-06","story":"Is Euler’s Identity Beautiful? and If So, How?","stance":"reference","why":"Visual of vectors cancelling onto the unit circle","quote":"If you look at the way the vectors at 38:00-39:20[1] cancel out to always land on the unit circle","text":"I think that e^(i*pi) + 1 = 0 is massively overrated. Because the beauty that people find is largely based on the symbols that are present and because it doesn't really say anything meaningful beyond itself. It's not even a formula or an equation - it's a result. It's like being impressed by cos(pi) = -1. The more general formula e^(ix) = cosx + i*sinx is a lot more impressive. Both because of it's implications for the rest of mathematics and aesthetically. If you look at the way the vectors at 38:00-39:20[1] cancel out to always land on the unit circle - I can get behind calling that beautiful. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24867768,"author":"rathel","month":"2020-10","story":"A Programmer’s Intuition for Matrix Multiplication","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I don't agree with what he said here @ 8:00","text":"True, but I don't agree with what he said here @ 8:00 [VIDEO] Is this really the case? I checked Wikipedia and MathWorld and nobody makes a distinction between e^x and exp(x). Even if it's a \"white lie\" for didactic reasons, I don't buy it, it will be much more confusing down the line for students. Math is about finding structures governed by some rules and then generalizing them. To me the e^x defined as repeated multiplication conceptually is the same thing as the exponential function, just over broader domain. Why? You can interpolate between integer-valued X's using geometric mean. e^3 = sqrt(e^2 * e^4). What stops you from interpolating this recursively to achieve in the limit the exponential function over real numbers?"}],"r":[["L3LMbpZIKhQ",1]]},"3bxcc3SM_KA":{"wy":"HN shares it as a humorous but accurate primer on patent trolling and why East Texas is a favored venue.","q":{"x":"Above two YouTube videos I’ve been passing around","i":19239523,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19239523,"author":"rixrax","month":"2019-02","story":"Apple to Close Stores in Eastern District of Texas to Fight Patent Trolls","stance":"rec","why":"Passed around to explain peculiar US patent law and East Texas.","quote":"Above two YouTube videos I’ve been passing around","text":"URL: [LINK] (Austin Myers) URL: [VIDEO] (John Olivier) Above two YouTube videos I’ve been passing around in regard to sometimes peculiar nature of patents law in US and in particular that of eastern district of TX."},{"id":41288855,"author":"soraminazuki","month":"2024-08","story":"EFF Presses Federal Circuit to Make Patent Case Filings Public","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Samsung even made an outdoor ice skating rink in front of the court house for that reason. An ice skating rink. Outdoor. In Texas. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11241424,"author":"donretag","month":"2016-03","story":"“A Popular Venue for Patent Cases”","stance":"reference","why":"Humorous view of Marshall, TX as a patent venue.","quote":"Here is a humorous view by John Oliver, of nearby (60 miles) Marshall, TX","text":"So blatant. Who is responsible for that site? Local chamber of commerce? Here is a humorous view by John Oliver, of nearby (60 miles) Marshall, TX, another venue for patent trolls [VIDEO]"},{"id":11034846,"author":"sesutton","month":"2016-02","story":"Apple Ordered to Pay $625M to Patent Troll in FaceTime Lawsuit","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver segment on patents and Marshall, Texas.","quote":"relevant John Oliver clip","text":"I don't think it's a coincidence there is that there is a ice skating rink right in front of the courthouse in Marshall, Texas with giant Samsung logos all over it (relevant John Oliver clip [VIDEO] ) ."}],"r":[["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["RfdZTZQvuCo",1]]},"5t1vTLU7s40":{"wy":"Cited for LeCun's views on LLM limits and alternative approaches to intelligence.","q":{"x":"I think you might find Lex Fridmans interview with Yann LeCun interesting[1].","i":40323744,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40323744,"author":"jtietema","month":"2024-05","story":"Ask HN: Anyone working on something better than LLMs?","stance":"rec","why":"LeCun on LLM limits and alternative approaches.","quote":"I think you might find Lex Fridmans interview with Yann LeCun interesting[1].","text":"I think you might find Lex Fridmans interview with Yann LeCun interesting[1]. It discusses exactly this, how LLMs just mimmick intelligent behaviour but have no understanding of the world at all. It also discusses other approaches we should look at instead of current LLMs. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41839110,"author":"zero-sharp","month":"2024-10","story":"Apple Study Reveals Critical Flaws in AI's Logical Reasoning Abilities","stance":"reference","why":"LeCun discussing LLM reasoning limitations.","quote":"Relevant conversation with Yann Lecun","text":">The findings reveal that even slight changes in the phrasing of questions can cause major discrepancies in model performance that can undermine their reliability in scenarios requiring logical consistency. Relevant conversation with Yann Lecun: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40270746,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2024-05","story":"“Meta spent almost as much as the Manhattan Project on GPUs in today's dollars”","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Meta's training compute facts.","quote":"","text":"These are all facts coming from publicly available information, such as the Llama 3 paper and recent interviews. I'd dig up the specific timestamps, but I'm working at the moment and can't spend the next hour scrolling through long interviews. The interviews are: \"Mark Zuckerberg - Llama 3, $10B Models, Caesar Augustus, & 1 GW Datacenters\": [LINK] \"Yann Lecun: Meta AI, Open Source, Limits of LLMs, AGI & the Future of AI | Lex Fridman Podcast #416 \": [VIDEO] The total FLOPS used in training is in bunch of places, I can't find a good link at the moment."},{"id":40516813,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2024-05","story":"Nvidia is about to pass Apple in market cap","stance":"reference","why":"LeCun on limits of current AI for real-world operation.","quote":"","text":"The Unitree is what made me think we are headed for a bubble. The machines themselves will become commodity hardware. Tesla bot will push the hype side. I just don’t believe current AI systems have what it takes to operate in the real world. It doesn’t have to be AGI at all, but it needs a bunch of capabilities not present in existing models, some of which cannot be created by training on large corpuses from the internet like text and images. [1] Also people can’t afford to spend $20k on a robot just to do their dishes. My good friend tried to make a robot that could pick up dog poop from your back yard, and he went to HAX accelerator and everything, and through extensive research he found that the addressable market for dog poop robots is just too small to fund a company. Now he makes farming robots. (I do too as it happens, on a separate project.) The point is a humanoid robot worth the price of that robot needs to be very useful, and I don’t believe current approaches can get us there. Maybe some uses will be found, just as current AI systems have their uses, but I say it will be a hype bubble because companies will fund raise on massive promises they will never achieve, and investment will move on to something else after 5 years or so. Could still be robotics, but I actually think modular purpose built machines make way more sense than general purpose humanoids. What if instead of a humanoid to load the dishwasher you had a dishwasher that cleaned dishes one at a time. Yo"},{"id":39692261,"author":"hn_acker","month":"2024-03","story":"Physical Intelligence is building a brain for robots","stance":"reference","why":"LeCun's estimate of visual data a child receives.","quote":"","text":"> A 4 year old child has 16k wake hours x 3600 s/hour x 1^6 optical nerve fibers x 2 eyes x 10 bytes/s = 1^15 bytes (approximation by Yann LeCun). Unfortunate typo. You meant 10^15 bytes at the end. Thanks to your citation I was able to find a podcast transcript [1] with Yann LeCun's explanation: > If you talk to developmental psychologists and they tell you a four-year-old has been awake for 16,000 hours in his or her life, and the amount of information that has reached the visual cortex of that child in four years is about 10 to 15 bytes. The transcript is missing \"the\" (10 to the 15 bytes). The corresponding timestamp in the podcast on YouTube is 4:48. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"AaZ_RSt0KP8":{"wy":"Commenters link it whenever bit flips, cosmic ray errors or unexplained computer glitches come up, as a good accessible explanation.","q":{"x":"The Universe is Hostile to Computers\" by Veritasium is good video.","i":31430814,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31430814,"author":"shmde","month":"2022-05","story":"Engineers investigating NASA’s Voyager 1 telemetry data","stance":"rec","why":"Good video explaining bit flips","quote":"The Universe is Hostile to Computers\" by Veritasium is good video.","text":"People interested in knowing more about Bitflip. \"The Universe is Hostile to Computers\" by Veritasium is good video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41369680,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2024-08","story":"Intelligence is not like height","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"ref : The Universe is Hostile to Computers","text":"There is no software at all, it is all hardware. These are mere abstractions because the reality offers some guarantees which allows one to transfer one system onto another as if the underlying system didn't matter. Those guarantees allows one to speak of commonalities between one stuff and another. ref : The Universe is Hostile to Computers [VIDEO]"},{"id":39063246,"author":"Etherlord87","month":"2024-01","story":"Fujitsu says it will pay compensation in UK Post Office scandal","stance":"reference","why":"Software rarely handles cosmic ray bit flips","quote":"Check out the video by Veritasium \"The Universe is Hostile to Computers\" [1]","text":"> There's an extent to which I think this is reasonable. If I'm accused of fraud based on evidence recovered from a bank computer, it should not be the case that I can require the prosecution to prove that the bank's computers function correctly, and the evidence be thrown out in case the prosecution are unable to do so. So a poor individual with no resources should find experts to analyze a software he doesn't even have access to (I know, discovery, but the practical limitations often make it near impossible to find the error within a given time, like having the access only in an isolated room without the internet or your own software, or having an access only to printed source code on paper), rather than the prosecutor (the state) or the company that definitely have the resources? Obviously almost no one would go to jail then. But this would be because of a very low coding standard. You pretty much can't prove the validity of software, because somewhere around 99,99% just isn't coded in a language and regime that would guarantee being bug-free. Check out the video by Veritasium \"The Universe is Hostile to Computers\" [1] - how much software, do you think, has internal error correction implemented to deal with a possible cosmic ray flipping a bit? The solution is to have extensive logs of data, stored by independent actors, with a lot of redundancy or otherwise error detection and correction. I find it horrifying that someone as well versed in law as you is fine with putting "},{"id":37221526,"author":"Scoring6931","month":"2023-08","story":"Brazilian hacker claims Bolsonaro asked him to hack into the 2022 voting system","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a vote count error from a bit flip","quote":"It sounds ludicrous, but actually has happened before","text":"In the Brazilian electronic voting system, there is no mechanism to check that the counting on each machine was performed correctly. Validation only occurs after that point. So if the machine somehow subtracts 256 votes from one candidate and transfers them to another, the total remains the same and this discrepancy isn't caught. It sounds ludicrous, but actually has happened before: [VIDEO] This is just one example of how the Brazilian system isn't ideal. The criticism against it is very well supported by sound arguments, and it's a shame that it got politicised. It's a pure technical matter."},{"id":33176466,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-10","story":"The computer errors from outer space","stance":"reference","why":"Video covers the same examples as the article","quote":"Some of the same examples are in the article and the video.","text":"A YouTube video on the subject : Veritasium - The Universe is Hostile to Computers [VIDEO] Some of the same examples are in the article and the video."},{"id":30004062,"author":"debdut","month":"2022-01","story":"Totenpass is a permanent digital storage drive made of solid gold","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It’s not about efficiency, think how many years a hard drive will last? 3 years. How many years a gold plate will last? thousands of years. Least reactive metal not for a reason lol Plus if there is a ion blast from a nearby supernova, it’ll erase all electronic storage devices on earth [VIDEO]"},{"id":28454318,"author":"reeeeee","month":"2021-09","story":"Protecting Supercomputers from an Extraterrestrial Threat","stance":"reference","why":"Video on cosmic rays changing a Belgian election result","quote":"Veritassium posted a video regarding cosmic rays changing the election result in Belgium some time ago[1].","text":"I did some looking into this subject after Veritassium posted a video regarding cosmic rays changing the election result in Belgium some time ago[1]. But looking further into the issue, it seemed that there was (and is) scepticism regarding cosmic rays being the actual reason for the miscount. [2][3] 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":28614022,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-09","story":"While posting to Tumblr, E and W keys just stopped working","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's actually testable and determinable from several perspetives. - Isolate any other sources of ionising radiation. - Check to see if prevalence increases or decreases with increased or decreased cosmic ray exposure (atitude, shielding, detected cosmic ray storms). - Are the characteristics those of single-bit flips? (E.g., power-of-two changes to values, or similar.) - Are the errors nott repeated for the same hardware component. (E.g., under increased cosmic-ray influence, bits flip at an increased rate, as predicted, but which bits flip is random and has no detectable pattern, as predicted.) You end up with very strong circumstantial evidence of altered bits due to cosmic ray influence. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aIx2N-viNwY",1]]},"Boi0XEm9-4E":{"wy":"HN users cite it as a short, inspiring look at the Dutch approach to bike-friendly urban planning.","q":{"x":"Shows the fantastic transformation of Utrecht.","i":20367645,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20367645,"author":"l4u532","month":"2019-07","story":"How Utrecht Became a Paradise for Cyclists","stance":"rec","why":"Short film on Utrecht's transformation for bikes","quote":"Shows the fantastic transformation of Utrecht.","text":"Having lived in Copenhagen I can only attest the surge in living quality when a city adopts a no-compromise bicycle strategy. Back in car-loving Munich I feel set back a century. With city council members putting forward arguments like that bike lanes will “destroy retail businesses”. It’s frustrating. Ps: here’s the short film the article talks about. Shows the fantastic transformation of Utrecht. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30236433,"author":"drran","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: How do you deal with getting old and feeling lost?","stance":"reference","why":"Netherlands as inspiration for safer bike lanes","quote":"We are heavily inspired by success of Netherlands","text":"I'm from Europe (Ukraine). Yes, I'm totally aware about risk of death in a road incident. After each incident, our bicyclist association does protest near to city administration, demanding more and safer bike lanes. Our plan to fix it is to make the city safer for cyclist. We are heavily inspired by success of Netherlands: [VIDEO] . Situation is improved in recent years. Currently, half of my path to work is safe, while the other half is not. Helmet is a good idea (I recommend to wear helmet after all), but it has some downsides. We can discuss upsides and downsides of helmet a lot, but I recommend using my OKR instead, when you recommend biking to newbies: «use helmet until you will fall 10 times at least». It's simple, easy to understand, easy to remember, actionable, and leaves no room for doubt."},{"id":21334453,"author":"jacobparker","month":"2019-10","story":"We Need to Dream Bigger Than Bike Lanes","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dutch cyclists mostly without helmets","quote":"count the helmets.","text":"> Biking isn’t safe enough to not wear a helmet Could you explain why you think that? That’s a pretty general statement. Are you imagining city biking here, or something more like racing in the country-side? Some data about how this is dangerous could be convincing: [VIDEO] count the helmets. Just to be clear, I’m not against helmets (I wear one, as is custom in my country)."}]},"Kzpv47o80eg":{"wy":"Cited in prefab and housing threads as proof that high-quality modular homes can be assembled on site in hours.","q":{"x":"assembling a modular home in Japan","i":17258253,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17258253,"author":"sithadmin","month":"2018-06","story":"Developers are manufacturing prefabricated apartment buildings","stance":"rec","why":"ToyotaHome assembling a modular home.","quote":"assembling a modular home in Japan","text":"Here's a cool video of ToyotaHome (yes, that Toyota) assembling a modular home in Japan: [VIDEO] Their website: [LINK] (JP only, no English)"},{"id":26419172,"author":"Mindless2112","month":"2021-03","story":"What We Can Learn from Japanese Prefab Homes","stance":"reference","why":"Prefab house built in a few hours.","quote":"it might only take 6 hours","text":"There's a good chance of not seeing one being built since it might only take 6 hours -- see [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13800237,"author":"tekacs","month":"2017-03","story":"A $10K tiny house 3D-printed in 24 hours","stance":"reference","why":"House assembled in a day in Japan.","quote":"a house built in a day (customisable, moderately expensive but very high quality)","text":"It might come down to culture/demand? In Japan where it's preferred to make a new home rather than buy a pre-owned one (outside the big city), pre-fab seems to have taken off rather more (13% of new builds in 2013). For example, a house built in a day (customisable, moderately expensive but very high quality): [VIDEO]"},{"id":13094624,"author":"Mindless2112","month":"2016-12","story":"Who Lives with Their Parents?","stance":"reference","why":"Toyota builds houses in Japan.","quote":"Toyota manufactures houses in Japan","text":"Toyota manufactures houses in Japan [1]. If they sold them in the US, I'd buy one in a heartbeat. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hpAMbpQ8J7g",1],["aT7mnSstKGs",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1]]},"OX31kZbAXsA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as informal evidence that higher refresh rates measurably help both pro and casual players, even though it is more entertainment than rigorous science.","q":{"x":"LTT also tested this for FPS games and both professionals and casual gamers improved significantly going from 60HZ to 144+.","i":34633323,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":27169271,"author":"akyu","month":"2021-05","story":"Google Says Everything at Stadia Is Fine, as the Water Reaches Their Noses","stance":"rec","why":"Informal experiment showing high refresh rates help competitive FPS.","quote":"This informal experiment is quite convincing","text":"This could work for casual FPS games, but for competitive FPS gaming like Counter-Strike or Valorant, 20ms is a deal breaker for players who want to perform at a high level. Pro FPS players consistently choose monitors with the highest refresh rates available. This informal experiment is quite convincing Does High FPS make you a better gamer?: [VIDEO] A 60hz refresh rate corresponds to 16.67 milliseconds frame time, and a 240hz rate corresponds to 4.2 milliseconds. The issue here is not reaction time, but \"feel\". Even a moderately skilled player can test this for themselves by switching between refresh rates. Specifically the \"feel\" is the relationship between moving the mouse and the shift of perspective on the screen. Low refresh rates cause players to over correct their aim as the gaps between frames are larger, and the motion on the screen is less smooth."},{"id":44482370,"author":"haiku2077","month":"2025-07","story":"Toys/Lag: Jerk Monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Casual players performed more consistently at 240Hz than 144Hz.","quote":"they found that even casual players were performing measurably more consistently at 240Hz than even 144Hz.","text":"> I can buy that humans can see at least 120hz at a minimum. 60Hz is the generally accepted threshold, but I’ve long suspected that 120Hz has mostly imperceptible effects that are still noticeable, if rarely. There are videos on youtube showing people perceive differences at much higher framerates. e.g. [VIDEO] (long video, so you can skip to the end - they found that even casual players were performing measurably more consistently at 240Hz than even 144Hz.) Anecdotally, I recently switched to playing racing games at 165FPS and the difference is massive!"},{"id":40555488,"author":"Mathnerd314","month":"2024-06","story":"Does anyone need a 1k Hz gaming display?","stance":"reference","why":"240Hz significantly better than 144Hz per LTT.","quote":"The results I remember are that 240hz was significantly better than 144hz,","text":"Ltt has done some videos, probably there are other channels but I haven't looked. The results I remember are that 240hz was significantly better than 144hz, [VIDEO] but past that fps is sort of meaningless as the response time is slower than the refresh rate and you get artifacts. [LINK]"},{"id":40414650,"author":"lylejantzi3rd","month":"2024-05","story":"4K 1000Hz Monitor Exhibited","stance":"reference","why":"LTT results on 60/144/240Hz benefits.","quote":"According to LTT, that's somewhere between 240hz and 500hz.","text":"According to LTT, that's somewhere between 240hz and 500hz. Does High FPS make you a better gamer? Ft. Shroud (60hz vs 144hz vs 240hz): [VIDEO] You don't deserve this monitor - does 360hz gaming make a difference?: [LINK] I THOUGHT I Wanted This - AW2524H 500Hz Review: [LINK]"},{"id":34633323,"author":"zerd","month":"2023-02","story":"Play Counter Strike 1.6, with full multiplayer, in the browser","stance":"reference","why":"LTT test: pros and casuals improved from 60Hz to 144+.","quote":"LTT also tested this for FPS games and both professionals and casual gamers improved significantly going from 60HZ to 144+.","text":"LTT also tested this for FPS games and both professionals and casual gamers improved significantly going from 60HZ to 144+. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34633634,"author":"enragedcacti","month":"2023-02","story":"Play Counter Strike 1.6, with full multiplayer, in the browser","stance":"reference","why":"Test showing lower render latency helps even at 60Hz.","quote":"LTT did a series of tests with pro players in CS:GO focused on monitor refresh rate","text":">Worst case we're talking about an additional input latency due to vsync of maybe 1/60s? 16ms? its generally closer to 2 frames with V-Sync [1][2] > I don't believe 16ms is significant for non pro level eSports players and even skeptical it's a big factor at that level. It actually is fairly significant. LTT did a series of tests with pro players in CS:GO focused on monitor refresh rate, but one test they did was 60hz/60fps vs 60hz/300fps and found that reducing the render latency drastically improved performance despite the display still being locked to 60hz. [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":32913879,"author":"madamelic","month":"2022-09","story":"GeForce RTX 40 Series","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as support for competitive players lowering settings.","quote":"","text":"> you're moving around too fast to really appreciate (or even notice) the difference RT gives you over fake reflections. You're also at a disadvantage running the graphics on High. In any game that I want to play at a \"competitive\" level, I always put the settings to the absolute lowest settings so the computer always gets 100+ FPS. No reason to even try to optimize. The point is winning, not it looking good. In fact, in some games, the developers have had to go back and make it more fair for higher settings because of low settings hiding tall grass. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30494981,"author":"faeriechangling","month":"2022-02","story":"Moving away from Windows – It begins","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that refresh rate benefits competitive gamers.","quote":"See this","text":"Well the benefit is most obvious for a competitive video gamer. Lets say they're having a sniper battle in a video game, the first to click will kill the other player. Somebody with a 60hz monitor might have to wait 17ms for the enemies image to render on screen so they can react. Somebody with a 240hz monitor might only have to wait 4ms. When reaction times in a competitive game are around 150 to 200ms that's a pretty large swing. See this: [VIDEO] This article is also interesting: [LINK] . Beyond that, I think latency has a significant performance impact on many human tasks, I know that humans in videoconferencing can detect extremely small differences in latency. Higher refresh rate I think outside of latency benefits does allow some things to be more easily perceived like text in motion which normally blurs."},{"id":25558980,"author":"smnscu","month":"2020-12","story":"Raytracing won't simplify AAA real-time rendering","stance":"reference","why":"LTT tests on high FPS, more entertainment than science.","quote":"I believe LTT performed a few tests like on this topic","text":"While they are more entertainment than science, I believe LTT performed a few tests like on this topic: - [LINK] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":25049005,"author":"Alupis","month":"2020-11","story":"Valve Is Working on Another Extension to Help in Direct3D-over-Vulkan","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for 240Hz being hard to distinguish.","quote":"","text":"> Most professional CS is played at 4:3 stretched so the PC is pulling in hundreds of FPS That's unlikely to be true anymore. Even a modestly modern GPU can produce 100's of FPS in CS:GO at 1920x1080 or higher resolutions. Benchmarks for the 3080 were showing 400-500 FPS in CS:GO at 1440p resolutions. After maybe double your screen refresh rate, all excess FPS don't matter and are effectively wasted. Pro's mostly use 144Hz monitors because 240Hz is indistinguishable[1] - making anything above 288FPS basically a waste (and it's debatable if anything above 144FPS with FreeSync enabled is a waste too). [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"Q2CCs-x9q9U":{"wy":"Cited in threads on American decline, civil unrest and economic crisis as a classic statement of the imperial-decay thesis.","q":{"x":"Here's an insightful prognostication by the late, great Chalmers Johnson around 2005","i":24567977,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24567977,"author":"hexbinencoded","month":"2020-09","story":"The N95 mask shortage America can't seem to fix","stance":"rec","why":"Chalmers Johnson's prognostication about American decline.","quote":"Here's an insightful prognostication by the late, great Chalmers Johnson around 2005","text":"Yep. It's a self-fulfilling prophecy. Actual exceptionalism ended coinciding with the end of WW2. It's America's slow and steady decline like Rome. 250 years / 10 generations is about all a civilization typically lasts. Apathy, corruption, incompetence, disorder, inequality, poverty, decay, tyrants, and ultimately: dissolution. Here's an insightful prognostication by the late, great Chalmers Johnson around 2005: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35366199,"author":"sacnoradhq","month":"2023-03","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Chalmers Johnson lecture on signs of societal decay.","quote":"Decline of Empires: The Signs of Decay\": Jan 22, 2008; Chalmers Johnson.","text":"\"Decline of Empires: The Signs of Decay\": Jan 22, 2008; Chalmers Johnson. [VIDEO] Apathy, learned helplessness, inaction, cult-like fervor, magical thinking, pan-colonial ambitions, and inability to fund or defend itself lead a society to ruin. The thing is that every 8-12 generations (250+-50 years), a civilization must rework or recommit itself or it slides into decline and decay. No rampant conservative moral panic crusade, violent upheaval, civil war, or totalitarian junta can rebuild what divides left or right, or rebalances fewer haves and many more have nots."},{"id":32672364,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2022-09","story":"Charlie Munger Predicts Horrible Economic Crisis Where Everything Will Collapse","stance":"reference","why":"Extends Johnson's ideas into macroeconomics and current events.","quote":"This is partly a redux of the ideas of Chalmers Johnson","text":"The title of this piece is clickbait and inaccurate compared to the content. And low quality media source. The speaker is credible (being CM, but it assumes it's an entirely authentic video). This is partly a redux of the ideas of Chalmers Johnson but extends farther into macro economics, history, and current events. [VIDEO] It's also doom prediction, in the spirt of people like Thom Hartmann."},{"id":23364780,"author":"inscartwheelies","month":"2020-05","story":"Gripped by disease, unemployment and police outrage, America plunges into crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Chalmers Johnson video on decline.","quote":"DECLINE of EMPIRES: The Signs of Decay (2003) video by Chalmers Johnson","text":"America was already in a long, slow decline for decades. America: The Farewell Tour book by Chris Hedges DECLINE of EMPIRES: The Signs of Decay (2003) video by Chalmers Johnson [VIDEO]"},{"id":22533128,"author":"forkexec","month":"2020-03","story":"Why Can't America Handle the Coronavirus Crisis?","stance":"reference","why":"Chalmers Johnson on signs of decay.","quote":"Chalmers Johnson's Decline of Empires: Signs of Decay","text":"The simplified answer: the US is a late-stage civilization in gradual decline since either post-WW2 or about 1960. The \"frog\" has been \"boiling\" slowly for a long time and is almost cooked. See also: - America: The Farewell Tour by Chris Hedges - Chalmers Johnson's Decline of Empires: Signs of Decay [VIDEO] - The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire by Gibbon - The Rise and Fall of the Great Empires by Taylor"},{"id":21351392,"author":"campfireveteran","month":"2019-10","story":"The decline in U.S. life expectancy is unlike anything seen in a century (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Chalmers Johnson on the decline of empires.","quote":"Chalmers Johnson and Chris Hedges concur.","text":"Exactly. Chalmers Johnson and Chris Hedges concur. America: the Farewall Tour - [LINK] DECLINE of EMPIRES: The Signs of Decay - [VIDEO] Today, in Paradise, the power was out for most of the day... like Mogadishu or Baghdad. America, like a slowly-boiled frog, has become a third-world country: - no universal healthcare - grotesque inequality: lower average pay, unlivable wages, more billionaires, millions of homeless. - wealth transfer from the poor to the rich through tariffs, fines, fees and other taxes - crumbling infrastructure - tens of thousands of structurally-deficient bridges and crossings - apathetic, uncaring police letting crime go unpunished and misery flourish Americans don't know how bad they have it... it's great for the very rich, but it's horrible for most people including the aspirational rich who will never be so and yet vote/work against their own interests and be cowardly traitors against We The People."},{"id":17411695,"author":"king_nothing","month":"2018-06","story":"31% of likely voters think US civil war likely within 5 years","stance":"reference","why":"Chalmers Johnson on signs of imperial decay.","quote":"Decline of Empires” Chalmers Johnson","text":"I thought this was a possibility, however there’s not much to fight for, except chaos, discontent and something new, so it’s more likely America/western civ would collapse within 0-30 years (collapse tends to precipitate quickly), if nothing major socio-politically is done to stabilize the ship. [LINK] “America is Done” Chris Hedges on C-Span [LINK] “Decline of Western Civilization” Peter Thiel [LINK] “Decline of Empires” Chalmers Johnson [VIDEO] ^- These aren’t exactly conspiracy theorist-centric nutters, but people known for being more right than most people v- Btw, just in-case anyone isn’t convinced yet corporate media will “save us” Phil Donahue on being booted from MSNBC [LINK] Jesse Ventura on govt abuse of secrecy and Wikileaks [LINK] Cenk Uygur on “mutual-separation” from MSNBC [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Thom Hartmann on an impeding collapse (wrong year, obviously) due to massive corruption and inequality [LINK] Amy Goodman arrested for doing her job [LINK] Assassinations of journalists continues to climb worldwide, and when it and censorship happens in your country, that’s when the real decline accelerates [LINK] ^- It’s hard to say when/how much will happen or how constructive the grassroot can be because most people tend to automatically dismiss these “dots” as “conspiracy theorist rhetoric” that “doesn’t affect them.” Occasionally possible: ”It is difficult to get a man to understand something, when his salary depends on his not understanding it.” - Upton Sinclair"}]},"U5kIdtMJGc8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a view of Simons as a humble, pragmatic person, with the caveat that the host's questions are weak.","q":{"x":"Full-length Numberphile interview","i":34759598,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":20297328,"author":"nikofeyn","month":"2019-06","story":"How to Understand the Universe When You’re Stuck Inside of It","stance":"rec","why":"Interview showing Simons' humility and funding of origins-of-life research","quote":"the TED interview ( ) is also good, despite the host's frequent vapid questions.","text":"that interview is really great. i have watched it a few times. i think it makes it clear why simons has been so successful. he doesn't seem to really second guess, just does things, delegates well, and despite being obviously brilliant, he seems to have a very practical and humble mindset. the TED interview ( [VIDEO] ) is also good, despite the host's frequent vapid questions. in that interview, simons elaborates on his funding of origins of life studies and efforts."},{"id":42187775,"author":"fuzztester","month":"2024-11","story":"Jim Simons: A short story of my life and mathematics (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Simons recounts being fired for opposing the Vietnam War","quote":"I also just started watching this TED video about Simons","text":"I also just started watching this TED video about Simons, in which, interestingly, near the start, he says he was fired by General Maxwell Taylor, when he was at the Institute for Defence Analysis, for writing an article opposing Taylor's views on the Vietnam War. Taylor was in favor of it, and Simons was against it, because he thought it was a \"stupid war\" (for the US to be engaged in, I guess). [VIDEO] [LINK] The Criticisms section of the Wikipedia article about Taylor is interesting."},{"id":34759598,"author":"bmitc","month":"2023-02","story":"A Jim Simons Market Mystery – Solved? Not:-)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Simons' humble, pragmatic demeanor in interview with Chris Anderson","quote":"Simons seems, to me, to be a rather pragmatic and humble person who doesn't particularly enjoy flattery.","text":"It's because James Simons is a serious and actually intelligent person with depth of knowledge, and thus, he is one that doesn't need to opine on random things. He also makes a lot of money in a very secretive way. I'm not sure why anyone expects him to expound in detail on how Renaissance Technologies does it. Simons also spends more money than some governments on very serious and advanced scientific research. I suppose he's rather busy with all his investments and philanthropy. Musk is busy selling himself and filling his unfillable ego. You can see in Simons' TED interview with Chris Anderson how he gets rather annoyed with Chris at times, particularly when Chris mentions Simons' \"amazing mind\" at around 8:30. Simons seems, to me, to be a rather pragmatic and humble person who doesn't particularly enjoy flattery. Chris even throws out a quote by Musk at the very end of the interview, fishing for some similar platitude from Simons, but Simons deftly avoids giving out such an empty quote. [VIDEO] Most interestingly, you can tell Simons' like of science and mathematics and alludes to his interest when he mentions his investment into the origins of life towards the end. There are two interesting talks or interviews I know of with Simons: * Full-length Numberphile interview: [LINK] * His lecture on his life given at MIT: [LINK] Simons actually attributes a lot of his success to luck."}],"r":[["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["ebeNeQFUMa0",1]]},"YAneTFiz5WU":{"wy":"Cited in AR versus AK reliability debates as evidence that the AR-15 handles mud better than its reputation suggests.","q":{"x":"the purported advantages of AK action over its competitors in this regard are rather drastically overstated in popular culture","i":42488883,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":24390149,"author":"donw","month":"2020-09","story":"The Last Gunsmiths of the Khyber Pass","stance":"rec","why":"Mud tests of AR versus AK platforms.","quote":"Both of those are worth watching","text":"Both \"Forgotten Weapons\" and \"In Range\" are fantastic. Forgotten Weapons introduced me to a lot of firearms history of which I was totally unaware. Things like the Liberator[1] and 2-Bore rifles[2], although with the latter, I'm going to assume that you're hunting smaller naval vessels and/or low-rise buildings. Or you're looking for the fastest possible way to bruise the hell out of your shoulder, and want to set fire to a small pile of money at the same time. In-Range -- which features both Ian from Forgotten Weapons, along with his co-host Karl -- does a lot of other interesting stuff. Tons of different shooting competitions, plus some informative Mythbusters-style segments. My two favorites are the Shockwave vs Double-Barrel[3] and the mud tests of the AR[4] vs the AK[5] platform. Both of those are worth watching, but if you want the tl;dr: (1) A double-barrel shotgun is a poor home-defense weapon, but the Shockwave is even worse in pretty much all cases; and, (2) the AR is a more reliable platform than the AK in real-world conditions. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":42488883,"author":"int_19h","month":"2024-12","story":"More than 140 Kenya Facebook moderators sue after diagnoses of PTSD","stance":"reference","why":"Compares AK and AR-15 in lots of mud.","quote":"take a look at these two vids showing how AK vs AR-15 handle lots of mud","text":"It should be noted that the purported advantages of AK action over its competitors in this regard are rather drastically overstated in popular culture. E.g. take a look at these two vids showing how AK vs AR-15 handle lots of mud: [LINK] [VIDEO] As far as cleaning, AK, like many guns of that era, carries its own cleaning & maintenance toolkit inside the gun. Although it is a bit unusual in that regard in that this kit is, in fact, sufficient to remove any part of the gun that is not permanently attached. Which is to say, AK can be serviced in the field, without an armory, to a greater extent than most other options. But the main reason why it's so popular isn't so much because of any of that, but rather because it's very cheap to produce at scale , and China especially has been producing millions of AKs specifically to dump them in Africa, Middle East etc. But where large quantities of other firearms are available for whatever reason, you see them used just as much - e.g. Taliban has been rocking a lot of M4 and M16 since US left a lot of stocks behind."},{"id":35623678,"author":"rgoulter","month":"2023-04","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"AR-15 mud test following the AK47 test.","quote":"InRangeTV simulated whether a weapon will still work if it gets covered in mud.","text":"> Compare that to an ultra precise AR-15, if you get a little dirt in it, it begins to malfunction. That 'ultra-precise machining' does have some advantages, though: InRangeTV simulated whether a weapon will still work if it gets covered in mud. Originally they tried doing this buy having a guy crawl around in the mud; later, they realised they could just drop mud on the weapon. AKM [LINK] AR-15 (\"This AR15 mud test was performed right after last week's AK47 test: the same exact mud, the same wheelbarrow, at the same location, a mere 10 minutes later.\") [VIDEO] Incidentally, InRangeTV's videos have been used in both Russian & Ukranian propaganda [LINK]"},{"id":33401449,"author":"int_19h","month":"2022-10","story":"Why did the F-14 Tomcat retire decades before its peers? (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows modern M16 reliability versus AK.","quote":"But today? Watch","text":"What's \"fetish\" about the M16 series? It merely makes different compromises than AK. And some of them ended up working better long-term - e.g. the split receiver makes optics easier to mount, and the gas system and the charging handle placement make the receiver much better sealed against dirt and such. The difference in ruggedness and reliability between the two is also very overstated - much of it dates back to the original introduction of M16, and the various associated early problems (none of which were due to the design as such). But today? Watch: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32392906,"author":"qball","month":"2022-08","story":"The AK-47","stance":"reference","why":"Shows action works without a dust cover in mud.","quote":"","text":">You have to remember to close it after firing You actually don't ( [VIDEO] ). The trick is that the bolt carrier itself seals the action so mud doesn't get into it; most other modern rifles eschew the dust cover entirely and just rely on this. It seems to work pretty well since they keep doing it."},{"id":26309971,"author":"int_19h","month":"2021-03","story":"Before you buy a Soviet Camera","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Stoner design keeps dirt out.","quote":"","text":"M4 and M249 don't really have anything in common, and the latter is a Belgian (FN) design. On the other hand, RPK is really the same gun as AK in essence, just beefed up. But even when comparing M4/M16 vs AK, it depends. The Stoner design is more sensitive to dirt, but it's also much better at keeping it out: [LINK] [VIDEO] (Not coincidentally, the AK slit for the charging handle that opens up whenever the gun is off safe is not-so-affectionately referred to as \"slit for dirt\" in Russian Army speak.)"},{"id":19480335,"author":"int_19h","month":"2019-03","story":"M-16: A Bureaucratic Horror Story (1981)","stance":"reference","why":"Mud test of AR-15 versus AK.","quote":"","text":"AK had its own issues, especially with accurate full auto fire. Adoption of M16, on the other hand, was partially backed by Project SALVO concept of accurate burst being much deadlier than a single heavier round. Originally it was supposed to be about flechettes, but they couldn't make that work. On the other hand, AR-10 was already there with a design that made recoil mitigation a priority - straight stock in line with bore, lighter reciprocating parts, and recoil impulse directed along the bore axis with no lateral component like you get from a piston. So scaling it down to a smaller round made sense. And the design is not inherently less reliable - depending on the conditions, it can actually outpace AK. AK is more reliable in a sense that it can tolerate more obstructions in the action before it jams; but at the same time, loose tolerances and the design of its safety make it that much easier to get dirt in. So if you dump a lot of mud on both, AK fails first, even when you pit it against the original Vietnam-era M16 design - take a look: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["DX73uXs3xGU",1]]},"aoag03mSuXQ":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible, documentary-style account of the XZ backdoor in supply chain security discussions.","q":{"x":"which was just fascinating to watch/listen","i":47508120,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47508120,"author":"ajoy","month":"2026-03","story":"Tell HN: Litellm 1.82.7 and 1.82.8 on PyPI are compromised","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating documentary-style account of the XZ backdoor story.","quote":"which was just fascinating to watch/listen","text":"Reminded me of a similar story at openSSH, wonderfully documented in a \"Veritasium\" episode , which was just fascinating to watch/listen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47647183,"author":"rolandog","month":"2026-04","story":"Nvim-treesitter (13K+ Stars) is Archived","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how Jia Tan became maintainer of xz.","quote":"","text":"Agreed. However, I often wonder if people like that are deliberately (or inadvertently) being a psyop seeking to burn out people ala how \"Jia Tan\" tried to become maintainer of xz [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47231823,"author":"briansm","month":"2026-03","story":"The Xkcd thing, now interactive","stance":"reference","why":"Notes the xkcd comic is cited around the 9:50 mark.","quote":"(at about the 9:50 mark)","text":"Just to mention the original was cited in the most recent Veritasium video: \"The Internet Was Weeks Away From Disaster and No One Knew\" [VIDEO] (at about the 9:50 mark)"},{"id":47167160,"author":"nerevarthelame","month":"2026-02","story":"Story of XZ Backdoor [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Quoted passage on who might be behind the XZ backdoor.","quote":"Still no smoking gun, but possibly Russia.","text":"Still no smoking gun, but possibly Russia. From the video [VIDEO] : > A lot of the aliases, like Jia Tan, they sound like Asian names, and the published changes are all timestamped in UTC+8, Beijing time. So the signs point to China. And that's why it's probably not China. I mean, why would they make it that obvious? Every other part of the operation has been so meticulous, so cautious. > And they also worked on Chinese New Year, but not on Christmas. And over the years, there were nine changes that fall outside of the Beijing time into UTC+2, which is a time zone that includes Israel and parts of Western Russia. That's why some experts have speculated that this could be the work of APT29, a Russian-state-backed hacker group also known as Cozy Bear. But again, do we know? No, of course we don't know who it is, and we likely will never know."}]},"bvlzZnhZhrc":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete demonstration of how fake local business listings on Google Maps are used for scams.","q":{"x":"This video has a demo of what is happening","i":28188231,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28188231,"author":"gravypod","month":"2021-08","story":"Porn Spam on Google Maps","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates fake map listings used by locksmith scammers","quote":"This video has a demo of what is happening","text":"This video has a demo of what is happening: [VIDEO] Essentially if you make a bunch of fake listings all around the State you work in people who are desperate will search for \"X near me\" and if you have the most dots it will resolve to you. This means more panicked buyers which directly translates to way more money."},{"id":39023061,"author":"richbell","month":"2024-01","story":"A man's pay-to-use toilet gag showed Google Maps can be used to track people","stance":"reference","why":"Example of rampant fake local businesses","quote":"It's rampant. Locksmiths, appliance repairs, flowers, you name it.","text":"It's rampant. Locksmiths, appliance repairs, flowers, you name it. Unfortunately, I have not had similar luck in reporting obviously fake businesses. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34923083,"author":"acatton","month":"2023-02","story":"“find a way to be a predatory squatter on a chokepoint” – Cory Doctorow","stance":"reference","why":"CBC investigation of fake locksmith listings across Canada","quote":"CBC's Marketplace did an investigation about this years ago.","text":"CBC's Marketplace did an investigation about this years ago. [VIDEO] TL;DW: this is widespread in most cities in Canada. Scammers just setup fake businesses on google to have your nearest locksmith be some generic chain. I would expect this to be the same for the US."}],"r":[["e08kOj2kISU",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["-Qph8BNrnLY",1]]},"by1xpy8ob8E":{"wy":"Cited as a fun, well-explained account of objects surviving nuclear blasts, relevant to Orion-style and spacecraft survivability discussions.","q":{"x":"You might also be interested in the story of Lew Allen's Balls","i":48746739,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48746739,"author":"pfdietz","month":"2026-07","story":"More evidence of life on Mars but still no life","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the steel spheres suspended near the Teapot test","quote":"You might also be interested in the story of Lew Allen's Balls","text":"You might also be interested in the story of Lew Allen's Balls (which is much less prurient than the name might suggest). These were pumpkin-sized steel spheres, coated in a thin layer of graphite, that were suspended as close as 10 meters from one of the Teapot nuclear explosions (22 kT) in Nevada. The explosion vaporized about 1 mm of graphite but otherwise didn't damage the spheres much at all. [VIDEO] (starting around 5:50)."},{"id":42421420,"author":"credit_guy","month":"2024-12","story":"Antimatter production, storage, control, annihilation applications in propulsion","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Orion's tests of graphite-coated spheres near bombs","quote":"Scott Manley covered this in one of his youtube videos","text":"Medusa Drive is interesting, and so are lots of other space propulsion idea, like the nuclear light bulb [1], the direct fusion drive [2], the fission fragment rocket [3] or the pulse nuclear thermal rocket [4]. The difference is that Project Orion was actually funded, and some of the smartest nuclear scientists worked on it for a few years. Of course, they never built the actual rocket, but they carried out a tremendous amount of theoretical and experimental work. All other designs were only done on paper. For Project Orion, they put actual spheres coated in graphite next to nuclear bombs to see if they withstand the explosion, while being inside the fireball. They did, and Scott Manley covered this in one of his youtube videos [5] (see 9:30 where he mentions the Orion project). They got a little island from the Navy where they could carry explosive tests. They built a conventionally powered version of the rocket. They found out by accident that adding a bit of oil on the pusher plate reduces the ablation significantly. They did a lot of testing to see if the pusher plate can withstand the various stresses. When someone comes up with a new idea for interstellar travel, they spend a few days thinking of a few aspect of the design, and then they publish a paper or write a novel. For Project Orion, they hired Ted Taylor, one of the greatest minds in nuclear weapons design, and they also hired Freeman Dyson, one of the greatest minds in the world in general. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK"},{"id":25568227,"author":"KMag","month":"2020-12","story":"Japan developing wooden satellites to cut down on space junk","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a steel sphere surviving a nuclear test nearly unscathed","quote":"it survived barely scratched.","text":"To add to this, given what we're learn about nuclear weapons in school/media, you'd think a small fission device would convert the entire mass of the satellite into a ball of mixed plasma and vapour. A lot of the non-metallic mass of the satellite would probably vaporize, but any surviving bits would have a bunch of added kinetic energy. Especially for things that reflect both infrared and soft x-rays, with pretty high melting points and pretty high thermal masses, nuclear explosions do less damage than you'd think. There was a nuclear test where they put a steel sphere about the size of a bowling ball pretty close to a device similar to Gadget / Fat Man. Spoiler alert: it survived barely scratched.[0] You could probably design a satellite with thin enough pieces of metal and add carbon black / carbon fiber to any polymers too transparent to IR/soft x-rays. You could probably also use a thermonuclear device, but the particle steams caught in the earth's magnetic field would damage satellites, and there's also the EMP to worry about. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1]]},"cNjA0aee07k":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a teardown from a YouTuber who dissects appliances, showing live wires in contact with water.","q":{"x":"Big Clive also did a teardown and analysis of them, including the fault conditions that would make it dangerous","i":41694865,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41694865,"author":"sitharus","month":"2024-09","story":"Brazilian Electric \"Suicide\" Shower Heads [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Teardown and analysis of shower heads including dangerous fault conditions","quote":"Big Clive also did a teardown and analysis of them, including the fault conditions that would make it dangerous","text":"Big Clive also did a teardown and analysis of them, including the fault conditions that would make it dangerous [VIDEO] He also did one on a UK-approved unit for comparison [LINK] The units aren't dangerous if installed properly, but they're not many failures from delivering a nasty shock."},{"id":38697387,"author":"tkems","month":"2023-12","story":"My cat water fountain comes with a spicy USB power adapter","stance":"reference","why":"BigClive video showing live wires in contact with water in shower heads","quote":"I remember this video [1] from bigclive about it.","text":"I remember this video [1] from bigclive about it. I had no idea it was so common to have live wires in contact with water. At least they are grounded sometimes? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32871148,"author":"kwhitefoot","month":"2022-09","story":"CPSC warns consumers to stop using male-to-male extension cords sold on Amazon","stance":"reference","why":"BigClive teardown related to electrical safety of appliances","quote":"","text":"Electroboom did a video on the subject. It seems that they are actually safe. Not sure I'd be willing to try it though. [LINK] And bigClive [VIDEO] diodeGoneWild [LINK]"},{"id":20200442,"author":"madaxe_again","month":"2019-06","story":"All Of Argentina, Uruguay Without Electricity After Massive Outage","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of a heated shower head by a YouTuber who dismantles appliances","quote":"There’s a really great YouTuber who is forever tearing domestic appliances apart","text":"The difference is that in Scotland and Europe, it’s just one or two insulated heating elements that the water flows past. There are numerous safeties, overheat protectors, usually an RCD - in short, you’d be very unlucky to get a shock. In South America, I’ve seen two types. One: an element, but it’s uninsulated and has no safety features. This is usually operated by a water pressure switch and operates by heating a thin resistive element up. High danger of faults to earth (you’re earth). The other kind has me rather gobsmacked - the water is the element. The idea is to just pump in enough juice that the water starts to flash boil, and forms a plasma channel that then heats the surrounding water. Ostensibly safe, if earthed, but I really disliked the 60hz skin tingle - later discovered that they’d hooked earth up to the third phase in that house, when I plugged my laptop in. Metal case, leg, funky chicken. I guess the shower is reliant on skin effect to not kill you. There’s a really great YouTuber who is forever tearing domestic appliances apart - it’s actually quite amazing how some stuff is built, both in a good way and a bad way. Links to showers: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SYeeTvitvFU",1]]},"i_mLxyIXpSY":{"wy":"It gets mentioned as a thought-provoking video that changes how you look at everyday objects, though HN mentions are sparse.","q":{"x":"it's made me actually think about can openers a bit now. You might find it interesting, too.","i":30350432,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30350432,"author":"gatonegro","month":"2022-02","story":"Google Search Is Dying","stance":"rec","why":"Made commenter think about can openers; likely interesting.","quote":"it's made me actually think about can openers a bit now. You might find it interesting, too.","text":"Not really what you're asking for, but I saw this[0] a while back and it's made me actually think about can openers a bit now. You might find it interesting, too. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36658295,"author":"Arch-TK","month":"2023-07","story":"Amateurs obsess over tools, pros over mastery","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A master carpenter might be able to build a house with pointy shards of glass for nails, rotten wood for timber and pumice stones for hammers, but he's going to struggle every minute of the time, hate you for making him do it, probably decide to switch jobs half way through and the house is going to look like total shit (even if the only thing swapped out was the hammer this would likely become true). In my first job I had to deal with an absolute trash-heap of a proprietary programming language (Clarion, no, don't bother googling it, you will only find misleading marketing material and a bunch of people who only know Clarion, COBOL and FoxPro and will spend all day long telling you how Clarion is the best thing under the sun). Normally when dealing with a shitty tool, I will spend some time either replacing it or finding some way to modify it to make it less shitty, but in this case, there was nothing I could do but to master its endless quirks. I wrote anything I didn't absolutely have to write in Clarion secretly in other languages (usually Python) and presented it at the last minute when it was too late for anyone to say no to me doing it (yes it really is much easier to ask for forgiveness than permission). No, it wasn't the most sensible decision to use languages nobody else in the company knew to write important bits (although I tried to keep all my code as straightforward as possible), but at this point there was already another person in the company who had built up "},{"id":30151426,"author":"notriddle","month":"2022-01","story":"Adblocking people and non-adblocking people experience a different web","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for people not knowing what products they could look for.","quote":"","text":"I do think there's a reasonable argument for ads, since people don't always know what they could be looking for [1]. Independent magazines can serve a similar purpose, but the difference is between an organization that claims to be unaffiliated with the manufacturer, and needs to be constantly policed to make sure they aren't lying about it, and just being up-front about your affiliation, and only needing to be constantly policed to make sure you aren't lying about the product itself. Unfortunately, web ads suck. Because there isn't enough Gatekeeping(tm), I can't buy things from web ads, because the probability of it being a scam is too high. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"l0P4Cf0UCwU":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an authoritative-sounding critique of Wikipedia's bias, coming from one of its own founders.","q":{"x":"I suggest you watch this interview with Wikipedia co-founder Larry Sanger on why you shouldn't trust Wikipedia.","i":34835690,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34835690,"author":"Jfreegman","month":"2023-02","story":"The Political Bias of ChatGPT – Extended Analysis","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Wikipedia co-founder Larry Sanger on why not to trust Wikipedia.","quote":"I suggest you watch this interview with Wikipedia co-founder Larry Sanger on why you shouldn't trust Wikipedia.","text":"Wikipedia is anything but neutral on political topics. Their political bias comes straight from the top with corrupt administration and disproportionate application of their own rules and guidelines. I suggest you watch this interview with Wikipedia co-founder Larry Sanger on why you shouldn't trust Wikipedia. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28160396,"author":"sleavey","month":"2021-08","story":"Wikipedia Has a Language Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Wikipedia co-founder no longer trusts the English site","quote":"the co-founder of Wikipedia (the other one, not Jimmy Wales) no longer trusts the English site either.","text":"This article talks about non-English Wikipedia sites, but it's worth noting that the co-founder of Wikipedia (the other one, not Jimmy Wales) no longer trusts the English site either. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28020047,"author":"lez","month":"2021-07","story":"Ask HN: Jobs in Software/Technology Activism?","stance":"reference","why":"Wikipedia co-founder says it is no longer free and open","quote":"According to one of Wikipedia's founders, it's no longer a free and open platform.","text":"According to one of Wikipedia's founders, it's no longer a free and open platform. There are politically strategic topics where no arguments/edits are tolerated other than what fits the mainstream viewpoint. The owner of the question definitely wants to push things in a different direction than mainstream. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31034294,"author":"capdeck","month":"2022-04","story":"Moskva cruiser sank while being towed in a storm – Russian Defense Ministry","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"even its founder no longer trusts the website","text":"... and you turn to Wikipedia when even its founder no longer trusts the website: Wikipedia co-founder: I no longer trust the website I created - YouTube [VIDEO]"}]},"nmC0ygr08tE":{"wy":"Cited in pilot-wave theory threads as a vivid visualization of how quantum-like behavior can arise in a classical system.","q":{"x":"a nice visualization of the ideas behind the pilot wave/particle system","i":14964420,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14964420,"author":"appleflaxen","month":"2017-08","story":"Pilot-wave quantum theory gains experimental support","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes pilot wave and particle system.","quote":"a nice visualization of the ideas behind the pilot wave/particle system","text":"this isn't theoretical, but is a nice visualization of the ideas behind the pilot wave/particle system [VIDEO] the plot at 1:40 is where my mind was blown."},{"id":12998047,"author":"eln1","month":"2016-11","story":"EmDrive study officially published","stance":"reference","why":"Couder's droplet experiments show quantum-like phenomena classically.","quote":"","text":"As the main hypothesis in this paper is that the momentum is transferred by pilot waves, it is worth to emphasize the (cited there) most known recent approaches - Couder's experiments which use classical object with wave-particle duality (droplet coupled with waves it creates), getting e.g. interference in double-slit, orbit quantization and many other quantum-like phenomena: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11720159,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2016-05","story":"Pilot-Wave Theory Gains Experimental Support","stance":"reference","why":"Walking droplets pilot-wave dynamics lecture.","quote":"","text":"I'm just going to leave these links for those as curious as I was: [LINK] [LINK] [Video: The pilot-wave dynamics of walking droplets] [VIDEO]"}]},"oIkhgagvrjI":{"wy":"Linked whenever the 301 view freeze or view-count validation comes up, as the standard short explanation.","q":{"x":"here's a good video on why it happens","i":4952646,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4952646,"author":"citricsquid","month":"2012-12","story":"Gangnam Style hitting 1 billion YouTube views","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why YouTube view counts freeze at 301.","quote":"here's a good video on why it happens","text":"They cache view counts heavily on highly viewed videos for validating views, here's a good video on why it happens (with information from Youtube -- the 301 refers to the first point at which video views stop incrementing in real time): [VIDEO]"},{"id":45282202,"author":"meinersbur","month":"2025-09","story":"YouTube addresses lower view counts which seem to be caused by ad blockers","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why YouTube verifies views rather than counting instantly.","quote":"","text":"From the GitHub issue it becomes clear that blocking happens by the EasyPrivacy blocklist. The blocked URL youtube.com/api/stats/atr is/can also be used for tracking users, this is why some are arguing that it legitimately on that blocklist. The tracking not malicious. YouTube has a legitimate interest to verify views, e.g. to recommend popular videos to others. If a view counter was increased by just invoking an API, view counts could be manipulated easily. Also see the video [1] from ... 13 years ago ... so it might be slighly outdated. Just slightly. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39395538,"author":"cedilla","month":"2024-02","story":"YouTube Oddities","stance":"reference","why":"Numberphile explanation of 301 view freeze.","quote":"","text":"You probably think of Numberphile explanation of why YouTube videos (used to) stay at 301 views for a while - leading to lots of confusion when the site showed 301 views with 5728 comments and 37592 likes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38273786,"author":"u2077","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Why do so many YouTube videos have exactly 54 upvotes?","stance":"reference","why":"Famous 301 view count freeze explained.","quote":"","text":"It may be related to the famous 301 view count where YouTube would stop updating the total pending further verification that the views are legitimate. See: [VIDEO] This behavior was later removed in 2015, but I wouldn’t be surprised if something similar is happening here."},{"id":5350711,"author":"vegardx","month":"2013-03","story":"Elon Musk’s SpaceX Is Testing Technology To Make Rockets Reusable","stance":"reference","why":"Explains frozen view counts on popular videos.","quote":"","text":"There's a good reason for it, and usually is a sign that the video is immensely popular. You can find out why on Youtube it self: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22815520,"author":"flingo","month":"2020-04","story":"This Video Has X Views","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Or, someone at youtube saw the video and thought it was cool. Like how this video will always have 301 views: [VIDEO]"}]},"uRGljemfwUE":{"wy":"HN treats it as a beloved piece of tech-workplace humor, quoted whenever an outage or sales-versus-engineering friction comes up.","q":{"x":"Something along similar lines that I really enjoyed was the \"Website is Down\" series of videos","i":21904993,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21904993,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2019-12","story":"The Bastard Operator From Hell (1999)","stance":"rec","why":"Series of videos on website outages and sales versus web.","quote":"Something along similar lines that I really enjoyed was the \"Website is Down\" series of videos","text":"Something along similar lines that I really enjoyed was the \"Website is Down\" series of videos, starting with \"Sales Guy vs Web Dude\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":39607121,"author":"samtheprogram","month":"2024-03","story":"Meta outage","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Have you never seen The Website Is Down?","text":"> do you really think there are masses of people who can’t tell the difference between a single sign on service being down and individual sites being down and reporting it to downdetector? Have you never seen The Website Is Down? [VIDEO] The answer is: way more people than a software developer might think. Ask anyone in IT, or go to anywhere bugs are reported and read a handful."},{"id":36541465,"author":"Der_Einzige","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: How much do you work, what's your salary and how experienced are you?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I was literally motivated to get into tech by the old \"The Website is Down\" videos","text":"Yeah, but how much of that time is actually butt in seat coding vs hardly paying attention in meetings? I've worked at multiple big firms and startups and I don't believe that even the majority of people are working \"40 hours\" in the same way that most people work 40 hours. This is the wonderful thing about technology. I was literally motivated to get into tech by the old \"The Website is Down\" videos, and in practice, it seems to be how many tech workers operate: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33094728,"author":"richbell","month":"2022-10","story":"How I learned about corporate firewalls","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A tale as old as time","text":"> We run a Saas and someone wrote an email saying that our server was down, and when we'd expect it to be up. Not having had a notification, I double checked from a couple of geographic locations that our application was indeed up and responding. A tale as old as time: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["GoW8Tf7hTGA",1],["S89BHnaxULo",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1]]},"xzjqjU2FOwA":{"wy":"Pointed to as a good explanation of preference falsification and its effects on speech and politics.","q":{"x":"This is a good video on it","i":21157507,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21157507,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-10","story":"Why do companies with unbounded resources still have terrible moderation?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains preference falsification effects of speech enforcement","quote":"This is a good video on it","text":"At this point I think most people are concerned about hate speech policies. Normal opinions are censored through these rules, and the claims of harm are rather dubious. Why should companies be trusted to enforce elusive controls on our speech? And is it fair that tech companies act like editors and still get Section 230 liability protections as a tech platform that no editor gets? The end result of enforcing political correctness is preference falsification, which have lots of very negative effects. This is a good video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43578073,"author":"wesapien","month":"2025-04","story":"Why I don't discuss politics with friends","stance":"reference","why":"Kuran on preference falsification and Soviet collapse","quote":"Timur Kuran writes about this idea","text":"Timur Kuran writes about this idea (preference falsification) in \"Private Truths, Public Lies\". He explains that its this idea that had kept the Soviet Union (this bad idea/system) from dying sooner. People thought it was the system of oppression but in reality it was people who hated the system showed approval. [VIDEO] \"Accordingly, for all the hardships of life under communism, they remained politically submissive for years on end.\" [LINK]"},{"id":21080973,"author":"adolph","month":"2019-09","story":"Public Opinion in Authoritarian States","stance":"reference","why":"Timur Kuran on preference falsification","quote":"A similar idea called","text":"A similar idea called \"preference falsification\" is put forth by Timur Kuran. See Episode 4 of the postcast \"The Portal\" [VIDEO]"}]},"2Ntp6BqhSng":{"wy":"Linked in microplastics and recycling threads as a debunking of a viral statistic.","q":{"x":"Here is a 4 minute video explaining how this bit of misinformation came to be spread across the internet.","i":32296887,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32894022,"author":"peteforde","month":"2022-09","story":"A remarkable kid has died in Newcastle, Utah","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I consider this rebuttal to be exceptionally well-argued, and I thank you for it. You do raise several excellent points, and I have no interest in being a shill for the LDS church. And yes, Bari Weiss should be considered a red flag. My bad for letting her name slide past without a sniff test. I stand by my point that we shouldn't be so damn skeptical that there are exceptional young people, because I've known them personally and arguably flown in the same formations. (I'm no prodigy, but I was encouraged into tech and business very early. Timing and privilege factor in, too.) That said, I concede to your assertion that it's unacceptable for me to declare the statements in this piece to be \"verifiable facts\" at this time. As penance, I offer this gem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37856393,"author":"hackeman300","month":"2023-10","story":"Think that your plastic is being recycled? Think again","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Good video here explaining why it likely isn't true","text":"Good article, but unfortunately it propagates the oft quoted \"you eat a credit card worth of plastic each week\" myth. Good video here explaining why it likely isn't true [VIDEO]"},{"id":32296887,"author":"Zacru","month":"2022-07","story":"Review of 17α-ethynylestradiol in water across 32 countries: Estrogenic effects","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here is a 4 minute video explaining how this bit of misinformation came to be spread across the internet.","text":">We eat a credit card's worth of microplastic every week No, we don't. Here is a 4 minute video explaining how this bit of misinformation came to be spread across the internet. [VIDEO]"}]},"33QdTOyXz3w":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a source for what Trump actually said, and commenters argue over whether it was sarcasm or whether the headline was accurate.","m":[{"id":40982032,"author":"bamboozled","month":"2024-07","story":"Trump injured but ‘fine’ after attempted assassination at rally","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Trump's bleach and sunlight remark.","quote":"","text":"For example, in Covid he required the States to make their own rules, He also suggested injecting bleach and sunlight might wipe out COVID in the body [1]. Federal institutions and health organizations had to warn people not to drink bleach after that, oh man. In my opinion, any leader who actually cared about solving the problem would try understand basic things better or delegate to someone who does. If anything it proves he is an authoritarian. Do you think Kim Jong Un actually tries to solve any problem for his people, or just bullshits about it to stay in power / popular with those who matter? You might see it as some type of freedom preserving move to let states do whatever they want, but his actions were about being popular with conservatives. If he actually has his way and is completely unaccountable for anything he does, he wouldn't even done anything at all. A pandemic is a thing which absolutely requires a federal response, not a \"hey Texas you do this, and California, you do that\". Please do not let this guy become king of America, It was fine when he was on the apprentice. He should stay there. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29737670,"author":"CorrectHorseBat","month":"2021-12","story":"North Korea's Loch Ness Monster (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Trump's disinfectant remark.","quote":"","text":">You have many people who believe Trump told his supporters to drink bleach in other to fight coronavirus. Indeed, he merely suggested to inject bleach in order to fight the coronavirus [1]. Of course afterwards he said that was sarcastic, but even if that was true it's not really much better. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24414413,"author":"takeda","month":"2020-09","story":"Covid vaccine makers commit to not seek approval until complete Phase III trials","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence the remark was not sarcasm.","quote":"","text":"It's quite amazing how people twist to pretzels to explain some ridiculous statements by the president, because no way person that I support would be this stupid. Then you have people posting pictures of \"UV infusions\" and others saying it was sarcasm. Like if sarcasm in that context makes that statement any better. Besides, no matter how you slice this, that wasn't a sarcasm[1]. There is a reason why his daily briefings ended abruptly after that statement. I might disagree with Republican policies, but I can understand them, but we are currently are being led by a moron. People in his administration are putting guardrails to ensure what he is doing isn't completely insane, but as more and more of his administration is being replaced by \"yes\" men, then more and more damage he is capable of inflicting. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25757511,"author":"interestica","month":"2021-01","story":"U.S. to require negative Covid-19 tests for international air passengers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Trump did not recommend injecting bleach.","text":"> I think many people tuned out the moment he recommended injecting bleach - but there was still value in the briefings. It was something that I know a LOT of people actually paid attention to. This is an interesting comment re:communications and the messaging that reaches people. Trump did not recommend injecting bleach. He went on a rambling mostly incoherent recollection of a conversation he had where he was trying to learn what was being done. He was trying to sound smart. His comments were all questions to the med experts. \"Is there a way we can do something like that?\" \"Are we going to check on that?\" Yet everyone jumped on it saying he \"claims\" or \"recommended\". And it became \"truth\" because news articles covering the press conference used phrases like \"claims\" and \"recommended\". > \"So I asked Bill a question some of you are thinking of if you're into that world, which I find to be pretty interesting. So, supposing we hit the body with a tremendous, whether its ultraviolet or just very powerful light, and I think you said, that hasn't been checked but you're gonna test it. And then I said, supposing it brought the light inside the body, which you can either do either through the skin or some other way, and I think you said you're gonna test that too, sounds interesting. And I then I see the disinfectant, where it knocks it out in one minute, and is there a way you can do something like that by injection inside, or almost a cleaning? Because you see it (COVID19) gets in"},{"id":24130408,"author":"gridlockd","month":"2020-08","story":"Mitochondria may hold keys to anxiety and mental health","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the title of this video is \"President Trump claims injecting people with disinfectant could treat coronavirus\".","text":"> Didn't Trump recommended people drink or inject bleach? No, he didn't, listen to what he actually said. [VIDEO] It's not a claim, it's an arguably stupid question to some guy off-screen. Yet, the title of this video is \"President Trump claims injecting people with disinfectant could treat coronavirus\". The outlet is the presumably reputable \"Telegraph\". Trump also never called Nazis \"fine people\". What actually was said: \"...you had very fine people on both sides...\" [LINK] \"And you had people - and I’m not talking about the neo-Nazis and the white nationalists - because they should be condemned totally.\" [LINK] The job of the media is to fabricate outrage and scandals, because that's how you get attention. Trump doesn't even mind, in fact he is taking advantage of it: [LINK] Remember when he \"accidentally\" mistook WWI for WWII in some press conference? The headline: \"Another dumb thing that Trump said!\" However, it's a mistake that anybody - particularly average Joe - could've made. It's not really that negative, in fact it could be endearing to some people. More importantly though, that headline is going to take attention away from actually negative news, such as \"over a thousand American Coronavirus deaths occuring every day\". So, if you think Trump is really that stupid, maybe you're not really that smart."}]},"3oGFogwcx-E":{"wy":"HN cites it as an infamous example of terrible advertising, though a few mention how big a splash it made in 2009.","m":[{"id":21534733,"author":"spoppy","month":"2019-11","story":"Microsoft Comic Chat","stance":"reference","why":"Songsmith commercial made a splash in 2009.","quote":"made quite a splash at the time with their famous video commercial","text":"Do you mean SongSmith? That wasn't 1990s, more like 2009, but made quite a splash at the time with their famous video commercial. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":469864,"author":"pedalpete","month":"2009-02","story":"Microsoft Songsmith creates an interesting song from just a voice recording","stance":"reference","why":"Songsmith promo compared favorably to Microsoft's internal video.","quote":"This is a much better promotional tool for songsmith","text":"This is a much better promotional tool for songsmith than that 'internal' video microsoft put out a few weeks ago. [VIDEO] this is actually appearing to be a pretty amazing product."},{"id":23564515,"author":"skuthus","month":"2020-06","story":"YouTuber creates fake Nirvana song using artificial intelligence","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the introduction campaign for Songsmith is one of the worst things I've ever seen","text":"Also, to follow up, the introduction campaign for Songsmith is one of the worst things I've ever seen [VIDEO]"},{"id":5745892,"author":"k-mcgrady","month":"2013-05","story":"Xbox One Revealed","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The commercial for Songsmith was one of the worst things I'd ever seen","text":"The commercial for Songsmith was one of the worst things I'd ever seen: [VIDEO] I really wish your comment hadn't reminded me of it :)"},{"id":1727460,"author":"scrame","month":"2010-09","story":"New Google ad strategy: 10 minutes of a guy saying \"pizza\"","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Microsoft has a long history of terrible advertising.","text":"Microsoft has a long history of terrible advertising. Here's ballmer in a tacky suit selling windows 1.0: [LINK] Windows 386 (second half, bear with it): [LINK] Songsmith: [VIDEO] And of course, the absolute worst: [LINK]"}]},"70vYj1KPyT4":{"wy":"HN mentions it only critically, as a rebuttal to Hossenfelder when her credibility is questioned.","m":[{"id":45537755,"author":"pas","month":"2025-10","story":"New nanotherapy clears amyloid-β, reversing symptoms of Alzheimer's in mice","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"did she? or is she simply preaching to a certain choir that's awash with the heavy anti-establishment and anti-intellectual sentiment of nowadays? [LINK] [VIDEO] ... that said, of course there's some valid criticism of science funding underneath, and (the whole role, purpose, and status of) the academic sphere (with its traditions and privileges) which largely determines the outcome of how grant organizations end doing funding decisions."},{"id":43675507,"author":"refulgentis","month":"2025-04","story":"CERN releases report on the feasibility of a possible Future Circular Collider","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They linked out to this Professor Dave @ IIRC.","text":"I can't find the subthread, oddly. Really oddly... They linked out to this Professor Dave @ [VIDEO] IIRC. (1M views!) He's got a couple more on it. Note I'd never say she was a fraud but I also don't know what to do with someone who has grown into being Not Even Wrong regularly. This might be the only way, fire with fire, but it makes me squeamish."},{"id":43507043,"author":"cthalupa","month":"2025-03","story":"I genuinely don't understand why some people are still bullish about LLMs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"She's youtuber who is happy to take whatever position she thinks will get her the most views and ad revenue","text":"Sabine has moved well into the deep-end and has all sorts of bizarre anti-science content at this point. I had been getting more and more uncomfortable with some of her videos and couldn't really put it into words until one of the guys who blew up debunking flat earther stuff, professor dave, put out a few videos on what she's been doing. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] She's youtuber who is happy to take whatever position she thinks will get her the most views and ad revenue, all while crying woe is me about how the scientific establishment shuns her."}]},"8JUepj7wIl0":{"wy":"HN links it as an informed view from a working translator on using GPT for translation.","q":{"x":"Translator for 20 years talking about using GPT for Japanese-English.","i":37526091,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42896543,"author":"tkgally","month":"2025-02","story":"Notes on OpenAI o3-mini","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"General-purpose LLMs are best for translating straight expository prose without much technical or organization-specific vocabulary.","text":"As I explained in a sister comment, it is not possible to rate translation quality objectively, as opinions and positions about what constitutes a good translation vary. But in my tests of reasoning models since the release of o1-preview, they have not seemed as reliable as the straight nonreasoning versions of ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini. The translation process itself usually doesn’t seem to require the kind of multistep thinking those reasoning models can be good at. For more than a year, regular LLMs, when properly prompted, have been able to produce translations that would be indistinguishable from those of some professional translators for some types of translation. General-purpose LLMs are best for translating straight expository prose without much technical or organization-specific vocabulary. Results are mixed for texts containing slang, dialogue, poetry, archaic language, etc.—partly because people’s tastes differ for how such texts should be translated. Because most translators are freelancers, it’s hard to get a handle on what impact LLMs have been having on their workloads overall. I have heard reports from experienced translators who have seen work drop off precipitously and have had to change careers, while others report an increase in their workloads over the past two years. Many translation jobs involve confidential material, and some translators may be hanging onto their jobs because their clients or employers do not allow the use of cloud-based LLMs. That sa"},{"id":37839426,"author":"tkgally","month":"2023-10","story":"ElevenLabs Launches Voice Translation Tool to Break Down Language Barriers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the original, in which I speak English","text":"I tried it on a video I made a few months ago. Here’s the original, in which I speak English: [VIDEO] And here are the first two and a half minutes with me dubbed into Japanese: [LINK] Aside from some pacing problems, the voice and intonation sound quite good to me. But anyone listening to the translation alone would find it hard to follow, as the tool seems to have mistranscribed some of my English and mistranslated some of the rest. (I understand Japanese.) The dubbing would probably be a lot better if I had spoken English more slowly and with awareness that what I said would be interpreted into another language."},{"id":37845328,"author":"fennecfoxy","month":"2023-10","story":"ElevenLabs Launches Voice Translation Tool to Break Down Language Barriers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"sometimes you speak incredibly quickly, at such a pace that you even miss some syllables or blend them together","text":"I think it did pretty well. No offense as your original video is still really great for a native English speaker (me) to listen to, but the English speech varies in pace a lot, sometimes you speak incredibly quickly, at such a pace that you even miss some syllables or blend them together for example [VIDEO] it sounds like you said \"technogial\" or \"technodule\" rather than \"tech-no-log-ical\". So I can understand that a machine may make mistakes on this; don't worry, as a Kiwi I mumble and do this too ha ha. But the Japanese result is still already very very impressive even if I haven't spoken since I was 15 and over there on exchange (and stopped learning once I realise they romanise everything, why label a bottle with appuru jyusu when you have RINGO SHIRU!)"},{"id":37526091,"author":"og_kalu","month":"2023-09","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Professional translator's view of GPT for Japanese-English","quote":"Translator for 20 years talking about using GPT for Japanese-English.","text":">but nobody in the very well funded translation industry has a GPT powered translation engine for the key reason that it's not ready for production. Translator for 20 years talking about using GPT for Japanese-English. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"Bg3RAI8uyVw":{"wy":"HN people cite it as the origin of the vanishing-in-a-screenshot effect, used in ideas for captchas and AI-resistant text.","q":{"x":"I first saw this effect in a video from Branta Games.","i":45285240,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48870668,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2026-07","story":"A font that humans can read but AI cannot","stance":"reference","why":"Related work using noise and flicker to hide content.","quote":"","text":"Related work (all involve noise and flickering images, photosensitive eyes/brains beware): - \"This game disappears if you pause it\": [VIDEO] - \"Illusion: If You Pause, The Image Will Disappear\": [LINK]"},{"id":48873760,"author":"vunderba","month":"2026-07","story":"I created a font called Ghost Font that only humans can read","stance":"reference","why":"Popular video hiding shapes in high frequency noise.","quote":"There was a pretty popular video showing this exact concept","text":"There was a pretty popular video showing this exact concept (hiding shapes/text in high frequency noise ) about a year ago, for those interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45285240,"author":"Syntonicles","month":"2025-09","story":"Show HN: The text disappears when you screenshot it","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the effect where content vanishes when paused.","quote":"I first saw this effect in a video from Branta Games.","text":"I first saw this effect in a video from Branta Games. [VIDEO] The effect is disrupted by introducing rendering artifacts, by watching the video in 144p or in this case by zooming out. I'd love to know the name of this effect, so I can read more about the fMRI studies that make use of it. What I've found so far: Random Dot Kinematogram Perceptual Organization from Motion (video of Flounder camouflage) [LINK]"},{"id":43430396,"author":"Trung0246","month":"2025-03","story":"Anubis: Proof-of-work proxy to prevent AI crawlers","stance":"reference","why":"Hides shapes in animated noise, an idea for no-JS captcha.","quote":"which could cleverly hide captcha answer within animated noise mess.","text":"For no js solution, I think some sort of using optical illusion as captcha could works, especially [LINK] or something like [VIDEO] which could cleverly hide captcha answer within animated noise mess. However these methods are not really accessibility-friendly tho."}]},"KEkrWRHCDQU":{"wy":"Posted as a joke whenever HN threads touch on hacking or hackers.","m":[{"id":39517119,"author":"firebaze","month":"2024-02","story":"How to find the AWS account ID of any S3 bucket","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Quite a few people in this thread assume that the AWS key id is part of a \"security by obscurity\" \"protection in depth\". This will probably be downvoted, but if you read this anyway: this is a good example of why \"security by obscurity\" is not a good defense. You will overlook something (a determined attacker will not ) Anything non-\"security by obscurity\" does not depend on you understanding something or not - it will apply, no matter what, as long as the attacker hasn't a genius on payroll which cracks e.g. AES-256 just so ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":38549115,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2023-12","story":"How Hackerman would create an image just by typing 0 and 1 – a deep dive to GIF","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(my favorite part is when he goes into hardcore hacking mode while putting a Nintendo glove on)","text":"Wait, TFA doesn't even contain a link to this vid from Hackerman hacking time!? [VIDEO] (my favorite part is when he goes into hardcore hacking mode while putting a Nintendo glove on)"},{"id":16972800,"author":"haZard_OS","month":"2018-05","story":"Digital Vigilantes Who Hack Back","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Apparently, the hacker who posted this to HN is so good, he/she hacked into the future to bring it here. Cue the HackerMan intro: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9630027,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2015-05","story":"Kung Fury – a comedy funded through Kickstarter [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Did anyone else notice that Hackerman looks like the 1980's edition of Bill Joy? HACKERMAN'S HACKING TUTORIALS - How To Hack Time [VIDEO]"}]},"PH_Z8Ghuq6E":{"wy":"Cited as proof that a fit person can outrun a Tube train over short distances.","q":{"x":"A fit person can run faster than a Tube train moves over short distances.","i":10918190,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":16096398,"author":"BuildTheRobots","month":"2018-01","story":"Under River, Outside Time: The Woolwich Foot Tunnel Anomaly","stance":"reference","why":"Racing the Circle Line from Mansion House to Cannon Street.","quote":"Here's a guy racing the Circle Line from Mansion House to Cannon Street","text":"There's a handful of stations on the tube where above ground they're but a few minutes walk even though underground the train takes much longer (and I believe I much more circuitous path). IIRC you can actually jump out at the platform and run above ground to the next station before the train arrives. Edit: Here's a guy racing the Circle Line from Mansion House to Cannon Street [VIDEO]"},{"id":10918190,"author":"Silhouette","month":"2016-01","story":"Why London Underground stopped people walking up the escalators","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a runner outpacing a Tube train over short distances.","quote":"A fit person can run faster than a Tube train moves over short distances.","text":"Sorry, but I don't think some of your assumptions make much sense. Tube train tunnels are large. If we were developing some alternative method of transport, why would we reserve the entire cross section of current tunnels for single occupancy vehicles? As demonstrated by the Tube itself, much higher densities could be achieved, even with private vehicles, either by having multiple people per vehicle or by using smaller vehicles that didn't require as much space and could run alongside others. Of course density isn't the only thing that matters. Consider that the average speed of an Underground train is about 20mph. A fit person can run faster than a Tube train moves over short distances.[1] Clearly if you had a system where loading and unloading happened without blocking the main tunnels, significantly higher speeds could be achieved over the kinds of distances a typical passenger might be travelling. [1] No, really! [VIDEO]"},{"id":9666557,"author":"cxz","month":"2015-06","story":"Crossrail: The monster tunnelling under London streets","stance":"reference","why":"Runner beats a Tube train between stations.","quote":"Someone managed to run between them actually, leaving the train at Mansion House and catching up to it again at Cannon Street!","text":"Someone managed to run between them actually, leaving the train at Mansion House and catching up to it again at Cannon Street! [VIDEO]"}]},"PX9reO3QnUA":{"wy":"Mostly used as a cited example of Trump's behavior in political arguments, not recommended as something to watch.","m":[{"id":20273277,"author":"eesmith","month":"2019-06","story":"Ravelry, a Popular Knitting Site, Bans Pro-Trump Content","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He mocks the disabled.","text":"\"Undisputed\" is a needlessly strong statement, don't you think? I mean, do you agree that the Earth is not flat, and not younger than 20,000 years old? Yet there are many Flat-Earthers and Young Earth Creationists who dispute those facts. So, if I said \"minimum surface distances between different cities on the Earth can be approximated using spherical trigonometry\", would you then complain that I was stating that like an undisputed fact, and therefore incapable of considering an alternative worldview for the shape of the Earth? In which case, I am indeed ideologically enslaved to a non-flat model of the Earth. Aren't you? And, why do you say they don't make any arguments? They wrote: > Much of this policy was first written by a roleplaying game site, not unlike Ravelry but for RPGs, named RPG.net. We thank them for their thoughtful work. For citations/references, see this post on RPG.net: [LINK] That in turn lists these \"Policy Citations\" arguments: > 1. Racism and rhetorical alliance with white supremacist groups. This is a fairly thorough collection of citations in itself, not just a single tweet. [LINK] > 2. Hostility to transgender persons. [ [LINK] ] > 3. Attempting to ban transgender service members from the military [ [LINK] ] > 4. He is personally hostile to both individual reporters and the very idea of a free press. [ [LINK] ] > 5. He mocks the disabled. [ [VIDEO] ] > 6. He mocked a sexual assault victim, to applause from his supporters. [ [LINK] ] > 7. For the reco"},{"id":44178934,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: Options for One-Handed Typing","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Trump mocks reporter with disability","text":"Even though the parent comment is useless and off-topic, I am vouching and unflagging it, so my response is visible. Please give MyPasswordSucks a chance to respond and attempt to redeem himself by trying to post something useful and interesting instead of whining. (Although I don't expect he's capable of doing that, so I feel sorry for him, but he deserves a chance to try to do better, or prove he can't by not responding.) ̀However aaron695's sister comment is so comically wrong and off base that it's not worth vouching for, since it adds nothing to the discussion, and only goes to show what kind of a horrible person they are. It's amusing just how wrong they are, but insulting and offensive to most people, so please set showdead=true if you want to see it. We all know very well by now how Team MAGA sees empathy as a weakness, and has a sick fetish with mocking and abusing people with disabilities. Trump mocks reporter with disability: [VIDEO] >Donald Trump is under fire again, this time for mocking a New York Times reporter that suffers from a chronic condition. CNN's John Berman reports Here's a low-effort ChatGPT generated TL;DR summary, to satisfy MyPasswordSucks who had trouble reading my other post: Here’s the straight-up rundown of the Dasher thread: DonHopkins jumped in to rave about Dasher—calling it a miracle for people who can’t type. He dropped links to videos, papers, GitHub, and even shared his personal emails with Ada Majorek, who uses Dasher because ALS stole"},{"id":39850222,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-03","story":"Things I Learned from René Girard","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I agree that he could easily be functioning as a scapegoat mechanism, uniting people who believe \"anyone but\". As to being an actual scapegoat, I doubt it: scapegoats are innocent. He is accused of being a racist (Central Park 5) misogynist (\"Grab em\") bigot ( [VIDEO] ) We are apparently a long time removed from 'quoniam meos tam suspicione quam crimine iudico carere oportere' ."},{"id":11628472,"author":"humanrebar","month":"2016-05","story":"ROI on a college degree depends what you study, not where","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> They dislike him because they have different values than he holds Right. I believe in the Golden Rule and Trump thinks it's not a big deal. [VIDEO]"}]},"Qdplq4cj76I":{"wy":"Quoted in threads on engineering and product building to argue that execution matters more than ideas.","q":{"x":"Steve Jobs on how ideas change when you actually do it","i":32837569,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":40978861,"author":"dilippkumar","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: How can I find something worthwhile to do?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I have found that one common element in the things I want to build or fix is that I am opinionated about that thing. I am going to claim that having opinions is a prerequisite to being motivated to shape the world to suit my opinions a little better. If I think about everything that I don't have an opinion about, it's things that I don't understand very well. For example, car washes. I do not care what a car wash tunnel has, if it is brushless or not, if it uses some fancy shampoo or not - I have zero opinions. I can never find motivation to want to go redesign it, fix it or develop software for it. But something like Github's pull request review? Oh boy. It's absolutely insane that you can not comment on an unchanged line in a file you are reviewing. If someone changes a function call 3 out of 4 places, and you want to leave a comment on that unchanged line of code saying \"Hey, you forgot to change this too\" - guess what? You can't. In my opinion, this is insanely moronic. An opinion like this can easily motivate one to want to go fix a thing or build a thing. (I don't want to solve this problem for other reasons, but that's unrelated to this discussion). How do you form opinions? I don't know, but my guess is that listening to opinionated people with good taste really helps. Dan Norman's book \"The Design of everyday things\" [0] is one example of a very opinionated work. Bryan Cantrill of Oxide is also often very opinionated on system design, and his podcasts and talks are r"},{"id":40590066,"author":"cletus","month":"2024-06","story":"Boeing Starliner launches first crewed mission","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quote on idea people, cited as obligatory.","quote":"Obligatory Steve Jobs quotes on \"idea people","text":"I reject the notion that a good manager can manage anything. I also reject the financialization of modern companies where we put accountants in charge who aren't subject-matter experts whose only playbook is to cut costs and jack up prices. It's exactly what's wrong with Boeing today. Obligatory Steve Jobs quotes on \"idea people\" [1] and Xerox [2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":32837569,"author":"projectramo","month":"2022-09","story":"The B-52 was designed in a hotel room over one weekend","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Jobs on how ideas change when you actually build them.","quote":"Steve Jobs on how ideas change when you actually do it","text":"I have a lot to say about this sort of X was done in 5 minutes, but I am going to refer to the person who said it best: [VIDEO] (Steve Jobs on how ideas change when you actually do it)"}]},"QrrH2lcl9ew":{"wy":"Used as a source for Google's data that Rust matches Go productivity with fewer defects.","q":{"x":"found no measurable difference in productivity between teams using Rust vs Go","i":44685668,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44898001,"author":"noelwelsh","month":"2025-08","story":"OCaml as my primary language","stance":"reference","why":"Source for a remembered claim about porting projects.","quote":"I tracked it down","text":"I tracked it down: [VIDEO] I don't think it was on-device code, as they talked about porting Python projects. But you can watch the talk to see if I'm misremembering."},{"id":44685668,"author":"ralfj","month":"2025-07","story":"There is no memory safety without thread safety","stance":"reference","why":"Presents Google's study of Rust versus Go productivity.","quote":"found no measurable difference in productivity between teams using Rust vs Go","text":"See [VIDEO] for a a presentation of Google's study, which found no measurable difference in productivity between teams using Rust vs Go."},{"id":41761824,"author":"echelon","month":"2024-10","story":"Rust needs a web framework","stance":"reference","why":"Google data: Rust rewrites match Go productivity, fewer defects, easier review.","quote":"85% of people believe that their Rust code is more likely to be correct.","text":"[VIDEO] > \"We have at this point rewritten a large number of systems [..] have some very concrete things that we can say\" > \"When we've rewritten systems from Go into Rust, we've found that it takes about the same sized team about the same amount time to build it. [...] no loss of productivity when moving from Go to Rust.\" > \"We do see some benefits [...] we see reduced memory usage in the services that we've moved from Go. [...] We see a decreased defect rate over time in those services that have been rewritten in Rust\" > \"Within two months about a third of the folks were feeling as productive, within four months about 50%.\" > \"People do indeed feel as productive in Rust as they do in their original language. C++, Java, Python, Go.\" > \"One of the biggest latencies is code review time [...] How hard is it to review code in Rust? [...] A little over half said that Rust is easier to review. The most incredible question [...] The confidence that people have in the correctness of the Rust code that they're looking at. [...] 85% of people believe that their Rust code is more likely to be correct.\""}]},"Rr8ljRgcJNM":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as a pop-culture reference in discussions of American cultural influence, sometimes jokingly.","q":{"x":"Their point is that the USA _exports_ its culture to the rest of the world","i":26703293,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32705348,"author":"rendall","month":"2022-09","story":"IMDB deleted all negative user reviews for The Rings of Power","stance":"reference","why":"Europeans' view of American cultural dominance","quote":"Hey, this is a cool song.","text":"From Wikipedia: Collodi often used the Italian Tuscan dialect in his book. The name Pinocchio is a combination of the Italian words pino (pine), and occhio (eye); Pino is also an abbreviation of Giuseppino, the diminutive for Giuseppe (the Italian form of Joseph); one of the men who greatly influenced Collodi in his youth was Giuseppe Aiazzi, a prominent Italian manuscript specialist who supervised Collodi at the Libreria Piatti bookshop in Florence. Geppetto, the name of Pinocchio's creator and “father,” is the diminutive for Geppo, the Tuscan pronunciation of ceppo, meaning a log, stump, block, stock or stub. It seems to me there's a lot of cultural references here that are elided for non-Tuscan audiences. I imagine that could be frustrating. I think it would not be easy for Americans to be sympathetic to this kind of thing. Hey, this is a cool song. It's a bit how Europeans can feel about American hegemonic cultural dominance: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26703293,"author":"Scarblac","month":"2021-04","story":"Streaming subtitled box sets is the new Eurovision","stance":"reference","why":"Satirizes the USA exporting its culture worldwide","quote":"Their point is that the USA _exports_ its culture to the rest of the world","text":"> I think the whole success of the Hollywood is based on their ability to create films that cut across all the cultures. Good time for Rammstein's _Amerika_ video: [VIDEO] Their point is that the USA _exports_ its culture to the rest of the world, and Hollywood is one of the main ways in which it does that."},{"id":26142332,"author":"d26900","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: What are the best websites that the Anglosphere doesn't know about?","stance":"reference","why":"Song expressing weariness at Americanization of everything","quote":"This sentiment is also expressed in Ramstein's song \"Amerika\"[3].","text":"Authors addendum: This post was a mistake on my part. Please downvote this post. Thank you! I would rather have a retract option, but there is none here on HN. Original post: Apologies to you on behalf of people who made you feel uncomfortable. Full disclosure: I had (still have?) negative biases/sentiments/stereotypes towards Americans: being ignorant, self-absorbed, reckless, careless etc. And this almost made me have a knee-jerk reaction to the expressed desire to make the content in question available in English. As if I am tired of you Americans Americanizing everything you touch[2]. This sentiment is also expressed in Ramstein's song \"Amerika\"[3]. But that's why it is called a \"stereotype\". I shouldn't stereotype a population of +300 m out of a small sample size (that includes YouTube/Twitter too). I lose a lot of potential good/bad experiences if I continue to do that. And even if, for the sake of the argument, 90% match my silly stereotype, I still should have an optimistic modus operandi. Pessimism/cynicism is a counterproductive trait. You should be an optimist[1]. But putting all that aside, what makes you think that the inquirer is an American? He could be a French person knowing Japanese and Russian besides English. Anyhow, we shouldn't follow protectionist policies. Nothing good comes out of that. We should be thankful/appreciative to people who are willing to share content with others (open source, creative commons, etc.). References: [1] Algorithms to Live By "},{"id":23433754,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-06","story":"The case for declaring armed and fully autonomous drone swarms as WMD","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I doubt anyone contests that point, least of all Lindemann","text":"I doubt anyone contests that point, least of all Lindemann: [VIDEO] (I think we're heading for the classical Oceania/Eurasia/Eastasia split, but I could have read too many entirely fictional books in my formative years. Personally, I'd have preferred to have been in the timeline with centrifugal bumble-puppy and feelies)"},{"id":17873752,"author":"mirimir","month":"2018-08","story":"Germany, seeking independence from U.S., pushes cyber security research","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And see \"We're all living in America\" aka \"Amerika\" by Ramstein","text":"Germany was occupied for decades after WWII. However, it's hard to imagine that it's still under US occupation now. I mean, how could something like that remain secret? But on the other hand, how would I know? And see \"We're all living in America\" aka \"Amerika\" by Ramstein < [VIDEO] ."}]},"VADudzQGvU8":{"wy":"Mentioned in Emacs threads as a talk on long-term editor commitment and on Emacs's blocking limitations.","q":{"x":"Later I found a talk which expresses the same sentiment in it's title","i":24598627,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24598627,"author":"phaer","month":"2020-09","story":"Toward a “modern” Emacs","stance":"reference","why":"Talk title matches the long-term editor sentiment.","quote":"Later I found a talk which expresses the same sentiment in it's title","text":"> That's great and all, but people who are learning emacs for the first time don't know or care that it's one of the most successful text editors or that it's been around for 40 years. They care about being able to write code effectively. I started using Emacs ~2008 and it's longevity has been a major factor in my decision, as I thought it would likely be usable for the next 30 years as well and help me to avoid changing environments every few years with each new hype. Later I found a talk which expresses the same sentiment in it's title: \"The Editor of a Lifetime\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19295956,"author":"elviejo","month":"2019-03","story":"Polar as a Personal Knowledge Repository","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The editor of a lifetime","text":"\"Will... any other system be around in 30 years?\" Yes, Emacs. \"The editor of a lifetime\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":8779424,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2014-12","story":"Emacs as the Ultimate LaTeX Editor","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers Emacs blocking and lack of threading.","quote":"Perry Metzger talks a bit about the problems of emacs blocking starting at the 34'39\" mark","text":"Perry Metzger talks a bit about the problems of emacs blocking starting at the 34'39\" mark in his \"The Editor of a Lifetime\" talk[1] for the NYC Emacs Meetup. Not that his points are new, but this is just one of the first things that sprang to mind when I heard your \"it works fine\" claim. Here are some excerpts: \"Threading. This has been an irritant for me for years. Emacs blocks all windows when you're doing computation in any window. It's an operating system, but not enough of an operating system. I myself will run three instances of Emacs to get around this. I'm slurping in my email and I can't do something at the same time because it's off in space slurping in my email. It's going off and doing some extended computation and I can't go in another window and do anything else at the same time. ... If it's doing extended computation and a lot of file IO and talking to the network, it blocks you. There's not more than one thread there.\" There's also this[2] discussion of lack of threading in Emacs on the Emacs Wiki. [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"}]},"WV9J6sxCs5k":{"wy":"Not recommended as viewing; it is cited in Ukraine and geopolitics arguments as evidence of alleged US involvement in Ukraine's government.","m":[{"id":43220275,"author":"nthingtohide","month":"2025-03","story":"Intel delays $28B Ohio chip fabs to 2030","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a taste of alleged deep state activity.","quote":"","text":"Deep State is real though. Has been real for decades. Wikileaks proved it. Current events also. Here's a taste of what I mean [VIDEO]"},{"id":32025221,"author":"nkingsy","month":"2022-07","story":"Homeland security workers indicted in scheme to silence China critics in U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Leaked recording of Nuland dictating the next government.","quote":"Victoria Nuland dictating exactly who would be in and out of the next govt in a leaked state dept recording","text":"Debatable. The 2014 coup was about as on brand for US imperialism as you can get. Victoria Nuland dictating exactly who would be in and out of the next govt in a leaked state dept recording: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32172483,"author":"freeflight","month":"2022-07","story":"EU Digital Markets Act, aimed at Google, Apple, Amazon, approved","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the Fuck the EU US interference claim.","quote":"","text":"> I’m not sure they’re even able to without mass protests because the lng pipeline from the us will immediately dry up. The cold war has already gone hot thanks to \"Fuck the EU\" US interference in Ukraine [0] by now having escalated into a full blown open war, just like there is no \"LNG pipeline from the US\" to Europe. > not sure they’re even able to without mass protests Do you mean mass protests like they we had over the invasion of Iraq? That didn't lead to anything and thus has crippled the global peace movement to this day. That's why the idea that European people will \"take to the streets\" to protest for \"American gas\", is pretty out there and quite fantastical. Before that happens you will see people take to the streets to protest the sanctions that are mainly responsible for this economic hardship, sanctions pushed for by Washington. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31284275,"author":"freeflight","month":"2022-05","story":"Yandex data center in Finland loses power due to contract termination","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding Nuland discussing Ukraine's new government.","quote":"","text":"> And the situation in Ukraine was manufactured by Russia on purpose. That makes no sense at all [0], it wasn't Russian politicians that went to Euromaidan to rile the people there up how more negotiations would be \"Not enough\" [1], that were pretty much Western, and particularly US politicians and \"their people\" in Ukraine, like Arseniy Yatsenyuk [2]. Who Victoria Nuland called \"our man in Ukraine\" when discussing how to grandfather in his new Ukrainian government at the UN [3] Even Germany threw in their bid for new PM of Ukraine, with a German national [4], thus Klitschko didn't even qualify for that position without an Ukrainian citizenship. I guess they could have done it like with Natalie Jaresko, an American banker who ended up becoming the new Ukrainian minister of finance, got her Ukrainian citizenship the same day she was sworn in as minister [5]. This whole situation also lead to the Russian military having massive supply issues, as East Ukraine used to be rather integral manufacturing hub for Russian military arms [6]. Why would Russia want to \"manufacture\" losing all of that? > It's true that USA has done some terrible things in the past. And it's too bad the perpetrators never got punished. But it doesn't mean we should tolerate this behaviour elsewhere. The US still is doing these things, US troops still illegally occupy territories in places like Cuba and Syria. US drones and Special forces still kill people in at least half a dozen countries [7], with the hel"},{"id":31572300,"author":"OrvalWintermute","month":"2022-05","story":"NATO no longer bound by commitments to Russia, to beef up East Europe presence","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as US selecting post-coup leaders.","quote":"","text":"> Ukraine is not \"NATO controlled\", and has been refused entry into NATO. Nobody has suggested putting NATO missiles in Ukraine; that's all Putin's propaganda. Ukraine is actually controlled by the US State Department, and has been since the Maidan Coup overthrew a democratically elected leader. It was Washington & Brussels that issued an Ultimatum to Yanokovych in [LINK] that preceded the protests and led to the coup. It was US State Department leaders that selected, rather than elected, Ukraine's leaders post-coup [VIDEO] The US's own STRATFOR, Private CIA, even calls this a coup [LINK] It was the Oligarch Kolomoisky that bankrolled Zelensky's campaign, providing him protection, a bodyguard, vehicles, and other resources [LINK] However, it is also known that Burisma hired Hunter Biden, [LINK] and, Burisma is controlled by the Oligarch Kolomoisky [LINK] It was the US State Department that also sanctioned the Oligarch Kolomoisky for public corruption in 2021 [LINK]"},{"id":30975620,"author":"maratc","month":"2022-04","story":"In Africa, U.S.-Trained Militaries Are Ousting Civilian Governments in Coups","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Euromaidan was organized from the West.","quote":"","text":"> Nobody in the west organized Euromaidan and it was not a coup. You can listen to the \"nobody\" organizing the \"not a coup\" here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30570983,"author":"inter_netuser","month":"2022-03","story":"Putin says Western sanctions are akin to declaration of war","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the call about picking Ukraine's government.","quote":"","text":"How is the Ukranian Donbass situation any different from Kosovo/NATO bombings of entire Serbia? Where was everyone during the last 8 years during the Donbass war where Ukraine deployed battalions composed of neo-nazis against unarmed civilians? Further unapproved thoughts from approved sources: 1. US ambassador decided who they'll pick for the puppet during the 2014 coup, right on this call: [VIDEO] 2. Ukraine is the only country with an official neo-Nazi battalion: [LINK] 3. Ukraine parliament speaker was an open anti-semite and neo-nazi, and also served as minister of national security(!): [LINK] [LINK] ... 4. Ukraine commits assaults and arson against their rather sizable ethnic minorities, again, using their neo-nazi groups: [LINK] ... 5. Ukraine has neo-nazis at every level of their government: [LINK] 6. Ukrainians killing civilians for 8 years using the same neo-nazi battalions mentioned above: [LINK] Just as Ukraine refused humanitarian corridors during the last 8 years in Donbass, they are refusing them now to their own civilians to exit cities under siege. It's amazing how people think Ukraine is this shining beacon of freedom and Russia is this evil orc empire, when in fact, they both have the same transparency score, aka just as corrupt. Now tell me, how is recognizing independence of a province populated by a persecuted ethnic group, and then marshalling troops to protect it any different from NATO vs Kosovo? The minority is Russian, so I guess it's a safe pre-app"},{"id":13431670,"author":"ZoeZoeBee","month":"2017-01","story":"US Govt Data Shows Russia Used Outdated Ukrainian PHP Malware","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of US involvement in Ukraine.","quote":"","text":"Oh you're a fucking moron who can't read a thread. Listen to the hacked Nuland-Pyatt call [VIDEO] Ron Paul: U.S. State Dept. Conspired To Overthrow Ukraine [LINK] Ukraine’s Pres. Poroshenko Says Overthrow of Yanukovych Was a Coup [LINK]"}]},"XrlrbfGZo2k":{"wy":"HN cites it as the key account of train DRM being broken, in threads on right to repair, reverse engineering and vendor lockouts.","m":[{"id":45826807,"author":"lesuorac","month":"2025-11","story":"Norway reviews cybersecurity after remote-access feature found in Chinese buses","stance":"reference","why":"Hackers' account of locomotive software lockouts and servicing dispute","quote":"Poland put out a separate bid for manufacturing and servicing of their locomotives","text":"I'm not sure this comment does justice to the situation. Poland put out a separate bid for manufacturing and servicing of their locomotives and one company won the manufacturing bid while another won the servicing bid. The servicing company was unable to get the trains into working order and after hiring hackers accused the manufactoring company of bricking the software on purpose by including geo-fences where the trains would no longer work after arriving at the servicing company's property. Perhaps the interesting part to me was Dragon Sector's (the hackers) claims that the software needs to be blessed so although they discovered problems they never changed anything because they don't have the authority to bless it and heavily imply that the fact that the manufactoring company is changing the software at will is illegal. The changes by the manufactoring company had an (undisclosed) activation sequence added to it so you didn't need to modify the software in order to get the train working so the servicing company never actually modified the software. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":42685897,"author":"archy_","month":"2025-01","story":"We've Not Been Trained for This: Life After the Newag DRM Disclosure [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier talk covering the Newag train DRM hacking","quote":"","text":"The whole train DRM saga has been endlessly entertaining as someone looking in from afar, but I can only imagine the stress this must place on operators trying to keep trains maintained with this dagger dangling over their heads. I wish I could hack a train, thats a once in a lifetime opportunity and would make for an incredible \"2 truth and a lie\" type story Also available at [LINK] Previous coverage: \"37C3 - Breaking \"DRM\" in Polish trains\" [VIDEO] \"O trzech takich, co zhakowali prawdziwy pociąg – a nawet 30 pociągów\" [LINK] Mastodon post by q3k [LINK]"},{"id":41075224,"author":"golebiewsky","month":"2024-07","story":"Veles: Open-source tool for binary data analysis","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on Dragon Sector hackers breaking DRM in Polish trains","quote":"Some people that made this are part of DragonSector [1] polish hacking group that broke DRMs in trains quite recently [2].","text":"Oh, I know something about this. Some people that made this are part of DragonSector [1] polish hacking group that broke DRMs in trains quite recently [2]. Sadly I joined company after there were long gone. Really surprised seeing this posted here as it was pointed out, the repo is pretty old and archived. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}]},"Y2F8yisiS6E":{"wy":"Mostly referenced for its specific segments or criticized for misleading performance comparisons, not recommended broadly.","m":[{"id":43406512,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-03","story":"Jensen Huang – Nvidia GTC 2025 Keynote","stance":"reference","why":"Robot progress comparison between years","quote":"","text":"It's all getting better though, especially the robots seem to be coming along. Compare the Disney robots at the end of 2024 and 2025 2040: [VIDEO] 2025: [LINK]"},{"id":42495720,"author":"schaefer","month":"2024-12","story":"Infinigen: Infinite Photorealistic Worlds Using Procedural Generation","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote segment on omniverse, robotics and digital twins","quote":"omniverse, robotics, and digital twins are discussed at length","text":"If you look up the nvidia keynote from 2024, omniverse, robotics, and digital twins are discussed at length [1]. The relevant parts are near timestamp: 1 hour 34 minutes [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39750658,"author":"qwertox","month":"2024-03","story":"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang announces new AI chips: ‘We need bigger GPUs’","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the comparison graph [0] with 30x above Hopper was a bit misleading.","text":"What is FP4, 4 bit floating point? If so, the comparison graph [0] with 30x above Hopper was a bit misleading. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"_IqWal_EmBg":{"wy":"Cited as an audible example of what hypoxia sounds like and how consciousness fades gradually.","q":{"x":"listen to the incoherency of this professional pilot suffering effects of hypoxia at 32,000 feet","i":7424431,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":24502192,"author":"slavak","month":"2020-09","story":"American Airlines Bans Wearing Too Much Personal Protection Equipment on Board","stance":"reference","why":"Learjet crew hypoxia recording showing consciousness isn't lost instantly.","quote":"famous recording of a Learjet 25 crew suffering hypoxia at 32,000 feet.","text":"There's also this ( [VIDEO] ) famous recording of a Learjet 25 crew suffering hypoxia at 32,000 feet. I think it demonstrates quite well that you're _not_ going to lost consciousness within a few seconds at that altitude, although the danger is not to be understated."},{"id":7424431,"author":"lake_rogue","month":"2014-03","story":"Cops find five Indian Ocean practice runways in MH370 pilot’s simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot sounds incoherent from hypoxia at 32,000 feet.","quote":"listen to the incoherency of this professional pilot suffering effects of hypoxia at 32,000 feet","text":"The effects of hypoxia are profound on human performance -- it's not as simple as being unconscious and incapacitated, it can be irrational and incoherent. As an example, listen to the incoherency of this professional pilot suffering effects of hypoxia at 32,000 feet (flying a LearJet 25) -- he sounds falling over drunk, complaining about flight control problems, oblivious to the profound effects of hypoxia that he and his copilot are experiencing: [VIDEO] Then listen to him after being coached to descend to 11,000 feet when everything really is \"A-OK\". He sounds like a completely different person at 4:10. The co-pilot who sounds young and spritely at 4:00 (who me? unconscious, nah, just a micro-nap) had this written about him: \"the unconscious first officer's arm was moving violently and uncontrolled all the time kicking the controls and thus disengaging the autopilot\"). [1] While I find all the wild speculation fascinating, sadly I believe the highest probability explanation is still a fire or depressurization, resulting in a pilot acting incoherently. Of course, all stones should still be turned over in the search, but there's been no evidence so far that changes the scenario above from being the highest probability in my assessment. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":3266429,"author":"blhack","month":"2011-11","story":"The Hushed Dangers of Startup Depression","stance":"reference","why":"Audio of what hypoxia sounds like.","quote":"(This is what hypoxia sounds like, it's terrifying","text":"Thanks for saying this, Thomas... The worst part of depression is a sortof profound loneliness. This is the feeling that ends up leading a lot of people towards suicide. There's nobody that \"gets\" them, and nobody that they can talk to about what they're feeling. They feel isolated from their friends and family, and this feeling is exaggerated by people who don't understand that they are suffering from a neurochecmial/neurophysiological deficiency; not from something that they can just \"push through\". Imagine telling somebody with cancer that they just have to wake up one day and decide they don't have cancer anymore. What makes all of this worse is the feeling that what you're going through is somehow invalid. Not only are you suffering from the worst kind of emotional pain and loneliness imaginable, but you're being told that you're a fool for it, and that you're a failure or a weakling for not being able to snap out of it. That's dangerous . You know...a lot of my family members are pilots. Part of the training that they go through, something that seems to be constantly drilled into them, is to watch out for hypoxia. This is what happens when you're flying too high without supplemental oxygen, and your body starts malfunctioning. You sound like you're drunk. (This is what hypoxia sounds like, it's terrifying: [VIDEO] ). This is recognized as a dangerous part of the job, something to monitor yourself for. I think that hackers need to keep in mind that depression is kindof o"}]},"aXV-yaFmQNk":{"wy":"Cited as a humorous, somewhat contrived example of children and technology dependence.","m":[{"id":19475848,"author":"samcday","month":"2019-03","story":"Human Contact Is Now a Luxury Good","stance":"reference","why":"Contrived example of a child and an iPad.","quote":"Contrived example","text":"Contrived example: [VIDEO] I'm right with you. I grew up on the cusp of the internet's explosive growth. I got online every chance I could get, but until I was in my mid-teens our internet was dial-up and I wasn't allowed to monopolize the phone line. I still spent a lot of time on computers, but without the internet it was a very different dynamic, IMHO. You had to engage the critical-thinking bits of your brain a lot more, since answering questions didn't involve inputting those questions into Google and having StackOverflow/Wikipedia/random blog posts give you the answer sans any of the process/context required to arrive at that answer. I also spent more time interacting with things physically too. Lego (parents bought me a Mindstorm kit one christmas), physical books, even just tearing apart old computers and putting them back together."},{"id":4960171,"author":"AndrewNCarr","month":"2012-12","story":"The best interface is no interface","stance":"reference","why":"Humorous take on technology dependence.","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of this: [VIDEO] (A Magazine Is an iPad That Does Not Work) Not that I disagree, it is just fun to think about possible unintended consequences of dependency on AI for every day tasks. I am sure there are some short stories that deal with people in an advanced civilization losing their automation, but I can't seem to track any down on Google at the moment."},{"id":10772559,"author":"EGreg","month":"2015-12","story":"Disney is safeguarding its future by buying childhood, piece by piece","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"wind up like this kid","text":"I honestly think I'll raise my kids watching 80s and 90s cartoons and not show them iPads til they're 6 or so. I don't want them to wind up like this kid: [VIDEO]"}]},"bdwjcayPuag":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of programmable airburst ammunition used against drones and fast aircraft.","m":[{"id":45401617,"author":"Beijinger","month":"2025-09","story":"High-power microwave defeats drone swarm","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates AHEAD ammunition against drones, though costly.","quote":"You use fancy AHEAD ammunition that disintegrates, and hopefully the drone with it.","text":"? You would not be able to hit a drone with a bullet. Statistics are against you. You use fancy AHEAD ammunition that disintegrates, and hopefully the drone with it. [LINK] Yet, the bursts fired are expensive as fuck. Much more expensive than the drone. Here you go: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37568760,"author":"saiya-jin","month":"2023-09","story":"US Military Asks for Help Locating Missing F-35 Fighter Jet","stance":"reference","why":"Example of automated AA guns with programmable airburst rounds.","quote":"every round is primed to detonate at exact altitude/flight duration.","text":"There are some automated AA guns (Oerlikon or Rheinmetall IIRC) who lock with combination of radar and visual, or just one of those. No locking missiles, just good old ammunition and 21st century computing power. Well not precisely, every round is primed to detonate at exact altitude/flight duration. Put a hundred rounds in few seconds (so 5-10k projectiles) on the sky where the plane will be in 3 seconds, they will create basically impenetrable cloud and yes you can quite easily shoot down F35 flying during day flying low enough just by visual lock. One example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27444729,"author":"imtringued","month":"2021-06","story":"To protect against weaponized drones, we must understand their key strengths","stance":"reference","why":"Programmable airburst ammunition demo against fast targets.","quote":"Can your drone instantaneously change direction at the speed of an explosion?","text":"How do you \"dodge\" airburst ammunition? Can your drone instantaneously change direction at the speed of an explosion? What the hell am I reading here? [VIDEO] Edit: Look they even made a smaller system! [LINK]"}]},"eXWhbUUE4ko":{"wy":"Posted as a laugh-line parody whenever a thread features doom-laden arguments for regulation or restrictions.","m":[{"id":47252545,"author":"HanClinto","month":"2026-03","story":"New York could prohibit chatbot medical, legal, engineering advice","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's for your own good! Think of the children! You don't want puppies to DIE, do you? [0] [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":38509025,"author":"RecycledEle","month":"2023-12","story":"Taping Batteries Saves Lives","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant parody/commentary from Reason TV","text":"If you store batteries in metal containers, you can expect sparks. If you store your batteries in the same drawer as your bare wire, you can expect sparks. If those batteries are Lithium Ion type, they might smoke or even catch fire. The claim that taping AAA cells will save lives seems far fetched. Relevant parody/commentary from Reason TV: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28847680,"author":"perryizgr8","month":"2021-10","story":"Covid lesson: trust the public with hard truths","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Brilliantly parodied in this music video","text":"> \"people are dying\" is the favored rallying cry for any number of poorly considered, knee-jerk reactions to unfortunate events. Brilliantly parodied in this music video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25326900,"author":"just-juan-post","month":"2020-12","story":"She Couldn’t Open for Outdoor Dining. The Film Crew Next Door Could","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"We also need mandatory condoms because we know AIDS is a killer. We also need to ban all alcohol because we knows how it kills people both directly and indirectly. We know how guns kill, we need to ban them all. We know how knives kill we need to restrict their ownership. Trying to have a disucssion with folks like you is pointless because rather than striving for balance you only take into consideration one half. You are, as much as you may not enjoy it, just like an anti-abortionist or a religious person. No matter what point is brought up the responese is always the same: Babies are dying. It's happening because God willed it to. People are dying and we cannot let another one die. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24551487,"author":"just-juan-post","month":"2020-09","story":"Almost 90 percent of NYC bars and restaurants couldn’t pay August rent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Have a good laugh with me this Sunday night","text":"> This isn’t about people making decisions for themselves Making decisions for ones self is exactly the principal that the USA was founded on. > You might be fine, but if you are asymptomatic, you can directly harm or kill others and not even know it. See also: Drinking, condom-less sex, smoking, and junk food. All of these things directly or indirectly harm others and if this is about public safety then it's time yourself and others start movements to ban them. The \"you may be hurting people indirectly\" argument never worked and it's getting more brittle with time. Have a good laugh with me this Sunday night: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24551622,"author":"just-juan-post","month":"2020-09","story":"Almost 90 percent of NYC bars and restaurants couldn’t pay August rent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Have a laugh with me tonight","text":"> If people are responsible and won't go to restaurants See here's the thing: Just because you and others like you have a certain definition of \"responsible\" doesn't mean it should or does apply to everyone. Should we ban skydiving because it's irresponsible? Condom-less sex? This is about choice. Re-open and let people and businesses choose. Have a laugh with me tonight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24551660,"author":"just-juan-post","month":"2020-09","story":"Almost 90 percent of NYC bars and restaurants couldn’t pay August rent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Note that I'm a leftie and I love the video","text":"> The point of a lockdown is to stop people who think like you from hurting others. So is the point of banning alcohol, unprotected sex, smoking, and junk food. Drunk people can fight me and kill me while drunk driving. Unprotected sex may be more pleasurable but if we want to stop hurting others then condoms must be mandatory. Smoking is an easy one, I can smell that from like 30 feet away. It's clearly getting into my lungs. Ban it completely. It's been repeatedly shown that obesity is a social disease. What foods would be banned in your area of the country? I'm having a good Sunday, enjoy this with me: [VIDEO] Note that I'm a leftie and I love the video so I hope you and others can get over them poking fun at Elizabeth This is about choice. Stop the restrictions and let the people choose."}]},"gE8ZikfrpFU":{"wy":"HN cites it, with schadenfreude, as evidence that Apple Music on desktop is poorly built.","m":[{"id":42113308,"author":"freetonik","month":"2024-11","story":"I Don't Have Spotify","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple Music has been utterly awful on desktop for years, with virtually zero positive progress.","text":"Apple Music has been utterly awful on desktop for years, with virtually zero positive progress. I made this angry video [1] 4 years ago, and have tried using Apple Music multiple times since then, and it never failed to disappoint. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28484612,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2021-09","story":"Thoughts on Clojure UI framework","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it's broken beyond all recognition and hope with all interactions and expectations of a native app just gone.","text":"Like it's broken beyond all recognition and hope with all interactions and expectations of a native app just gone. See this review: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27979612,"author":"fluxem","month":"2021-07","story":"Apple Reports Third Quarter Results","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple Music seems to be a disaster","text":"\"unique style of content\" is a strange way to describe a lackluster library. It doesn't even seem to be improving. There is not a lot of new content compared to 6 month ago (when I first checked). Unless you're very into one of few shows, it's not worth it. And it's not me speaking. They offer 1 year free trial! Who does this? I have't tried other services, so I cannot comment. Although, Apple Music seems to be a disaster: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27267015,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2021-05","story":"How Intuitive Are Macs Really?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's Apple Music review (spoiler: swearing)","text":"In the past few years Apple has dropped the ball on HIG and MacOS. Here's AppStore mini-review: [LINK] \"Get 15 people, put them in 15 different offices with no communication between them, set them to work on one feature at a time. You will get something very similar to the App Store.\" Here's Apple Music review (spoiler: swearing): [VIDEO]"},{"id":26684445,"author":"CharlesW","month":"2021-04","story":"Why Software Jewels Are Rare (1996) [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Enjoy the schadenfreude of this recent Apple Music for macOS review","text":"Assuming you mean Apple Music (iTunes was an okay MP3 player/ripper, but is dead), I consider it the exception that proves the rule. Enjoy the schadenfreude of this recent Apple Music for macOS review: [VIDEO]"}]},"kfVsfOSbJY0":{"wy":"Brought up as a joke or pop-culture reference, such as bankers being sick of the song.","m":[{"id":38694790,"author":"mauvehaus","month":"2023-12","story":"The Birth of Rock 'N' Roll Is Found at Sam Phillips's Sun Records","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Some places will even write you a song and film a music video with their equipment","text":"Hell, they'll do all that and more! Some places will even write you a song and film a music video with their equipment: [VIDEO] Now if you'll excuse me, I need to find a bowl and some cereal."},{"id":35288091,"author":"paganel","month":"2023-03","story":"Deutsche Bank Shares Plunge in Renewed Bout of Stress","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm pretty sure bankers hate this song [1] by now.","text":"I'm pretty sure bankers hate this song [1] by now. On a more serious note, this was to be expected, I was basically asking the same thing on here a few days ago [2] and I'm not an institutional investor handling billions of dollars/euros in investments. I do wonder though when it will all stabilise? Will it stabilise? Is there anything stabile left to stabilise, so to speak? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":4692341,"author":"littlebopper","month":"2012-10","story":"Kim Dotcom","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It is an interesting combination of Rebecca Black","text":"Did you listen to Kim's music. It is an interesting combination of Rebecca Black [VIDEO] and Justin B. I like it. My life is precious. I get to choose who I am. Words to live by. I wonder why he isn't getting more attention for his music..."}]},"ohmajJTcpNk":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference point in deepfake and fake-news discussions, showing that convincing real-time video manipulation was already possible in 2016.","q":{"x":"The face2face[1] tech looks pretty convincing to me and it already works in real-time","i":13264962,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":27332087,"author":"0xx","month":"2021-05","story":"Create AI videos by simply typing in text","stance":"reference","why":"Founder's co-founder authored this seminal research","quote":"one of my co-founders is Prof Matthias Niessner who's been behind a large chunk of the seminal and widespread research in this space.","text":"Founder here Maybe – one of my co-founders is Prof Matthias Niessner who's been behind a large chunk of the seminal and widespread research in this space. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27333280,"author":"0xx","month":"2021-05","story":"Create AI videos by simply typing in text","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Founder here. AMA :) To answer a few recurring questions in the thread ---> Use case. Video is a way more effective way to communicate than text. Not for the HN crowd, but if you're a blue collar worker a 2 minute video in your native language is much preferred to a 5 page pdf for training. Anyone who has tried to record a simple corporate video know the pain of cameras, film crews, 25 takes to get one that works and post production. Cumbersome, slow and multidisciplinary. By the time the video is done the content is out of date. Synthetic video is not yet at the quality of real video. Eventually it will be. But the mistake many are making here is comparing it to real video; it should be compared with text. In X years we'll be able to make Hollywood films on a laptop without needing anything but time and imagination. Just like we can digitally compose music in Ableton, create images in Photoshop and type novels on keyboards rather than with pen and paper. My (obviously biased;)) belief is that synthetic media will eventually become foundational technology that will move media production from cameras/microphones to API's. We'll be able to do all kind of things we couldn't do before. Eg. personalized and interactive rich media, video-driven chatbots and eventually Hollywood blockbusters made by your favourite YouTuber from his or her bedroom. ---> Uncanny valley Simulating real video is incredibly hard. We're constantly improving and launching more expressive synthesis soon. Fr"},{"id":17711300,"author":"cwkoss","month":"2018-08","story":"Voting system to be used in West Virginia elections is vulnerable","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Face2Face would defeat this","text":"[VIDEO] Face2Face would defeat this: anyone with enough images of the victim (and copy of ID) could impersonate them in this scheme."},{"id":17358536,"author":"Nadya","month":"2018-06","story":"Keybase Exploding Messages","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Photo, audio, and video evidence should already be dismissed until one is able to verify the integrity and source. All of these can already be believably faked - it's just a matter of educating people that a layperson can easily create fake things by using tools developed by research teams. Fake text is the easiest to fake if you can identify the font used - any image editor will work. HN uses 9pt Verdana, even without using dev tools I could fake your post to say anything I wanted it to say since it would just be 9pt Verdana on a solid background set to text wrap every 1050px. See: [VIDEO] & [LINK]"},{"id":16693927,"author":"opportune","month":"2018-03","story":"Learning to write programs that generate images","stance":"reference","why":"Transfers a face to public figures' expressions in real time","quote":"researchers transferred someone's face in real time to facial expression and mouth movements of public figures","text":"Here's another scary GAN proof of concept [0]. In this case, researchers transferred someone's face in real time to facial expression and mouth movements of public figures. Combined with DeepMind's new tech that seems to be able to produce human voice with believable candor and inflection [1], you could make some very convincing fake footage. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":15423581,"author":"olegkikin","month":"2017-10","story":"A scientist who spots fake videos","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Creating a very realistic fake is now trivial","text":"We're already at the stage where one can't trust the video evidence, unless it's backed by the camera's signature/encryption. Creating a very realistic fake is now trivial: [VIDEO] [LINK] Google is very close to synthesizing realistic voice. It's game over, as far as I can see. It's a matter of time someone creates a fake video of someone famous saying something very outrageous, like nazi propaganda, and it will result in the destruction of that person's career and life. We really need something like Secure Enclave in every camera. EDIT: Another related video: [LINK]"},{"id":15194402,"author":"adbrebs","month":"2017-09","story":"Launch HN: Lyrebird (YC S17) – Create a digital copy of your voice","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Seems from right out of the gate, they are breaking their own ethical guidelines as a cheap promotional tactic. If they care that little about themselves and a former president of the United States, what do they care about your likeness. We state in our blogpost that we make an exception for Obama/Trump in order to raise public awareness. Both of them are regularly used in Machine Learning benchmarks (for example [0] [1]). Note that we don't allow users to generate from Trump/Obama's voice. Once again, we care a lot about these issues and that's why we only allow users to copy their own voice. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] These issues are challenging and suggestions about how you think the technology should be introduced/regulated are very welcome."},{"id":14682487,"author":"some1else","month":"2017-07","story":"Generating convincing audio and video of fake events","stance":"reference","why":"Research behind the fake-video concerns","quote":"This is an art project which highlights the implications of research like Face2Face","text":"This article title is a bit off. The audio is not generated, the video is not convincing, and the original conversation is not fake. This is an art project which highlights the implications of research like Face2Face: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14876979,"author":"icebraining","month":"2017-07","story":"Future of Fake News [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Real-time face capture and reenactment research","quote":"","text":"Face2Face: Real-time Face Capture and Reenactment of RGB Videos (CVPR 2016 Oral) [VIDEO] And I agree, I find this much more verosimil."},{"id":14183335,"author":"eadz","month":"2017-04","story":"Lyrebird – An API to copy the voice of anyone","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it is truly time to be very careful verifying videos or even live streams.","text":"Combined with Face2Face[1] live video impersonation, it is truly time to be very careful verifying videos or even live streams. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13760228,"author":"mbrookes","month":"2017-03","story":"Deep Voice: Real-Time Neural Text-To-Speech","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"Fake News\" is about to get a lot more compelling hen you can make anyone say anything as long as you have some previous recordings of their voice. Adobe has already developed that technology: [LINK] Now imagine combining it with this: Face2Face: Real-time Face Capture and Reenactment of RGB Videos [VIDEO] Perhaps using the intonation from the face-actor's voice to guide the speech synthesis."},{"id":13729358,"author":"omegaworks","month":"2017-02","story":"Combating “Fake News” with a Smartphone “Proof Mode”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Tech for faking video is getting more powerful day by day.","text":"I still think that it is incredibly important that we make tools for validation of primary source material easy to use and friendly for non-technical people. Tech for faking video is getting more powerful day by day. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13264962,"author":"rawnlq","month":"2016-12","story":"Carrie Fisher has died","stance":"reference","why":"Convincing real-time face reenactment","quote":"The face2face[1] tech looks pretty convincing to me and it already works in real-time","text":"Maybe CG will be good enough to keep casting dead actors? The face2face[1] tech looks pretty convincing to me and it already works in real-time (which isn't necessary for films) and you have plenty of old footage of them. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12676381,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2016-10","story":"Deepgram – Find Damning Soundbites","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"OK, throw this into the mix: many of you reading this have smartphones which are voice controlled, and for which voice control is activated at all times. In the case of Google, that processing must take place on Google's centralised servers. Siri may or may not do centralised processing (and can operate in standalone modes). Microsoft's Cortana, Facebook's \"M\" (IIRC) and Amazon's Aero are all various stylings of \"Stasi in a Glade form factor\", as Maciej Czeglowski so memorably put it.[1] Voice stores distressingly cheaply in terms of space, and with the Internet of (broken) Things (that spy on you), odds of finding yourself surrounded by microphones in the most unexpected locations,[2] controlled by a wide variety of quite probably competing interests.[3] And if they cannot find what they're looking for in the surveillance tape itself, they'll simply manufacture their own evidence using your own phonemes[4] and video.[5] ________________________________ Notes: 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK] 5. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12680371,"author":"RGamma","month":"2016-10","story":"Deepgram – Find Damning Soundbites","stance":"reference","why":"Transfers actor facial expressions onto politicians on TV","quote":"a live-editing software that can transfer facial expressions including lip movements from actors to e.g. politicians on TV","text":"It gets even better when combined with Face2Face, a live-editing software that can transfer facial expressions including lip movements from actors to e.g. politicians on TV: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12228675,"author":"jefe_","month":"2016-08","story":"Interactive Dynamic Video","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the propagandist use cases are terrifying.","text":"The consumer use cases are interesting but the propagandist use cases are terrifying. Along with this (Real-time Face Capture and Reenactment): [VIDEO]"},{"id":11850079,"author":"dgacmu","month":"2016-06","story":"Former NASA chief unveils $100M neural chip maker KnuEdge","stance":"reference","why":"Real-time face reenactment shows video synthesis progress","quote":"We're already getting pretty decent at this for video","text":"I find it amusing that they're touting it for an application where I find it quite likely that similar NN algorithms will excel at generating speech in someone else's voice. (c.f., \"neural style transfer\" in images, but applied to speech.) We're already getting pretty decent at this for video -- see, for example, [VIDEO] (Face2Face: Real-time Face Capture and Reenactment)"},{"id":11364149,"author":"sigmar","month":"2016-03","story":"Microsoft demos 'holoportation' 3D presence tech with HoloLens","stance":"reference","why":"Real-time face reenactment demo","quote":"","text":"Yea, perhaps. Like in the recent \"face2face: realtime face capture and re-enactment video\"[1]. and your reference to \"your avatar\" reminds me of the scene from the matrix where Morpheus describes \"residual self-image\"[2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13854115,"author":"teleclimber","month":"2017-03","story":"Online Demo of DeepWarp: Photorealistic Image Resynthesis for Gaze Manipulation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this technology enables nuclear-grade deception.","text":"While the technology is amazing, I am a bit bothered by all these picture and video modifying algorithms. The issue is that we can't know what's real any more. It used to be if you saw a video or a photo depicting an event you could be pretty sure that what you're looking at actually happened. Now, if you see a video of a prominent politician saying something awful in your twitter timeline (or whatever), they may have actually never said anything remotely close. It could be a completely fictional video that looks perfectly realistic[0], made by some teen in Macedonia.[1] I realize photography and video have always been used to trick people into thinking things that aren't true, but this technology enables nuclear-grade deception. I am wondering: is there a use-case for such an algorithm that is practical and good for the world? PS: I know an eye-rolling algo is quite innocuous but I've had this thought on my mind about these in general and needed to air it out. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}]},"ov5kvWSz5LM":{"wy":"Only cited as a source for conspiracy claims about a counter-coup, and one commenter flagged it as a precursor to QAnon narratives.","m":[{"id":21573907,"author":"alwaysanagenda","month":"2019-11","story":"Sweden Drops Julian Assange Rape Investigation","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Pieczenik as explaining Assange as part of a counter-coup.","quote":"Dr. Steve Pieczenik explains it all","text":"Good to see this voice among the masses who have a memory long enough to understand the big picture. I think Assange will be taken care of when all is said and done. This is from 2016, Dr. Steve Pieczenik explains it all -- Assange was part of a 'counter-coup' from within the US Government to take down the Clinton cabal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13547008,"author":"hellbanner","month":"2017-02","story":"Tech firms are departing from their see-no-evil stance on society and politics","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Pieczenik's claims about a counter coup as supporting evidence.","quote":"","text":"\"I must once again challenge readers of Hacker News to consider the possibility that the popular news feeds do not provide an accurate view of the global political situation. This is because information control is a critical aspect of warfare, and that comes in the form of propaganda. And, whether you believe it or not, the United States maintains its position in the world through deadly force that requires projection. This projection comes in the form of extensive, connected military campaigns that have been ongoing for decades. This warfare makes it important to shape information around it -- hence American propaganda.\" Yep. And let's not forget Steve Piecnizk \"The counter coup saying, we will convict and indite the president of the united states\" ... \"we are going to stop you from making hilary the president of the united states\" this guy was Psychological Warfare !!!!! for US State Department [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24657386,"author":"AndrewBissell","month":"2020-10","story":"America Is About to Enter Its Years of Lead","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"pushing a larval version of the QAnon \"deep state patriot\" narrative in the leadup to the 2016 election","text":"You may need to catch up on the news a bit, Steve Pieczenik of the U.S. State Department (common cover for CIA) admitted that he killed Aldo Moro to prevent him from divulging state secrets. [LINK] I would imagine some of those secrets may have had to do with other incidents where the Red Brigade and other left wing groups were framed in false flag incidents as part of the Gladio strategy of tension. It's maybe noteworthy that the same Steve Pieczenik was pushing a larval version of the QAnon \"deep state patriot\" narrative in the leadup to the 2016 election: [VIDEO]"}]},"r-V_Z3bD_PA":{"wy":"Linked whenever someone reports a monitor blacking out from static electricity, as prior art on the same problem.","m":[{"id":47782710,"author":"deckar01","month":"2026-04","story":"Fix monitor that goes black, off or blinks due to static electricity in chair","stance":"reference","why":"EEVblog investigation of a monitor blacking out from static","quote":"EEV blog has already investigated the same issue for the same device.","text":"It looks like the youtube embed is broken. It is supposed to link to a EEV blog video. It is wild how many times someone brings me broken equipment and it turns out EEV blog has already investigated the same issue for the same device. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35744922,"author":"grumbel","month":"2023-04","story":"Check if your IKEA chair is compatible with your screen","stance":"reference","why":"Older EEVblog video on monitors blacking out from static","quote":"There is an old EEVblog[1] video on the topic.","text":"There is an old EEVblog[1] video on the topic. Mine doesn't do it from chairs, but it regularly blacks out if I turn on the nearby LED strip. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18978236,"author":"dazzawazza","month":"2019-01","story":"Everytime Employee 2 walks by a cubicle, one of the screens blacks out","stance":"reference","why":"Dave covers static turning off a monitor","quote":"Dave covers \"static\" turning off a monitor in this video.","text":"I posted this to reddit but I'm more likely to get a reasonable discussion on here. [VIDEO] Dave covers \"static\" turning off a monitor in this video. Could it be this?"}]},"rDqQcmVqAm4":{"wy":"Cited for first-person Jobs remarks, such as nobody knowing the market in the early days, and for dating the footage.","m":[{"id":48004812,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-05","story":"I Recreated the Apple Lisa Computer Inside an FPGA – The LisaFPGA Project","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on nobody knowing the market at the time","quote":"No one knew the market at the time. From Jobs himself","text":">No one knew the market at the time. From Jobs himself: [VIDEO] $10k for a home computer enthusiast is still a big ask in modern markets. Have a great day. =3"},{"id":30613136,"author":"rvense","month":"2022-03","story":"Low process culture, high process culture","stance":"reference","why":"Dating the interview to the Triumph of the Nerds documentary","quote":"I think this is from an interview made for the documentary Triumph of the Nerds, so 1995.","text":"> no date I think this is from an interview made for the documentary Triumph of the Nerds, so 1995. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26585364,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2021-03","story":"Steve Jobs Interview in 1981 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here's another instance, refining the delivery of \"a bicycle for the human mind\": [LINK] . It is a \"performance art\", like a stand-up comic, an actor, a politician, a presentation, or a congressional hearing. Some people do not want to rehearse as they feel it \"empties them\". He was known to meticulously practice the delivery of keynotes so he could hit things on time, as if it were a play, if I recall correctly. You can see that there's a good part of storytelling. If you watch Elon Musk videos, there often is repetition on the three things he focused on in college: the internet, multi-planetary life, and sustainable energy. In more recent videos, the narrative has shifted to five things he focused on during college, and an addition of genetics and artificial intelligence made it to the list. This is a bit different, like a git history change, which goes unnoticed by most interviewers. However, people think about a lot of things and nobody would be able to list all their ideas or thoughts, let alone someone like him. One of the best interviewers in the space is Sarah Lacy, in my opinion. She has enough raport with her guests in Pando Monthly videos (there are bout fifty of them, and I invite everyone to watch them), but she doesn't hesitate to invite her guests to better recall the events, timeline, or context... \"Yes, but X had invested before and you had replaced so and so on the board at that time, so that must have been before Z\". Way better interviews because she has th"}]},"sZOuz_laH9I":{"wy":"Cited as a good explainer on EV charging, including the CCS standard with Tesla as the holdout.","m":[{"id":33565479,"author":"thevoiceless","month":"2022-11","story":"Opening the North American charging standard","stance":"reference","why":"Technology Connections explainer on EV charging.","quote":"obligatory Technology Connections video","text":"Tesla said in May that it would be adding CCS adapters to its Supercharger stations, already used in Europe: [LINK] Now they're trying to declare their own charger as a \"standard\", after launching a $250 adapter in September to use the actual standard plug? Also, obligatory Technology Connections video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32958225,"author":"meatmanek","month":"2022-09","story":"Charging cars at home at night is not the way to go: study","stance":"reference","why":"Explains CCS as the industry standard with Tesla as the hold-out.","quote":"The industry _has_ standardized, except for one hold-out (Tesla)","text":"The industry _has_ standardized, except for one hold-out (Tesla): [VIDEO] > CCS is the established industry standard DC fast-charging connector, and every automaker selling cars for the US market is already using it, or, in the case of Nissan, has pledged to use it going forward. Except, oh right, except for... them [Tesla]. The charger situation is quite analogous to Lightning vs. USB-C for smartphones. USB-C is the industry standard, and there's one manufacturer still using a proprietary connector. And in both cases, the hold-out manufacturer provides an adapter cable so you can use the standard connector. (To your point about standardized batteries having approximately 0% chance of happening any time soon, I 100% agree.)"},{"id":31536967,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2022-05","story":"Cost of ultra-rapid EV charging increases by almost 50%","stance":"reference","why":"Covers EV road-tripping and fast charging times.","quote":"The newest cars combined with the best DC fast charge you can buy will fill the car up in about 10-20 minutes.","text":"I have no experience with EVs, but everything I've heard from people who own them is that they slow-charge them at home. You only need to use a rapid charger if you're driving very long distances or all day long. The newest cars combined with the best DC fast charge you can buy will fill the car up in about 10-20 minutes. The next tier down of charger and car battery is more like 30 minutes. This is from this video on the subject of EV roadtripping: [VIDEO]"}]},"xo9cKe_Fch8":{"wy":"Cited as the primary source for the original App Store terms and Apple's stated reason for the 30% cut.","q":{"x":"Job's original announcement [1] suggested that the App Store's 30% was the cost of running the App Store","i":24037656,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":46685509,"author":"amatecha","month":"2026-01","story":"Apple testing new App Store design that blurs the line between ads and results","stance":"reference","why":"Contrast with how simple the App Store was in 2008","quote":"Check out nice and simple it was in 2008","text":"I remember when it was a news headline that Apple showed ads _at all_ in App Store. It's sad that they're straying even further into scummy ways to nickel-and-dime every ounce of profit they can get out of everyone using their products and services. (Check out nice and simple it was in 2008: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":28110341,"author":"Dracophoenix","month":"2021-08","story":"Google “contemplated buying some or all of Epic” to stop “contagion”","stance":"reference","why":"30% cut has existed since 2008 announcement","quote":"it has existed since Jobs first announced the App store in 2008 [1]","text":"No. But I don't take a 30% cut as an indication of cartel-building when it has existed since Jobs first announced the App store in 2008 [1] and was done at a time where the Walmarts and Best Buys of the world charged 70% if not more. Quite recently Apple sought fit to reduce it's revenue share to 15% after the first year on the store store. Google has done the same. I wouldn't call putting more money in the pockets of developers a lack of competition. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24037656,"author":"d4rti","month":"2020-08","story":"Telegram files EU antitrust complaint against Apple’s App Store","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs said 30% was cost of running App Store","quote":"Job's original announcement [1] suggested that the App Store's 30% was the cost of running the App Store","text":"Yup, I'd certainly agree that the App Store has various consumer utilities, and even in centralizing payment it has value as a consumer; I don't really want to give out my credit card to every app and I certainly want a central place to cancel subscriptions with no bullshit. That said, I do think that 30% is excessive. Job's original announcement [1] suggested that the App Store's 30% was the cost of running the App Store; I don't believe that it is really costing that much to run now. Reducing the payment premium and/or allowing customer choice of paying the App Store rate, or the outside the store rate is maybe the least bad option here. It's telling that there has been no substantive movement on margin from Apple or Google here. Steam is also 30% until you reach higher tiers, then it's 25%. Epic is only 12%. I think a lot of the current concerns would be allayed if the cut was only 12%. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20566093,"author":"Brendinooo","month":"2019-07","story":"You’re Paying into a Broken System Whenever You Buy Something on iOS","stance":"reference","why":"Original App Store announcement and the problems it solved","quote":"It's worth going back to the App Store reveal video.","text":"It's worth going back to the App Store reveal video. [VIDEO] 30% might be high, and there's certainly a case to be made for conflict of interest in the Netflix/Spotify arena, but the App Store is solving a lot of problems: it's a single, universal way to get an app out to every iThing, it manages updates, and there's no nickel-and-diming in terms of processing fees or hosting or anything like that. I thought it was in that keynote but maybe not: I remember someone comparing a 30% cut to the costs associated with getting a physical copy onto retail shelf, and if I recall correctly it was in the same universe."}]}});
